sion":1},{"readerLearnsAtEventId":null,"originEventId":null,"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","title":"Corran is Anselm Rook, a Harl factor","description":"","tags":[],"id":"mkdnWfMh9PSUHBKYNn3A2","createdAt":1794680949697,"updatedAt":1794680949697,"version":1}],"knowledgeReveals":[{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"W-ICbtpDrgPP-0dNU7Gfn","characterId":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","note":"","id":"-BeXmU5C0XD0VReeUvbB7","createdAt":1794681009067,"updatedAt":1794681009067,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"JOyyKCayHFckuMK3aUEbw","characterId":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"-fmAdjAv_ynLaj8vGY6OQ","createdAt":1794681000751,"updatedAt":1794681000751,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"JOyyKCayHFckuMK3aUEbw","characterId":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","note":"","id":"4ljDBjJVsS5vXnqcetwz3","createdAt":1794681003241,"updatedAt":1794681003241,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"JOyyKCayHFckuMK3aUEbw","characterId":"Gli2vCm9fdUmDG0ELTIlL","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"8Zs8G2kkhcviFgiQnmGrj","createdAt":1794681001567,"updatedAt":1794681001567,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"JOyyKCayHFckuMK3aUEbw","characterId":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","note":"","id":"A-QGAxQkzmPfhaVStdTU6","createdAt":1794680999084,"updatedAt":1794680999084,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"JOyyKCayHFckuMK3aUEbw","characterId":"QTT6KgihBjZcHYe5qBsBk","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","note":"","id":"Bjm8L3IwXRE3MXJ9lBi66","createdAt":1794681005723,"updatedAt":1794681005723,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"W-ICbtpDrgPP-0dNU7Gfn","characterId":"cY_CqgHaFVrViHC17jmnd","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"N83LGWG9OGrh7mlw1CUJK","createdAt":1794681007450,"updatedAt":1794681007450,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"W-ICbtpDrgPP-0dNU7Gfn","characterId":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","note":"","id":"SyCP5L2zVCRwPrx6Qnx8L","createdAt":1794681008250,"updatedAt":1794681008250,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"JOyyKCayHFckuMK3aUEbw","characterId":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","note":"","id":"Urs8Q2G5T8oNz2U8aX10u","createdAt":1794681004068,"updatedAt":1794681004068,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"JOyyKCayHFckuMK3aUEbw","characterId":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"X8lYl2yAQcr3D8fYXfi8b","createdAt":1794680999918,"updatedAt":1794680999918,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"JOyyKCayHFckuMK3aUEbw","characterId":"cY_CqgHaFVrViHC17jmnd","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"Y5I4MDdMWbJg6xjSuAYq0","createdAt":1794681002418,"updatedAt":1794681002418,"version":1},{"worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","factId":"JOyyKCayHFckuMK3aUEbw","characterId":"EhAf-qbdXAA5t1VnQtFGO","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","note":"","id":"uQJkFYoiQ8j79AG2_0yzn","createdAt":1794681004904,"updatedAt":1794681004904,"version":1}],"characterGoals":[],"sceneTexts":[{"id":"1GIUhdbMsCnZGyhrmSyez","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N5Fb_YPhoAqUWmyzlORZt","text":"Kesh did not come to the hearing. But on the morning after it, when Ilse came into the Exchange at six with her sleeves already pinned, there was a pair of sea boots standing on the tally desk, her father's: the one she had lost on the crane wall, grey with the flats to the ankle, and the one she had left by the door in Coil Lane, which meant that Kesh had been in Coil Lane. Stuffed into the toe of the first was a scrap of paper that said, in a sprawling hand, *his name is Anselm Rook. it was on the letter he paid me off with in the summer. a Harl factor once, till they threw him out. you owe me five crowns.*\n\nSefa came at noon with bread.\n\n\"Your counting face,\" she said, sitting on the step below the desk, which was still not allowed. \"The old one. The good one.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"It is.\" Sefa tore the loaf. \"What are you counting?\"\n\nIlse showed her. It was the cellar book for cellar three, dried out page by page in front of the fire in the tally room, cockled and stained and smelling of the sea. At the ninth, where the stub of the cut page was, she had pasted in a fresh leaf. On it, in her own hand, she had written the entry for the *Constant* as it should have been.\n\nForty-two sacks of Harl white. Received at the north quay. Weighed. Entered.\n\nAnd underneath, smaller, in the margin, where a clerk notes a correction:\n\n*Four sacks struck. See the magistrate's record. — I. M.*\n\n\"There,\" said Ilse. \"Now it adds.\"","wordCount":278,"createdAt":1794683303137,"updatedAt":1794683367557},{"id":"BcJKQR9LCMBfD7mVdkKcX","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","text":"The tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives watched her go and then stared at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap. For a while she only held it. Then, because she was a clerk and it was the one thing she was sure how to do, she took down the marbled book and entered it: the letter, word for word, every stop where the writer had put one, and under it, small and careful, the two keys in their circle. She wrote the time beside it, as near as she could put it. Only when the ink was dry did she let herself think.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with an interest in the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. One of the six had written this, and she did not know which, and she did not need to. She needed one who could not have.\n\nShe made herself go at it the way she would go at a column that would not add. Whoever wrote the letter wanted the vote on the gate held off until the new year. Aud Brennick had been asking for that vote for ten years, loudly, in the council house and on the quays, every spring when the water came a little higher. She had asked Vael to look into the gate this very summer; Hanne had said so. The one councillor in Varrow who had spent ten years trying to bring the vote on could not be the one keeping it off. It was the first thing all week that had added up cleanly, and Ilse was so glad of it that she did not check the sum a second time.\n\nAnd Brennick was the one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\" She did not say Hanne's name. Hanne was old, and three shawls deep, and had trusted her with a drawer that stuck.\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned to the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home fast and did not look back once.","wordCount":1276,"createdAt":1794680440248,"updatedAt":1794685539740},{"id":"D2t68nqlvs3z7qKP4LgFQ","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, just above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth. Each sack was stamped with a mark Ilse could have drawn in her sleep: the Harl mark, the little ship under full sail. White salt. Something in the ship caught at her, the way a wrong figure catches in a column before you have found it, and she bent closer with the lantern, and could not find it, and let it go.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. Four sacks on a man's back at every low water is a living. Forty in a lighter up the old channel at the top of a spring tide, straight in through the gate, is a fortune. That's what the gate's being cleared for.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and said nothing.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"On Monday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, on Monday night, late. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the gate is yours at every spring flood until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1062,"createdAt":1794680434127,"updatedAt":1794697780092},{"id":"MYNkYbxUcbmMM6svs_l7h","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not seem surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me this morning, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. If a magistrate stopped listening after five minutes, then he would have to be given something he could read instead. A clerk's book did not stop being true because nobody was listening to it.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.","wordCount":712,"createdAt":1794680428038,"updatedAt":1794683217179},{"id":"WiCcYeevomSB8HuFyfOxI","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","text":"The wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a north-easter behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the water gate tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the water gate had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer, a course at a time, from the culvert side. Enough for a man with a sack on his back. The rest went this week. Enough for a lighter's load.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing. That isn't Harl salt, Miss Marrow. It's Ost white, from across the water, that no one in Varrow may buy and everyone would, dressed up in a Harl coat. I had the man who brings it in under the great clock a week ago, talking prices, so that I could get a look at his ring.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Nobody's coming,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use. Ten years I asked your guild and my council to open that gate before the sea took the lower town, and ten years you sent it to a committee. So I opened it myself, and found that it paid. The sea's coming in anyway, Merrit. I thought I might as well be rich when it does.\" Brennick came down the last step and took in the vault: the salt, the gates, the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought of the marbled book on the shelf in Coil Lane, the letter in it in her own hand and the keys at the foot of it, which nobody in this cellar knew about. Then she thought about not giving the page. She thought about it hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the water gate. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.","wordCount":1119,"createdAt":1794680446332,"updatedAt":1794685538087},{"id":"ZLpkh295NC6DScW7LhUv0","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken to pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in for hours. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was bitterly cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had. I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea stayed black. The water went on rising under them, patient as a clerk, and the chain below their feet swung slower as the warehouse filled. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell, and not a line of light anywhere. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.","wordCount":534,"createdAt":1794680434064,"updatedAt":1794685534788},{"id":"_GetgEPpOZT3DncM5jwtk","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The note came under the door at Coil Lane a little after eight, folded small, in a hand she knew from years of chits: *Tally room. Nine o'clock. Bring what you took from cellar three. — M. V.*\n\nShe sat with it a long while. Brennick had told her to speak to no one, and the man who had written this was the man the letter said could manage her. But only someone who had opened the cellar book since Tuesday knew there was anything missing from it to bring; and she wanted, more than she had wanted anything all week, to see his face when she told him the letter was already with the council. A clerk checks a figure against the hand that wrote it.\n\nThen she put her shawl on again, and on her way down Weld Street she left two lines for Sefa at Orley's: *Gone to the tally room, to Vael. If I'm not back by ten, tell Captain Orrin.*\n\nThe Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down. \"You sent for me,\" she said, and held out the note.\n\nVael looked at it. Then he put his hand inside his coat and took out its twin, folded the same way. *Tally room. Nine o'clock. — I. M.* It was not her hand. It was not a bad copy of it.\n\n\"Then neither of us did,\" he said. \"Sit down anyway.\"\n\nShe sat.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book towards her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"","wordCount":616,"createdAt":1794680440115,"updatedAt":1794697781036},{"id":"_m3oEuhOM42CcdMptrvYC","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"XGmpbItpsK6pu6YzV87iu","text":"Somebody was hammering on the door.\n\nIlse came up out of sleep the way you come up out of cold water, all at once and gasping. The room was dark. The fire was a red eye in the grate. Tobin's cot across the room was empty, the blanket thrown back.\n\n\"Ilse! Ilse, open the door!\"\n\nSefa. She knew the voice before she knew she was standing, and she had the bar off the door and Sefa was in the room, still in her dye apron, her hair coming down, her blue hands grabbing at Ilse's arms.\n\n\"It's the *Gull*,\" Sefa said. \"She's burning. She's out on the bar and she's burning. I saw it from the roof at Orley's, I was up stirring the vat, I saw the light—\"\n\n\"Tobin's here.\" Ilse turned to the cot as if he would be in it. \"He was here. We ate. He went to bed.\"\n\n\"Then where is he?\"\n\nThey ran. Down the stair, along Coil Lane, down the second stair to the quay, where the fog had thinned to rags and there were people already, lightermen in their shirts, a woman with a baby wrapped in a shawl, two boys from the rope walk. Everyone was looking the same way, out across the black water towards the bar, where a long orange smear of fire lay on the dark like a wound.\n\n\"That's the *Gull*,\" said one of the lightermen. \"That's Marrow's boat.\"\n\n\"Who took her out?\" Ilse's voice came out high and strange. \"Who took her out? She was empty. She was moored right there.\"\n\nThe place where the *Gull* had been moored was empty water. The painter was gone from the ring.\n\n\"Saw her go out on the ebb, an hour back,\" said the lighterman, a heavy man named Coby whom Ilse knew a little. \"Thought it was your brother. Didn't think anything of it. Lot of the lads go out to the bar on the ebb to fish the channel.\"\n\n\"In the fog?\"\n\nCoby shrugged, and did not look at her.\n\nOut on the bar the fire leaned over and went down, slowly, the way a candle goes when it falls into its own wax. Then there was only a red glow on the water, and then nothing at all, and the bell buoy ringing.\n\n\"Get a boat,\" Ilse said. \"Somebody get a boat out. He might be in the water.\"\n\nNobody moved. It was Sefa who said it, gently, her blue hand on Ilse's back.\n\n\"Ilse. It's slack water at the bar. If he's in it, he's—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"—he's swimming. Tobin swims like a seal. You know he does.\"\n\nThen two of the lightermen were unshipping oars. A boat went out, and then another, their lanterns swinging. Ilse stood at the edge of the quay and watched them go into the dark.\n\nShe found that she had the scrap of tally paper in her hand, though she did not remember taking it out. She had slept with it under her pillow.\n\nThe boats were gone an hour. When they came back, the first held three men and a coil of burnt rope. The second held Tobin.\n\nHe was sitting up in the stern, soaked to the skin and black with soot, his left hand wrapped in somebody's shirt. He was alive. Ilse's knees went, and Sefa held her up, and for a moment the whole quay swam.\n\nThen she saw the two men in the boat behind him, in the dark blue coats of the harbour watch, and the way one of them had his hand on Tobin's collar.","wordCount":601,"createdAt":1794680400266,"updatedAt":1794682764046},{"id":"aZ6fxU1Zh6pIgD8_O7wR_","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","text":"She did not find Pell. Pell, the north quay foreman told her, had not come in that morning, nor sent word, and his wife had been round twice asking for him. The foreman was not worried. Pell drank, he said, and when he drank he sometimes slept in the sail lofts.\n\nIlse tried the sail lofts. Pell was not there. She went back to the Exchange and did a day's tally with her copy of the culvert in her sleeve and the page from cellar three against her ribs, and she did not make a single mistake, which she was afterwards rather proud of.\n\nIt was dark when she came out. The streets of the old town were narrow and crooked and the lamps were few, and she had walked them all her life and knew every step, which is how she could tell, by the bottom of Weld Street, that she was being followed.\n\nShe did what her father had taught her. She did not hurry. She did not look round. She turned into the rope walk, a covered alley three hundred yards long where the ropemakers twisted hemp in the daytime, and which at night was empty and dark and smelled of tar. Halfway down there was a place where the roof had fallen in and you could step out of the walk into the yard of a cooper's. She stepped out, and waited behind a stack of staves, and counted.\n\nAt thirty, a figure came past the gap in the roof. It stopped. It looked into the cooper's yard.\n\n\"You're very good,\" it said. \"But you breathe like a bellows.\"\n\nIt was a woman. She was young, younger than Ilse, with cropped black hair and a sailor's coat too big for her, and she stood in the gap with her hands in her pockets as though she had all the time in the world.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"Kesh.\" The woman came into the yard and sat down on an upturned barrel. \"Just Kesh. You're the Marrow girl. Tally clerk. Sister of the man who burned his boat.\"\n\n\"He didn't burn it.\"\n\n\"I know he didn't. I watched who did.\" Kesh smiled. She had a gap between her front teeth. \"That's what I've come to sell you.\"\n\nIlse came out from behind the staves.\n\n\"Sell.\"\n\n\"I'm a woman of business. Everybody in Varrow is. You count salt, I carry things.\" Kesh took a hand out of her pocket, palm up. \"Five crowns.\"\n\n\"I haven't got five crowns.\"\n\n\"Then you can't afford what I know.\" Kesh shrugged and began to stand.\n\n\"Wait.\" Ilse put her hand to her sleeve, where the copy of the culvert was. \"I can't pay you. But I can tell you something you don't know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"There's a culvert,\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange. It runs from the old water gate out to the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Somebody's using it to bring white salt into cellar three that never went through the scales. Three or four sacks a time, since midsummer. And the master of the guild went and looked at the plans of it on Saturday.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for a while. Somewhere out in the old town a dog was barking, and a woman was shouting at the dog, and the dog was not listening.\n\n\"That's worth something,\" Kesh said at last. \"That's worth something to the right people. All right, Marrow. Here's mine for yours. The man who took your brother's boat out was a southerner. Big, soft-spoken, wears a seal ring. I've carried for him, once or twice. He calls himself Corran. That's not his name.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Because I've seen his real name,\" said Kesh. \"On a letter. In a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight.\" She stood and pulled the big coat around her. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. Low water's about four in the morning. If you want to see where your culvert comes out, meet me at the end of the east causeway at two on Wednesday night, and wear boots you don't love.\"\n\n\"Thursday's when the magistrate sits.\"\n\n\"Then you'll want to be back by breakfast,\" said Kesh, and went out through the gap in the roof, and was gone.","wordCount":714,"createdAt":1794680428115,"updatedAt":1794685275165},{"id":"cHGcMWc2UwJ0pHIZ3sV6x","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"KRsrJ5iPhhiIQpc1GaDZW","text":"The captain's leave, as it turned out, could be had for a basket.\n\nSefa brought bread, and a pot of Mistress Orley's fish stew with a cloth tied over it, and a clean shirt of Tobin's that she had taken off the line at Coil Lane, and a little jar of goose grease for his hand. She set them down on the table in front of the sergeant with the cold and smiled at him, and kept smiling, until he got up and unlocked the iron door.\n\nTobin was sitting on the bench in the cell with his back against the wall. His left hand was bound up in grey rag. He looked up when she came in and his whole face changed, and then changed again, carefully, into something else.\n\n\"Sefa.\"\n\n\"You owe me a shilling,\" said Sefa. She sat down on the bench beside him and put the basket on his knees. \"Eat that. Mistress Orley made it and she'll ask.\"\n\nHe ate. He ate like a man who has had nothing but watch-house gruel for three days, which he had, and she watched him and did not say anything until the pot was empty.\n\n\"Where's Ilse?\"\n\n\"Out. She left me a note last night. *Gone to look at something.*\" Sefa took his bad hand and began to unwind the rag. \"She came back at noon looking like something the tide brought in, and went to the watch, and then she went home and put her good shawl on and went up the Mount.\"\n\n\"The Mount? Who does she know on the Mount?\"\n\n\"Nobody. That's what worries me.\" Sefa got the rag off. The burn ran across his palm and up the inside of his fingers, shiny and red. She opened the goose grease. \"Hold still.\"\n\nHe held still. She worked the grease into the burn, gently, and he did not make a sound, though his jaw went tight.\n\n\"I saw her go out,\" Sefa said. \"The *Gull*. I was on the roof at Orley's. Somebody was steering her. I told Ilse and I've told the captain.\"\n\n\"I know. He told me. Sefa. When I got to her, out at the bar. She was already burning. Nobody aboard. But there was a boat pulling away. A gig, four oars, going east, towards the drowned warehouses. I saw it against the fire. And there was a man in the stern of it, and he turned round to look at the *Gull*, and the light was on his face. I told Orrin that, the first night. He wrote it in his book and said there were forty gigs in Varrow and half of them went east after dark.\"\n\n\"Did you know him?\"\n\n\"I didn't think so. Not then.\" Tobin was quiet. \"But I've been sitting here three days with nothing to do but think. And I've seen him since. I've seen him in the Exchange. Talking to Master Vael by the clock, a week back, the day I came in for my pay. A big man, soft-spoken. Southern. With a ring.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" said Sefa.\n\nTobin stared at her. \"How do you know that name?\"\n\n\"Because there's a girl called Kesh who drinks at the Lantern and talks too much when she's had three, and she's been talking about a man called Corran since the summer.\" Sefa wound a clean strip of linen round his hand, firmly. \"Half the rope walk knows that name, Tobin. We just don't know who it belongs to.\"\n\n\"Tell Ilse,\" Tobin said. \"Tell her Vael knows him. Tell her tonight.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Sefa.\" He caught her hand with his good one. \"Tell her not to go up the Mount again.\"","wordCount":615,"createdAt":1794680440191,"updatedAt":1794683237013},{"id":"fLobLxpPqvj8ADlqBY932","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","text":"The bell at the Salt Exchange rang six times before the light came over the flats, and by the sixth stroke Ilse Marrow had her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut and the day's first ledger open on the slope of the tally desk.\n\nShe liked this hour. The great hall was empty except for the porters sweeping out yesterday's grit, and the windows along the east wall were still grey, and the only sound was the brooms and, under them, the long breath of the tide going out across the mud. In an hour the hall would be full of factors shouting prices and lightermen shouting back and the clerks of the six houses arguing over whose scales were honest. For now it was hers.\n\nThe ledger was the lading book for the *Constant*, a coaster out of Harl that had come in on the evening tide with forty-two hundredweight of white salt in sacks. Ilse ran her finger down the column. Forty-two sacks, each a hundredweight, each stamped with the Harl mark. Received at the north quay by the weigher, a man named Pell whose twos were a byword in the tally room. Carried by lighter to the Exchange cellars. Entered.\n\nShe opened the cellar book, where the same salt should have been entered again by the cellarman, and frowned.\n\nForty-six.\n\nShe checked the date, checked the mark, checked that she had not turned two pages at once. Forty-six sacks of Harl white, received into cellar three on the night of the ninth. Four more than had come off the ship.\n\n\"Morning, Miss Marrow.\"\n\nShe did not jump, though it was a near thing. Merrit Vael stood at the end of the desk with his hat in his hand. He was the master of the guild, a tall, soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and ink on his cuffs like any clerk, and he had a way of arriving in a room without having walked into it.\n\n\"Master Vael. You're early.\"\n\n\"So are you. So are you always, I'm told.\" He nodded at the open book. \"The *Constant*?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Anything wanting?\"\n\nShe could have said no. Afterwards she would wonder why she didn't, and the only answer she could find was that she had been a tally clerk for six years and her whole worth in the world was that she said what the numbers said.\n\n\"The cellar book has four more sacks than the lading book. Forty-six against forty-two.\"\n\nVael came around the end of the desk and looked, long enough that she could hear the porters' brooms again.\n\n\"Pell,\" he said at last. \"Pell writes his sixes like his twos. I've told him.\" He tapped the lading entry with one finger. \"That's a forty-six, Miss Marrow. Look at the tail on it.\"\n\nIt was a two. She had seen Pell's twos every working day for six years and this was one of them, and the tail on it was the tail on all his twos, a little hook like a fishing barb.\n\n\"I'll check with the ship's master,\" she said.\n\n\"The *Constant* sailed on the morning ebb.\" Vael closed the ledger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over. \"Write it up as forty-six. I'll have a word with Pell about his hand.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\nHe smiled at her and put his hat on and went out through the side door, and Ilse sat looking at the closed cover for a while. Then she opened it again and copied both entries, the lading and the cellar, onto a scrap of tally paper, and folded the scrap twice, and put it inside her stays where it lay against her ribs all day like a cold coin.\n\nBy seven the hall was full. Factors from the inland houses stood under the great clock with their samples in little linen bags and tasted salt off the backs of their hands. A Harl agent argued with a Varrow weigher about moisture. Somebody's dog got in and was chased out. Ilse entered the *Grace of Tidings* and the *Meg* and a lighter-load of grey salt from the south pans that was mostly sand, and did not think about the *Constant* more than once an hour.\n\nAt noon Sefa came by with bread.\n\nSefa was apprenticed to a dyer in Weld Street and her hands were blue to the wrist and would be, she said, until she died, and probably after. She sat on the step below the tally desk, which was not allowed, and tore the loaf in half.\n\n\"You've got your counting face on,\" she said.\n\n\"I always have my counting face on. I count.\"\n\n\"Your other counting face. The one where something doesn't add.\" Sefa chewed. \"Is it a man?\"\n\n\"It's four sacks of salt.\"\n\n\"Oh,\" said Sefa. \"Well, that's much worse.\"\n\nIlse almost told her. But Master Vael crossed the far end of the hall just then, talking with a woman in a grey council cloak, and he glanced up at the tally desk, and Ilse swallowed the words with the bread.\n\n\"Tobin's in tonight,\" she said instead. \"He's bringing the *Gull* round from the south pans. Come and eat with us.\"\n\n\"Can't. Mistress Orley's got a vat of woad going and she says if it isn't stirred on the hour it'll go green, and if it goes green she'll have my hide for a dye bag.\" Sefa stood and brushed the crumbs off. \"Tell your brother he still owes me a shilling.\"\n\n\"He'll say he paid it.\"\n\n\"He'll be lying,\" said Sefa, and went.","wordCount":933,"createdAt":1794680379810,"updatedAt":1794685529804},{"id":"jwyD5SnrNXKmXN8DtDF0l","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay with Dall, who had crewed her for six years, and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs. Neither of them said anything for a while. Then Dall said that she had been a good old boat, and Tobin said he supposed they would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, who began with Ferris on the south pans and the eleven crowns owing on the *Gull*'s mast and then set them aside himself; and Vael; and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and who told the court, among a great deal else, that he had written both the notes himself, at Councillor Brennick's table, since a cellarman who copies forty hands a week into a cellar book can copy two more; and the sergeant with the cold; and all without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to clear her brother by hanging a councillor.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday afternoon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate studied the page. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Your Tuesday has gone brown at the edges,\" he said at last, \"the way gall ink goes when it has had a week in the air. Your Thursday night is still black. A woman may change her pen when she likes, Miss Marrow. She cannot make her ink older.\"\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the witnesses' bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick after him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":851,"createdAt":1794680446214,"updatedAt":1794697782221},{"id":"kO0-NKcH5sV599zuid8pB","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Kghlu5z8Cey3C2HTmpZvF","text":"The east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had served the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at two in the morning in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, put it at her back, and sighted along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"","wordCount":675,"createdAt":1794680434188,"updatedAt":1794685276176},{"id":"m7H28ggI0fBzuWaYRS2QV","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. In the lamplight he was grey. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange on Thursday night. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the top of Thursday's spring tide, when there's water enough in the old channel.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was what the lightermen still called the old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin knocked the pipe out on the bollard. \"He said forty sacks. A full load, the *Gull* down to her marks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down towards the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.","wordCount":904,"createdAt":1794680379832,"updatedAt":1794685271434},{"id":"oXT1tDIXhX9UPrzerrxQI","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, Mott the cellarman's careful round hand. She went back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nBefore midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. A page in a book could be written over by whoever had the key to the cupboard. A page inside her stays could not. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both there. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.","wordCount":466,"createdAt":1794680400150,"updatedAt":1794685273298},{"id":"on-x2dCaF63RjWvKG9uU2","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","text":"She did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the east side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the lamps under the great clock. In their light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house this afternoon,\" said Orrin, \"with a pot of stew and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. She came back at ten with your note. I know a clerk's hand when I see one, and your brother was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the top step of the cellar stair. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"I burned the letter before you were home,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"","wordCount":519,"createdAt":1794680446275,"updatedAt":1794697800551},{"id":"pO25Y2H1qg5Qs0akkwVuB","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"K3nyf6eQkfA7bwMjLc83B","text":"The harbour watch kept its house at the foot of Tarrant Stair, a squat stone building with barred windows that had been a customs post in the old days and still had the king's arms over the door, half worn away by salt. Inside it smelled of tallow and wet wool. There was a long bench, a table, a fire, and at the back an iron door with a grille.\n\nTobin was behind the iron door.\n\n\"He's not under arrest,\" said Captain Orrin. \"Not yet. He's under my eye. There's a difference, and I'd advise you to be grateful for it.\"\n\nDace Orrin was younger than Ilse had expected for a captain of the watch, perhaps thirty, with a long serious face and a scar through one eyebrow that gave him a look of permanent mild surprise. He had taken off his coat and was writing in a daybook at the table, and he did not stop writing while he talked to her.\n\n\"He was in his bed,\" Ilse said. \"He went to bed at ten. I heard him.\"\n\n\"Did you hear him get up?\"\n\n\"No. I was asleep.\"\n\n\"Then you heard him go to bed and you didn't hear him get up, and an hour after midnight his boat was seen going out on the ebb, and an hour after that it was burning on the bar, and he was in the water beside it.\" Orrin dipped his pen. \"Miss Marrow, I'm not saying he set it. I'm saying I've a burnt lighter across the main channel at the bottom of the tide and every ship due in on the morning flood will have to stand off until we clear it, and the owner of the lighter was the only man found in the water.\"\n\n\"It's his boat. Why would he burn his own boat?\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" said Orrin, without looking up. \"Debt. A woman. A quarrel with his partner. I've seen men burn their boats for all of those, and once for a dog.\" He wrote something. \"What's the matter with his hand?\"\n\n\"I don't know. He was hurt. He was hurt getting her off, or putting the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"He says he woke because her chain stopped knocking on the wall under your window, and he went down to the quay and saw her going out, and took a skiff after her, and when he got to her she was already burning and there was nobody aboard. He says he burnt his hand on the tiller trying to bring her round. He says there was a gig going east. There are forty gigs in Varrow.\" Orrin looked up at last. \"Does that sound like your brother?\"\n\nIt did. It sounded exactly like Tobin.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It sounds like a story to me,\" said Orrin. \"But I've been wrong.\" He closed the daybook. \"Go home, Miss Marrow. He'll be here in the morning. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"That's three days.\"\n\n\"It's two. Today's Tuesday.\" He stood, and she saw that he was tired, and that the hand he put out to take his coat off the chair was not quite steady. \"If there's anything you know about how that boat got out to the bar, now is the time to tell me. Not Thursday. Now.\"\n\nShe thought of the southern man with the ring. She thought of the river door, bricked up, and the four sacks of Harl white, and Master Vael closing the ledger gently, like a book someone had fallen asleep over. She thought of Tobin saying *don't tell anyone*.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"Nothing.\"\n\nOrrin looked at her for a moment longer than was comfortable. Then he nodded, as though she had told him something after all, and opened the street door for her.\n\nOutside, Sefa was waiting on the step, her apron wrapped round her shoulders against the cold. The fog had gone. The sky over the flats was black and pricked with stars, and the tide was coming back in, filling the channels with a sound like a long slow pour of sand.\n\n\"Well?\" said Sefa.\n\n\"Thursday. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Then we've got until Thursday.\" Sefa put her arm through Ilse's and started walking her up Tarrant Stair. \"What do you need?\"\n\nIlse stopped on the stair.\n\n\"Sefa,\" she said. \"What were you doing on the roof at Orley's at one in the morning?\"\n\n\"Stirring the vat. I told you. It goes green.\"\n\n\"The vat's in the yard. Not on the roof.\"\n\nSefa laughed, a short tired laugh with not much in it.\n\n\"There's a man,\" she said. \"He's a ropemaker. We go up on the roof. It's the only place Mistress Orley can't hear the door. Is that what you want to know?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Don't be. He's not much of a man. He talks about hemp.\" Sefa tugged her on up the stair. \"But I saw what I saw, Ilse. I saw the boat go out. There was a light on her when she went out, a little light at the stern, like a lantern. And there was somebody at the tiller. I couldn't see who. But they were steering. Somebody was steering her out to the bar.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"","wordCount":876,"createdAt":1794680400201,"updatedAt":1794685519872},{"id":"tGxlA8R6Z0bSPlAvMEv61","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","text":"The archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river door was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the river was turned, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"The gate's at the cellar end, the slip beside it where the barges came up, and the culvert runs out from behind the gate. When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man could walk it at any low water, if he knew it and didn't mind rats.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman. At the top of a spring tide, with the gate open, you could float a barge up the old channel to it, same as my grandfather did.\"\n\nIlse bent over the drawing. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1055,"createdAt":1794680428178,"updatedAt":1794685274454}],"plotThreads":[],"motifs":[],"proseNotes":[{"id":"-Bk-sDYChzzmgKqfEBS8T","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","quote":"A clerk's book did not stop being true","prefix":"omething he could read instead. ","suffix":" because nobody was listening to","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"There's the plant, and the book's argument in one sentence.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684702000,"updatedAt":1794685570504,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685570504},{"id":"2rzqpGFDetjT0ec5f5SqV","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","quote":"He stood up and stretched","prefix":"in, \"and the say-so to use it.\" ","suffix":". \"Come on. I've got a fish and ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Cut the creak: the bollards are stone (\"The stone was wet through her skirt\"), and stone doesn't creak.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683018672,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683018672},{"id":"2v4mQpxFxvpgPPAZoDUle","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","quote":"Here's the gate, under cellar three.","prefix":" your cellars, one through six. ","suffix":" There was a ramp up from the ri","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Mechanics: from here on I'd like one picture and one set of names. Is the water gate the cellar end of the culvert (gate inside, culvert running out to the warehouses), with the old slip and ramp beside it? You use river door, water gate, river gate, the gate, slip and culvert, and in Ch6 the gates open onto the slip, not the culvert. A sentence of Hanne's here could fix the picture for the rest of the book.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"TE8a1w4GaXTrrgDqx4xi3","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Settled: the water gate is the cellar end of the culvert; the culvert runs out under the old town to the warehouses; the slip is beside the gate, from the old river. \"River door\" is only what the lightermen still call the gate. I'll make the narration say water gate.","createdAt":1794683013272}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683761841,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794683761841},{"id":"3xDX5KWRgK_DnsyGYDiKW","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","quote":"A page in a book could be written over","prefix":"that had been spoiled with ink. ","suffix":" by whoever had the key to the c","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Exactly the clause, and in her terms.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684700000,"updatedAt":1794685569170,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685569170},{"id":"4hStEOolEiHRgDeV_dCts","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","quote":"M. says he can manage her.","prefix":"w year. The clerk is a concern. ","suffix":" If he cannot, I will. Burn this","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Is M. Mott? If so, that's a lovely double, with Kesh's \"any other M. in a town full of them\" playing fair. But it only works if Mott's name is in the reader's head by now, and it isn't (he's first named in Ch6). I've put his name into Ch2 for you.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683766441,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683766441},{"id":"5YDtogBlR1XEL_xhIbJp6","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"The Hearing","prefix":"","suffix":"","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"A note about the file rather than the book: this heading, its title, and the # before Kesh's boots don't show as tracked changes. Chapter Seven appears as if it had always been there, and the # it replaced has gone without a mark. That's no harm to the book, and your cover note told me. But if your software counts changes, it hasn't counted these.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"BOG261HXQfDMqOTmptkUn","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Thank you. Yes: Chapter Seven is new in this draft, split from the old end of Six, and my software does not mark new chapters or scene breaks. I will say so in my notes when it happens.","createdAt":1794685578316}],"outside":"heading","createdAt":1794684710000,"updatedAt":1794685578953,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794685578953},{"id":"5nztp5I_MSRmuMDt_IpYt","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","quote":"drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago","prefix":"sheets of yellowed tissue, were ","suffix":", before the river was turned, w","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"\"Before the flats were drained\": the flats aren't drained, the sea covers them twice a day. I think you mean before the river was turned, as you say everywhere else, so I've made it that.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683020005,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683020005},{"id":"649jxs8dneeAJSAD0EHHV","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","quote":"The Exchange was closed when Ilse got there","prefix":"","suffix":", but the side door was on the l","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Query: why does she go to the Exchange? Brennick has told her to go home and speak to no one, and she \"walked home very fast\". Something has to send her out again: a message (forged?), or her own need to look at the cellar book. As it stands she arrives because the plot needs her there.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683770975,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683770975},{"id":"9ZKnjlYuHkP0xjPW-pF3g","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","quote":"It meant nothing.","prefix":"nd a stick of red sealing wax.\n\n","suffix":" Every house on the Mount bought","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Yes. The reader gets it and Ilse explains it away, in her own voice. This is the \"saw it coming\" you asked for. With the change I've suggested above, this lands as dread rather than exasperation.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683770325,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683770325},{"id":"Bus8G9NLQIeghbg1SipIn","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","quote":"whose sevens looked like ones","prefix":"y the weigher, a man named Pell ","suffix":". Carried by lighter to the Exch","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Query: two pages on Vael's excuse is that Pell writes his sixes like his twos, and from then on the book is about Pell's twos (\"a thousand twos\", \"couldn't write a straight two\"). Are the sevens and ones deliberate (Vael naming the wrong figure, and Ilse ought to catch it), or a leftover? If deliberate, let her notice. If not, make it his twos here.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"ipuG2UdbPbe33bJaqW_bk","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"A leftover. It's his twos from here on; I'll make it so.","createdAt":1794683010702}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683760525,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794683760525},{"id":"Db8lVH8v3-lAGnq8-dYPi","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","quote":"She read it once.","prefix":"nfolded it and read the letter. ","suffix":" Her face did not change at all.","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Ilse read it twice. Brennick reads it once, because she knows what it says. Very good, and it plays fair.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683024742,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683024742},{"id":"FJxSbkxQtheuxai-BHuhi","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"The magistrate sat on the following Tuesday,","prefix":" seemed to be what was wanted.\n\n","suffix":" in the long room over the custo","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Your question 4. The hearing is the payoff of the book's whole idea, that a clerk's honest record is a kind of truth, and it's told in one paragraph of summary. One short dramatised exchange with the magistrate (his question about the ink is already perfect) would carry it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683773562,"version":3,"madeOn":"The magistrate sat on the following Tuesday.","resolvedAt":1794683773562},{"id":"FtX44g2dAE_V6OTeq-Izx","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","quote":"under the great clock.","prefix":"ors. Somebody had lit the lamps ","suffix":" In their light Ilse saw Tobin, ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"\"Gas\": everything else in Varrow is tallow, oil and candle. If this world has gas lighting I'd want to see it earlier; I've made it lamps.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683022705,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683022705},{"id":"HbQ5-3QWJjT02O7Ec-kzH","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","quote":"Hanne grunted.","prefix":"omething to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\n","suffix":" \"The gate's at the cellar end, ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Two small moves in Hanne's speeches, same words in new places. The geography sentence now comes first, so \"But the culvert's still there\" follows \"bricked the gate and filled the slip\" again. And the barge sentence now comes after \"Or a woman\", so her joke lands straight after its setup (\"A small man could walk it\").","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"UqlcUeIUwaAzJEBlHj2O4","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Both taken. The joke landing straight after its setup is much better.","createdAt":1794685564602}],"createdAt":1794684701000,"updatedAt":1794685569839,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794685569839},{"id":"KKb3QGYDjMAFuxy6_iov1","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"Now it adds.","prefix":" — I. M.*\n\n\"There,\" said Ilse. \"","suffix":"\"","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Perfect last line. Don't touch it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683024055,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683024055},{"id":"LAm83jg9yUIjm3RJsjTku","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","quote":"embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors","prefix":"ted on the council's bills, and ","suffix":" who sat for the town.\n\n\"The cle","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"This matters for Ch5: from this moment Ilse knows the letter was written by a councillor. So tomorrow she takes a councillor's letter to a councillor. She can still do it, but she has to think it, and choose Brennick for a reason the reader can see is a bad one.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683767091,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683767091},{"id":"LHcMM_1n-5QOAOubAD-Iw","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","quote":"Bottom of the ebb is two hours before dawn.","prefix":"er. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. ","suffix":" If you want to see where your c","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Tides: if low water is two hours before dawn (say half past five in December), meeting at midnight means four hours on the mud before the bottom of the ebb. But in Ch4 the flood starts while she's reading the letter, an hour or so after they reach the warehouse. Either they meet at three, or low water is about one. See letter: I've worked the week's tides out roughly.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683763808,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683763808},{"id":"LSWYOvEyTG8msiDFrrTQU","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","quote":"The letter's ash,","prefix":"tair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"","suffix":"\" she said. \"And the page is in ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"When did she burn it? Between Ilse leaving and coming down the stair, I assume. Fine, but one word from her (\"I burned it before you were home\") and this is the cue for the notebook, which is why the notebook needs to be in the reader's head by now.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683772908,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683772908},{"id":"M-HWRJjRKW4sxKq9Wx98a","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","quote":"I saw him with it, on the quay, on","prefix":"tied with string. \"He had this. ","suffix":" Monday night, late. He was show","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Continuity: if Pell had this letter last week, it can't say \"The clerk is a concern\", because Ilse only became a concern on Monday when she saw the four sacks. Either Kesh saw him with a different paper, or the letter is newer (Monday night or Tuesday), which also fits \"cleared by the eleventh\".","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683765164,"version":3,"madeOn":"I saw him with it, on the quay, last week.","resolvedAt":1794683765164},{"id":"MCuI0qru6DxFYKc8GxnCs","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"Corran's real name turned out to be Anselm Rook","prefix":"was discharged without a stain. ","suffix":", a factor of the Harl salt hous","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Kesh told Ilse she'd seen his real name on a letter, \"in a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight\". That's a promise to the reader. Either Kesh delivers the name, or cut the promise.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"ObiYuANalNgaVI2XnTCHH","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Delivered, and in the boot, which is the right place for it.","createdAt":1794684723000,"broughtIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794685203691,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794683774177},{"id":"MEO8Jvul3NCE6jBPMAIRC","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"KRsrJ5iPhhiIQpc1GaDZW","quote":"Talking to Master Vael by the clock, a week back","prefix":" I've seen him in the Exchange. ","suffix":", the day I came in for my pay. ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"This is the one thing pointing at Vael that the ending never explains. Why was Vael talking to Corran? If it's part of his slow case, have him say so in Ch6; otherwise the red herring is a cheat.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"t0elm6Gm9FrLPXYhqwuaN","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Part of Vael's slow case: he had Corran in by the clock, talking prices, to get a look at the ring. Vael says so in the cellar now.","createdAt":1794683015839},{"id":"i2v0JCNp2broVKkMN2BcC","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Works, and it's very Vael: a look at the ring, done civilly. I've moved the sentence up beside the forged mark, where the ring belongs. Settled.","createdAt":1794684719000,"broughtIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794685203683,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794685203683,"resolvedIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"},{"id":"MUkrma2zu5BtvuNAPerTQ","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","quote":"Then she went home and washed","prefix":"and wrote it down very slowly.\n\n","suffix":" and put on her other dress, the","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"The sentence I've added at the end of this paragraph is a placeholder in my words, not a suggestion of wording. The hearing needs Ilse to have copied the letter into the marbled book, and on the page she never does. This is the moment: she has it in her lap and nothing else to do.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683768390,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683768390},{"id":"OPhgE_lF6n_FuYxRiPQPM","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","quote":"There was blood in his beard.","prefix":"e top step of the cellar stair. ","suffix":" \"And Corran, whatever his name ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"They've come up into the great hall, so he'd be on the top step; and he was \"holding his head\" a page ago.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683023387,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683023387},{"id":"SVKGVR087KypMP_DXi1d9","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","quote":"\"He came to Hanne,\"","prefix":"s hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n","suffix":" said Ilse. \"On Monday. He asked","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"It's Wednesday night now, so Hanne's \"yesterday\" was Monday.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683020672,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683020672},{"id":"UGSPd_aXZA8gvxQQI7Eib","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","quote":"The gate will be cleared by the eleventh.","prefix":"water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— ","suffix":" After that the culvert is yours","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Query: the eleventh is Tuesday, the morning after the fire. But Tobin's man wants the river door used on Monday night, and Vael says in Ch6 the bricks came out \"in the summer\". And if the gate is clear, what is the culvert still for? I think I can see what you intend, but the reader needs it said once, plainly.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683765791,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683765791},{"id":"WyHsQo5VkPvIV3lJgxoI7","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","quote":"She cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book","prefix":"ecause a clerk had made it so.\n\n","suffix":", close to the binding, the way ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Query: why does she cut it? I think: so it can't be rewritten before anyone else sees it. But she's also taking the only evidence out of the book, and Vael uses exactly that against her in Ch5. One clause of her reasoning here would make it a choice rather than an impulse, and it's a big step for someone who has never blotted a ledger.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"dBDHCl3L6bSAxIR_WiZIk","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"So that it can't be rewritten before anyone else sees it. I'll give her the clause.","createdAt":1794683012421},{"id":"oAktaWXxPWbLjXxJUc2L8","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Good: two sentences, and her reason is a clerk's.","createdAt":1794684722000,"broughtIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794685203662,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794683761192},{"id":"XKmhUiH7IED7bP1hclUbN","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"with a pen going blunt in it","prefix":"t's a clerk's hand over a week, ","suffix":".\"\n\nThe magistrate studied the p","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"This is the scene, and it works. One small doubt a sharp reader will have: a forger could change pens on purpose. Aske's glass suggests he's looking for something harder to fake. One line on what he sees (ink that has dried and darkened over days rather than an afternoon?) would close it.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"f07gMsQC0uWZTth-Hgbmz","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Aske now says what the glass shows: Tuesday's ink gone brown at the edges, Thursday night's still black. \"She cannot make her ink older.\"","createdAt":1794685568518},{"id":"RVGmEk4D8_6pi-y4RD1ky","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"\"She cannot make her ink older.\" Perfect. Settled.","createdAt":1794697478000,"broughtIn":"14pTzKwD20UenNOQXoOlp"}],"createdAt":1794684713000,"updatedAt":1794697751088,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794697751088,"resolvedIn":"14pTzKwD20UenNOQXoOlp"},{"id":"Y4CqTwvjLqD39ugbG_WFq","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","quote":"began to write down everything that had happened","prefix":"ed cover — and cut her pen, and ","suffix":" since six o'clock on Monday mor","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"The notebook. You asked if it's planted firmly enough to carry the hearing: not yet. It's here once, and the next time we hear of it is the magistrate's bench. I've cut \"She did not know why\", which tells the reader not to notice it. Give her a reason (Orrin has just told her the magistrate stops listening after five minutes; a clerk answers that with a record) and let us see her write in it once more. I've drafted a sentence in Ch5.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683764458,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683764458},{"id":"YzeaJiQ9ndgC_eJv_dSp_","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"K3nyf6eQkfA7bwMjLc83B","quote":"He says he woke and the boat was gone","prefix":" the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"","suffix":" and he went down to the quay an","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Query: how does he know the boat's gone from his bed in Coil Lane? And what wakes him? Something should (a sound off the water, the man's threat on his mind). Also: in Ch5 he tells Sefa about a four-oared gig pulling away east with a man's face lit by the fire. Did he tell Orrin that tonight? If not, why not? He has every reason to.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"0YDNUQsjrt_zk2aa9k58u","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"He hears her go: the Gull's mooring chain knocks on the wall under their window every night, and he wakes when it stops. And yes, he told Orrin about the gig — Orrin wrote it down and didn't believe him. I'll put a line of that in his cell scene.","createdAt":1794683011571},{"id":"qr4o-r-sLvNufS8DR3_RK","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"The chain is right: it's the boat's own sound, and it's been under their window all along. From his bed he can only know the chain stopped, so I've cut \"and the boat was gone\" from Orrin's account. The gig line in the cell scene does the rest. Settled.","createdAt":1794684717000,"broughtIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794685203659,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794685203659,"resolvedIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"},{"id":"ZeJgEf35sh8rIGsNKqToI","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"with Dall, who had crewed her for six years","prefix":". Tobin stood on the north quay ","suffix":", and watched the salvage men br","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Dall gets her epitaph, and Tobin gets \"they\". Good.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794697476000,"updatedAt":1794697810448,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794697810448},{"id":"Zty5g3toh-xXgZeCM-QJ5","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","quote":"with your note, and a pot of stew,","prefix":" the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"","suffix":" and a story about Master Vael a","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Small query: the stew went to the watch house in the afternoon, and Ilse's note can't have reached Sefa till after nine. As Orrin tells it, it sounds like one visit. If it's two, \"with your note, on top of the stew\" or similar would square it. If you like it as Orrin's shorthand, leave it.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"tAKH0mNKQPzVEIwxDEOyJ","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Two visits: the stew in the afternoon, the note at ten. Orrin now says so: \"Sefa came to the watch house this afternoon ... She came back at ten with your note.\"","createdAt":1794697809159}],"createdAt":1794697475000,"updatedAt":1794697809797,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794697809797},{"id":"_W3lyOm1k95paqjFUJWzl","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","quote":"It was not her hand. It was not a bad copy of it.","prefix":"ly room. Nine o'clock. — I. M.* ","suffix":"\n\n\"Then neither of us did,\" he s","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Good. But who wrote the two notes? Someone who can copy both their hands. Mott, who copies the cellar entries? Brennick? The reader will want to know, and one line in Ch7 would do it (Mott, \"still talking\", could say it).","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"997pwW7QGAjkOr7j-RCaQ","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Mott, at Brennick's table: he says so at the hearing, among a great deal else.","createdAt":1794685566985}],"createdAt":1794684708000,"updatedAt":1794685574403,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794685574403},{"id":"_nSwqP9FAEm6qeMFLSGlB","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","quote":"the way a wrong figure catches in a column","prefix":"hing in the ship caught at her, ","suffix":" before you have found it, and s","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Exactly right, and in her idiom. Now the extra mast in Ch6 is a clue.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684703000,"updatedAt":1794685571153,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685571153},{"id":"_oBO0mX41D1AqXr5mv2S8","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","quote":"had trusted her with a drawer that stuck","prefix":"old, and three shawls deep, and ","suffix":".\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"This answers my query without a word of explanation.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684706000,"updatedAt":1794685573102,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685573102},{"id":"aOcbeD7fRuyyKcGJi0kGh","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","quote":"She sat with it a long while.","prefix":"e o'clock. Come alone. — M. V.* ","suffix":" Then she put her shawl on again","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Query: as of this afternoon she thinks Vael is M., the man who \"can manage her\". So why does she go to meet him, alone, at night, when Brennick has just told her to speak to no one? She can go, but she needs a reason the reader accepts: to see his face when she tells him the letter is with the council, say, or because the note says something only Vael would know. Or she tells Sefa where she's going. As it stands it reads as the plot sending her.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"DwwojjdAOVVHNtI0wYGn0","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"The note now says \"Bring what you took from cellar three\" — only someone who'd opened the cellar book would know to ask — and she goes because she wants to watch his face when she tells him the letter's with the council. She leaves two lines for Sefa at Orley's, which is what brings Sefa and Orrin. Does it hold?","createdAt":1794685566203},{"id":"VKAvSPQCSvlXCX0SxDpF_","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"This works. The note is the reason, and \"A clerk checks a figure against the hand that wrote it\" is her all over. It also points quietly at Brennick, who watched her hand go to the page, for a reader who's paying attention. Settled.","createdAt":1794697477000,"broughtIn":"14pTzKwD20UenNOQXoOlp"}],"createdAt":1794684707000,"updatedAt":1794697751075,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794697751075,"resolvedIn":"14pTzKwD20UenNOQXoOlp"},{"id":"bKxXF4cry3Pej5EwQ0dvT","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N5Fb_YPhoAqUWmyzlORZt","quote":"grey with the flats to the ankle","prefix":"she had lost on the crane wall, ","suffix":", and the one she had left by th","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Fixed, and better than fixed.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684716000,"updatedAt":1794685577603,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685577603},{"id":"bMaLOBvcf3tC8TIdvTgHN","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","quote":"They sat on the crane beam","prefix":"re Pell was, and still coming.\n\n","suffix":" with their backs to the wall, s","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"With the flood starting after four now, it can't come in \"all night\"; I've made it \"for hours\".","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684704000,"updatedAt":1794685571802,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685571802},{"id":"bmMdbItH2MsENkRy_9X0X","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"KRsrJ5iPhhiIQpc1GaDZW","quote":"there were forty gigs in Varrow","prefix":"e wrote it in his book and said ","suffix":" and half of them went east afte","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Good. You might plant it in Ch2 as well: in Orrin's account of Tobin's story, a dismissive half-line (\"He says there was a gig going east. There are forty gigs in Varrow.\") would make this a callback rather than news.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"RJu6WMwTq35kepLtDJxu-","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Planted: Orrin's account in Ch2 now ends \"He says there was a gig going east. There are forty gigs in Varrow.\"","createdAt":1794685565402}],"createdAt":1794684715000,"updatedAt":1794685573753,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794685573753},{"id":"cAzrNlaDWtJznfjOJlfXd","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","quote":"I thought I might as well be rich when it does.","prefix":"sea's coming in anyway, Merrit. ","suffix":"\" Brennick came down the last st","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"This makes Ilse's sum in Ch5 wrong for exactly the right reason: Brennick did want the gate open; she wanted it for herself first. Very good.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684709000,"updatedAt":1794685575070,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685575070},{"id":"cBoCWmxQO72ZP9pWOYAWA","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","quote":"the Harl ship on it had one mast too many","prefix":"ood stamp, but not quite right: ","suffix":".\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she s","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"A great clue that arrives too late to be one. Plant it in Ch4: she's seen the Harl mark more times than anyone in Varrow, so let her see something not quite right on the warehouse sacks and fail to place it. Also: if the mark is forged, is the salt Harl white at all? It changes what Kesh says in Ch4 and what a \"factor of the Harl salt houses\" is doing.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683772259,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683772259},{"id":"csiYlsb6O80O5oA9z_76h","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,","prefix":" had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"","suffix":"\" he said, \"that the original of","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"\"To hang the woman who accused her brother\" stopped me. Brennick never accused Tobin in public, and \"her\" could be Ilse or Brennick. I've read counsel's charge as \"you made this up to clear your brother by blaming a councillor\" and changed it to say that. If you meant something else, put it back and make it clearer.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"0dhosbAhb7lLnfolecVbT","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Yes, that is what I meant. Taken.","createdAt":1794685567750}],"createdAt":1794684711000,"updatedAt":1794685575702,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794685575702},{"id":"czBS_lu8NxUISB7rgmI1A","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","quote":"At the bottom of a spring tide.","prefix":" a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man. ","suffix":" If he didn't mind rats.\" Hanne ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Query, and the biggest one in the book: if the culvert can only be walked at the bottom of a spring tide, that's twice a month. But the cellar book shows extra sacks \"every week, sometimes twice in a week\" since midsummer (Ch2), and the letter says \"every spring ebb\" (Ch4). They can't all be true. See letter.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"yTh2V_YtN57nNJANDcFP3","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Settled: the culvert can be walked at any low water by someone who knows it — that's the three or four sacks a week, carried by hand since midsummer. The spring tide is for the big run: with the gate cleared, a lighter can come up the old channel at the top of a spring flood and unload straight through it. That is what they wanted Tobin for.","createdAt":1794683014138},{"id":"Qt-qL7_8M-lVo6q2GFNGO","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Yes, this works, and it now reads the same in Tobin, Hanne, Kesh, the letter and Vael. Two small leftovers, both fixed in the text: the man on the south pans still wanted the load \"tonight instead of in the morning\" (now \"on Thursday night\"), and the letter said \"the gate\" twice. Settled.","createdAt":1794684718000,"broughtIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794685203667,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794685203667,"resolvedIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"},{"id":"f98jJ99HeP2f0-scOSUQA","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","quote":"sixty feet above the flats","prefix":"m with their backs to the wall, ","suffix":", and watched the sea come in. I","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Tides again: the flood starting an hour or so after midnight puts high water around seven, and \"the tide turned\" at the six o'clock bell. But Ch1 has the six o'clock bell ring \"before the light came over the flats\", and here the sky has gone grey and white before six. In December, at six, it's dark.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683767742,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683767742},{"id":"irmRbbtRTH4-hHkLSV4VB","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","quote":"No one.","prefix":"es anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"","suffix":" My brother knows about the man ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"And Hanne, who knows about the plans, the culvert and Vael's visit. Is Ilse protecting her? I'd like to think so; a half-line would tell us. But Hanne then disappears from the book. If Brennick is tidying up, Hanne is the next loose end. Even a line in Ch6 would do.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683769676,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683769676},{"id":"jF31WkoPTrdttwYE38HHK","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","quote":"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?","prefix":"arrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"","suffix":"\"","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Terrific chapter ending.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683022038,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683022038},{"id":"liOpgsuHjYCV6G14BBGyC","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","quote":"said a voice behind them","prefix":"h this—\"\n\n\"Orrin isn't coming,\" ","suffix":", and the lamp on the pallet swu","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"As written (\"Orrin is here\") this reads as Orrin arriving, and then it's Corran. I've guessed you meant Corran taunting her. If it's meant as a trick in the dark, it needs another beat.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"2TFGQ9mVJhhA9HacwlYyY","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"You guessed right — Corran taunting her. I'd already changed it to \"Nobody's coming\" before your file arrived, so I've kept mine and rejected yours.","createdAt":1794683015004},{"id":"Zp-Sw7ppXRNJNNNhZe5uk","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"\"Nobody's coming\" is better than mine. Settled.","createdAt":1794684720000,"broughtIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794685203688,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794685203688,"resolvedIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"},{"id":"ndcCoG2Dynb_jd9SAy0Be","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"the one she had lost on the crane wall and the one she had not","prefix":"n the tally desk, her father's, ","suffix":", and stuffed into the toe of th","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Continuity: she kept one boot and ran home in it. So how does Kesh have both? If Kesh fished the lost one off the flats and lifted the other from Coil Lane, that's a very Kesh thing to do, but say so (or let Ilse notice the one from under her bed is gone).","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683774807,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683774807},{"id":"noZY4uYD1OG9yD19POsSB","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","quote":"There's a culvert,","prefix":"t know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"","suffix":"\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"This is the first of four full explanations of the scheme (here; the warehouse in Ch4; Brennick's sitting room in summary; Vael in the cellar in Ch6). This one is earned, because she's trading it for something. Keep it, and trim the others (I've started on Ch4).","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"D54wNDdIM9W50cUAGWs_e","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Kept as you suggest — she's trading it, so it stays. The warehouse version is shorter now, and Vael only adds what's new.","createdAt":1794683759874}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683763159,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794683763159},{"id":"nqKzdDrchNiZ7XFqBQjtb","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"He was a thin, dry man named Aske","prefix":" one over the watch-house door. ","suffix":" who had been a shipowner's cler","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Three small changes in this sentence. \"To the magistrate's visible surprise\" sat against \"without once changing his face\" at the other end of it, so I've kept the face. And the list's items now carry commas of their own, so I've put semicolons between them. If you'd rather, break it into three sentences: Orrin and Ferris; Mott and the notes; the sergeant and the face.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"gxtgUw3mpVoMJL5b6F7mU","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"All three taken. Thank you for everything, Maren.","createdAt":1794697811160}],"createdAt":1794697474000,"updatedAt":1794697811798,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794697811798},{"id":"nuZAeB0Lw8TTk1e64w8mz","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","quote":"patient as a clerk","prefix":"ater went on rising under them, ","suffix":", and the chain below their feet","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Lovely. And the bell ringing six in the dark is more frightening than the old dawn.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684705000,"updatedAt":1794685572455,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685572455},{"id":"oj_Neb7yp4DS0DOpxQ5F5","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","quote":"Tobin was two years younger than she was","prefix":". In the lamplight he was grey. ","suffix":", and broader, and he had their ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"I've moved \"In the lamplight his face was grey\" up two sentences (Word shows it as a cut and an insertion), so the paragraph ends on what his face means rather than on its colour.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683017989,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683017989},{"id":"pVHDuzdf4qW6bpBy-0fRF","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"Some of it, sir.","prefix":" it written after the event?\"\n\n\"","suffix":" Monday is written on Tuesday ni","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Good: this answers the \"every night\" problem honestly, and in her voice. I've made noon \"afternoon\", since at noon she was at the watch house in one boot.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684712000,"updatedAt":1794685576335,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685576335},{"id":"qKBHeyhBZJk1I6gFnSPKd","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","quote":"Then she saw the hand.","prefix":"thought it was a sack at first. ","suffix":"\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying fac","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"You asked me to cut \"hand\" freely. I've cut the stage-business ones. But look how many are about handwriting and where a hand goes: Pell's hand, the cellarman's hand, her hand going to the page and Brennick watching it go. That's a motif. Keep these.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683025404,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683025404},{"id":"qsCy9sizT2VXor4OJevAD","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","quote":"Aske closed the marbled book with one finger","prefix":"eant with the cold sneezed, and ","suffix":", gently, the way you'd close a ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Vael's gesture from page three, and here it means the opposite. Lovely.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794684714000,"updatedAt":1794685576970,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794685576970},{"id":"rYBhdhrmeIN3NOaaH0X_1","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","quote":"He drank and he couldn't write a straight two.","prefix":"r face. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. ","suffix":" But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Perfect epitaph.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683021372,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683021372},{"id":"rc2kGkpbrYvo5G2pbd0Ih","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","quote":"the way other people believed in the gods of the tide","prefix":" the one thing she had believed ","suffix":", was that a ledger was a promis","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"This is the heart of her, and of the book. When the ending turns on her record being believed, this is why.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683019342,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683019342},{"id":"se3FyZT4PKDctJmZF1U3E","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","quote":"And a westerly behind it.","prefix":"ng.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"","suffix":" It'll come over the seawall ton","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Geography: the sea is east (the light comes up over the flats; the drowned warehouses are on the east flats; the Exchange's east windows are grey at dawn). A westerly blows offshore and would hold the tide down. For a surge over the seawall you want an easterly or north-easterly, and then it's the east windows that go in the hall, not the west.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683771608,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683771608},{"id":"uV2xLSQfLw0TbC_sRwDOz","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","quote":"a woman in a grey council cloak","prefix":"he hall just then, talking with ","suffix":", and he glanced up at the tally","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Good: Brennick is in the room on page four, and the reader will have forgotten her by Chapter Five. Keep this exactly as quiet as it is.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683017305,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683017305},{"id":"wYOmhTxjA1DVpywKV1zNO","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","quote":"He said she'd asked him to look into it.","prefix":". Councillor Brennick's notion. ","suffix":"\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Note for Ch5: what Hanne says is that Vael said Brennick asked. In Ch5 Ilse remembers it as \"Hanne had said so\". That slip is exactly the mistake you want the reader to see and Ilse not to, and it's already here. See my comment there.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794683762508,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794683762508},{"id":"yDWBcf2h6buDODJYnczsb","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","quote":"Not the council. One councillor.","prefix":" could take it to the council.\n\n","suffix":" The one who had asked Vael to l","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"Your question 3. This is the paragraph that decides whether she's mistaken or foolish, and at the moment it leans foolish, for one reason: she never thinks about the crossed keys. She learned last night that the letter came from one of six councillors, and here she picks a councillor without a flicker. Let her face it, and reason her way to the one who can't be it: Brennick has argued in public, for years, to open the gate; why would the person using it in secret want a vote on it at all? That's a mistake a clever person makes. Then \"Hanne had said so\" (when Hanne said Vael said so) is the crack the reader sees and she doesn't.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"lh6lNjxngfTuFqvpcf8II","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Rewritten. She thinks it through now, and gets it cleverly wrong.","createdAt":1794683016657},{"id":"g1WRK6FdsJFj9Q-M8fhTt","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Maren Hollis"},"body":"It works. \"She did not check the sum a second time\" is exactly the mistake a clever person makes, and it tells the reader to check the sum for her.","createdAt":1794684721000,"broughtIn":"z3GPOUrnpgjfUFuy9BNXb"}],"createdAt":1794682255000,"updatedAt":1794685203676,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794683769025}],"revisionTasks":[{"id":"0ecbO_KGX7Zb8CtBKS9PI","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Is it Harl salt? Kesh calls it \"duty-free Harl white\". In Ch6 the four sacks carry a forged Harl mark, one mast too many. If the mark is forged, the salt is something else dressed as Harl, which is a different and rather better crime. And then what is Anselm Rook, \"a factor of the Harl salt houses\", doing forging his own houses' mark?","group":"The scheme: told too often, and never quite pinned down (your question 1)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":4,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683823221,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683823221},{"id":"3oHPBgiv3WTryNssvWXh6","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"The simplest fix that keeps your noon return: Kesh says low water's about four, they meet at two, and the flood comes in around half past four. They're on the crane through the dark, with the six o'clock bell still in the dark, which is more frightening anyway. High water mid-morning, and the water below the weed line by noon. Thursday night's high water then falls at about half past ten, which suits Vael's \"top of the flood tonight\".","group":"Days, tides and light (your question 2)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":9,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683825389,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683825389},{"id":"4oAIlGAYB6DxlTmr5obws","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Ilse goes to the Exchange (Ch5, scene 3). The forged notes are a good device, and the twin note in Vael's coat is a lovely beat. But as of that afternoon Ilse believes Vael is M., the man who \"can manage her\", and Brennick has just told her to speak to no one. So why does she go, alone, at night? She can go, but give her a reason: to watch his face when she tells him the letter is with the council, or a detail in the note only Vael would know. Or let her tell Sefa where she's going, which would also give Sefa a reason to be at the watch house with her story. And who wrote the notes? Someone who can copy both their hands. One line in Chapter Seven would settle it, and Mott, \"still talking\", is the obvious mouth for it.","group":"WHAT'S LEFT","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"BX4c5IPhQy0fLBOo3Mvam"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"},{"kind":"character","id":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8"},{"kind":"character","id":"86XxjRM6tp1_kkH4xzJcc"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":32,"createdAt":1794685440771,"updatedAt":1794685594102,"version":2,"doneAt":1794685594102},{"id":"5RHy-djnwORpBy2PYBisW","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"So let her think it. One of six councillors wrote this. Which one couldn't have? And let her reason her way to Brennick for a reason that's clever and wrong. For instance: Brennick has argued in public for years to open the gate, so why would the person using it in secret want a vote on it at all? (That's a nice irony, because the letter says the vote is being held off.) Then \"Hanne had said so\", which is not what Hanne said (Hanne said that Vael said so), becomes the crack the reader sees and Ilse doesn't. The paper and the wax on the writing table will then land as dread rather than exasperation, which is what you want.","group":"Who knows what: Brennick (your question 3)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8"},{"kind":"character","id":"EhAf-qbdXAA5t1VnQtFGO"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":12,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683826688,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683826688},{"id":"6WhWki7QCFtY8eLGvzM6G","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Ch5: let us see her copy the letter into it. At the moment she never does, and the hearing depends on it. I've put a placeholder sentence in the file at the moment she's sitting on Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap. It's in my words; please rewrite it in yours.","group":"The ending (your question 4)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":24,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683831888,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683831888},{"id":"6ft3FSjk_KJS16NGP8sUu","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Two more. Light: Ch1 has the six o'clock bell ring \"before the light came over the flats\", which in December is right, but on the crane the sky has gone grey and then white before six. Wind: the sea is to the east of Varrow (the light comes up over the flats, and the drowned warehouses are on the east flats). A westerly blows off the land and would hold the tide down. For a surge over the seawall you want an easterly or a north-easterly, and then it's the east windows of the hall that go, not the west.","group":"Days, tides and light (your question 2)","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":10,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683825821,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683825821},{"id":"6oS4h51Rkx27Ao9T42nBo","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Brennick asks who else knows, and Ilse says \"No one\" apart from Tobin. Hanne knows nearly everything. Is Ilse protecting her? I'd like her to be. And Hanne then disappears from the book; if Brennick is tidying up, Hanne is the next loose end.","group":"Also worth looking at, under the same heading","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"},{"kind":"character","id":"EhAf-qbdXAA5t1VnQtFGO"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":13,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683827121,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683827121},{"id":"6vuePpvHy-VBAICnhfJmE","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Right now it reads closer to stupidity than to a reasonable mistake, and the reason is one paragraph. In Ch4 Ilse sees the crossed keys at the foot of the letter and the book tells us every child in Varrow knows them: the writer sits on the council. Next day, in Ch5, she takes it to a councillor without once thinking about that. It's the not-thinking that makes her look foolish, not the choice.","group":"Who knows what: Brennick (your question 3)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":11,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683826255,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683826255},{"id":"CbgXGM4d4TA8S52VO9GkU","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"What is the gate, and when was it opened? The letter says \"the gate will be cleared by the eleventh\", Tuesday. Vael says in Ch6 \"the bricks came out in the summer, a course at a time\". Tobin's man wants four sacks brought to the river door on the Monday night, and says it isn't closed. If the gate was open from the summer, why carry salt through a culvert at all? If it's only cleared on the eleventh, how have three sacks a week been coming in since July?","group":"The scheme: told too often, and never quite pinned down (your question 1)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"},{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":2,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683822354,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683822354},{"id":"D9lF6G9wF9k2YhbHPKbzr","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Pell's letter: Kesh saw him with it \"last week\", but it calls the clerk \"a concern\", and Ilse only became a concern on Monday. Either it's a newer letter or Kesh saw a different paper.","group":"Also worth looking at, under the same heading","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"Gli2vCm9fdUmDG0ELTIlL"},{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":18,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683829288,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683829288},{"id":"FnqfClTdwTcR4qX8qYHSn","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Ch6: when Brennick says \"The letter's ash\", the reader should already be thinking of the book before Ilse says it.","group":"The ending (your question 4)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":25,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683832321,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683832321},{"id":"IXUs6cEn708_TzlkgBmfF","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Ferris and Dall. I'm happy with your reasoning on Ferris. But a red herring needs to be dragged across the path at least once more, or it's just a loose thread. One mention in Orrin's mouth, at the watch house or the hearing, and he's doing his job. Dall I'd cut, or give him a single line on the quay in Ch7.","group":"WHAT'S LEFT","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"QTT6KgihBjZcHYe5qBsBk"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":34,"createdAt":1794685440771,"updatedAt":1794685595167,"version":2,"doneAt":1794685595167},{"id":"KiD6CJNkKMs6K71Uj_moj","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"You're right on both counts. Chapter Six is the same length as the others (just under 2,000 words) but carries the cellar, the flood, the rescue, three arrests, the hearing and the epilogue. The cellar and the flood are well paced; it's after the flood that the book starts summarising.","group":"The ending (your question 4)","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"FQtZAtEyI13cdoVe1DMNS"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":20,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683830155,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683830155},{"id":"LZNuXrHH-66n_f9ZcjvfJ","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"My suggestion: decide the whole thing on one sheet of paper, with dates. Then let Hanne fix the geography in Ch3 (one clear sentence about where the gate, the slip and the culvert are), let Ilse put the scheme together in the warehouse in Ch4 (I've trimmed that speech, and cut Kesh's \"laundered\", which is a modern word and not needed), and let Vael in Ch6 add only what's new: the gate, the greased hinges, the forged mark. Ilse's speech to Kesh in the rope walk can stay as it is. She's trading it, so it earns its place.","group":"The scheme: told too often, and never quite pinned down (your question 1)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"EhAf-qbdXAA5t1VnQtFGO"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":6,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683824088,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683824088},{"id":"M9QwEFEV7i4H7cc05x8VQ","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"The tides are where it slips. Tides come about fifty minutes later each day. If it's slack low water at the bar around two on Tuesday morning (which fits everything in Ch2), then the low before dawn on Thursday is somewhere around four, and high water mid-morning. Kesh's \"bottom of the ebb is two hours before dawn\" is roughly right. But in Ch4 they meet at midnight, walk an hour, and the flood starts while Ilse is reading the letter, so low water is happening at about half past one. And on the crane \"the tide turned\" at the six o'clock bell.","group":"Days, tides and light (your question 2)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":8,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683824956,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683824956},{"id":"Ngf0AcfXMfk9627LU8VN6","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Everything is close on Ilse except Ch5, scene 2, which is Sefa's. I think it earns its place. \"His whole face changed, and then changed again, carefully, into something else\" tells us more about Sefa and Tobin than anything else in the book. But at the moment its main job is delivering two pieces of information (Vael and Corran by the clock, and Corran's name). Either give it one thing that only Sefa could see, or give Sefa one more short scene earlier (the roof on the night of the fire would be a gift), so the book has two viewpoints rather than one and an exception.","group":"Point of view","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"86XxjRM6tp1_kkH4xzJcc"},{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"},{"kind":"character","id":"cY_CqgHaFVrViHC17jmnd"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":27,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794685592455,"version":2,"doneAt":1794685592455},{"id":"RiRPH0hEvwEP8sThyTO4w","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Brennick's motive. We learn what she did and never why: money, the vote, the gate itself, some old grudge against the guild. One line in the cellar would do it, and she's the kind of character who'd say it with contempt.","group":"Also worth looking at, under the same heading","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":19,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683829721,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683829721},{"id":"VIMjlGl1x2LIXgqpTiGxJ","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Other habits to watch: \"for a long time\" (four), \"a thousand\" (four, and Pell's \"thousand twos\" is the one to keep), and \"very\". There are a few idioms from the wrong century: \"the most Tobin thing she had ever heard\", \"laundered\", \"duty-free\". And one spelling point: \"toward\" and \"afterward\" in an otherwise British book. I've made them \"towards\" and \"afterwards\".","group":"Prose","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"Gli2vCm9fdUmDG0ELTIlL"},{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":30,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794685593535,"version":2,"doneAt":1794685593535},{"id":"VZ4l8ZaUcG_A48ph5PTf1","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"The hearing. Counsel's charge, \"to hang the woman who accused her brother\", stopped me. I've changed it to what I think you mean; check it. And one doubt a sharp reader will have: a forger could change pens on purpose. Aske takes out a glass, so give him one line on what he sees that's harder to fake. Ink that has dried and darkened over days, not an afternoon, would do.","group":"WHAT'S LEFT","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":33,"createdAt":1794685440771,"updatedAt":1794685594635,"version":2,"doneAt":1794685594635},{"id":"Xc9phiQXs2FseN8x5GJcL","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Line level. You said the pass on \"for a long time\", \"a thousand\" and \"very\" is next, so I've mostly left those for you. As counts to work from: \"a thousand\" now appears five times (I changed the new \"thousand chits\"), \"for a long time\" three (I cut the one I introduced last round), \"very\" sixteen and \"looked\" twenty-five. I've made three small moves, in Hanne's two speeches and Vael's in the cellar. They're the same words in new places, and the comments explain why.","group":"WHAT'S LEFT","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"EhAf-qbdXAA5t1VnQtFGO"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":35,"createdAt":1794685440771,"updatedAt":1794685595701,"version":2,"doneAt":1794685595701},{"id":"Z_cEkSoR58hPjfDwbGpOD","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Both, I'm afraid. The reader is told what the scheme is four times: by Ilse to Kesh in the rope walk (Ch3), by Ilse and then Kesh in the warehouse (Ch4), in summary to Brennick (Ch5), and by Vael in the cellar (Ch6). Yet at the end I still couldn't have drawn it for you, because the details don't agree with each other:","group":"The scheme: told too often, and never quite pinned down (your question 1)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"},{"kind":"character","id":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":0,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683821490,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683821490},{"id":"aYWlFRmemHfSW4BO_QHoT","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"What is the river door, the water gate, the river gate, the gate, the slip and the culvert? I think it's one gate at the cellar end of one culvert, with the old slip beside it, but you use six names, and in Ch6 the gates open onto the slip rather than the culvert. I've made Ilse say \"river door\" and Hanne say \"water gate\" in Ch3. The rest wants your decision.","group":"The scheme: told too often, and never quite pinned down (your question 1)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"EhAf-qbdXAA5t1VnQtFGO"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":3,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683822790,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683822790},{"id":"jlTvJpMTH8MvcdgptrHpu","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"The prose is good, and the line edit is light because of it. You were right about \"looked\", \"turned\" and \"hand\": I count 66, 27 and 53, in all their forms. About two dozen of my changes cut them. But look at how many of your \"hand\"s are about handwriting and where a hand goes: Pell's hand, the cellarman's careful round hand, Ilse's hand going to the page and Brennick watching it go. That's a motif, and I've left those alone. The ones I cut are stage business: hats turned in hands, lanterns in hands, faces wiped with the backs of hands.","group":"Prose","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"Gli2vCm9fdUmDG0ELTIlL"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":29,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683833188,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683833188},{"id":"juTXnTb7F-cDIvbm5VTC-","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Tobin saw Corran talking to Vael by the clock a week ago, and the ending never explains it. If it's part of Vael's slow case, Vael should say so in Ch6. Otherwise it's a cheat rather than a red herring.","group":"Also worth looking at, under the same heading","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"},{"kind":"character","id":"cY_CqgHaFVrViHC17jmnd"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":16,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683828422,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683828422},{"id":"ltEkEnz-OaZRgAfPFsC8W","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Plant two other things earlier. First, the forged mark. Ilse has seen the Harl mark more often than anyone in Varrow, so let her notice something not quite right on the warehouse sacks in Ch4 and fail to place it; then the extra mast in Ch6 is a clue rather than a fact. Second, Mott. He's first named in Ch6 as if we know him. I've named him once in Ch2, where Ilse reads the cellarman's hand. That also makes \"M. says he can manage her\" properly double-edged. If M. is Mott, Kesh's \"any other M. in a town full of them\" is playing fair with the reader.","group":"The ending (your question 4)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":26,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683832754,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683832754},{"id":"nRubzkZpgr6EgQ51IHiNN","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"My suggestion is to split it. Chapter Six ends with \"I wrote it all down.\" A new short Chapter Seven gives us the hearing as a scene: not all of it, one exchange. The magistrate's question about whether she wrote it at the time, and her showing him where the ink changes when she cut her pen, is already perfect. Write that as dialogue and you have the payoff of the book's whole idea, that a clerk's honest record is a kind of truth. Then the boots, then Sefa and the bread.","group":"The ending (your question 4)","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"FQtZAtEyI13cdoVe1DMNS"},{"kind":"character","id":"86XxjRM6tp1_kkH4xzJcc"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":21,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683830589,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683830589},{"id":"oT_s-GjjwTzzR2JjtDu1W","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"When can the culvert be used? Hanne says \"at the bottom of a spring tide\", and the letter says \"every spring ebb\". Spring tides come twice a month. But the cellar book shows extra sacks \"every week, sometimes twice in a week\" since midsummer. One of these has to give. (Perhaps the culvert can be walked at any low water by someone who knows it, and the spring tides matter for something else, such as the gate, or a full lighter-load.)","group":"The scheme: told too often, and never quite pinned down (your question 1)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"EhAf-qbdXAA5t1VnQtFGO"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":1,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683821921,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683821921},{"id":"oUSEvEbKEvkSccJ63jxM7","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"What was Tobin's lighter for? Kesh says a lighter could bring the salt \"right up the channel to the river side\", and that's why they wanted him. Good. But say once what it would have changed for them: more sacks? Any tide instead of a spring one?","group":"The scheme: told too often, and never quite pinned down (your question 1)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"},{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":5,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683823655,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683823655},{"id":"p40LhgU-QCfT-jxxReIg9","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Tobin tells Sefa about the gig and the man's face, but there's no sign he told Orrin. Why not?","group":"Also worth looking at, under the same heading","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"},{"kind":"character","id":"86XxjRM6tp1_kkH4xzJcc"},{"kind":"character","id":"QTT6KgihBjZcHYe5qBsBk"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":15,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683827988,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683827988},{"id":"sp6xRg_9TQmm27VaoNPfD","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"A few times a cut left two speeches by the same person sitting side by side with nothing between them (Kesh on the crane, Tobin in the cell). I've joined those into single speeches. Have a look and see if you'd rather put a different beat there.","group":"Prose","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"},{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":31,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683833621,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683833621},{"id":"t5sIYk_JqO6AnAJ5bMmBR","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Pell's \"sevens looked like ones\" on page one against \"sixes like his twos\" and the \"thousand twos\" afterwards. The south cellars. \"Before the flats were drained\" (fixed). The stone bollard that creaks (cut). Corran's \"Orrin is here\" in the dark, which reads as Orrin arriving (I've made it \"Orrin isn't coming\"; check that's what you meant). Gas lighting in the hall (made lamps). Vael on the \"bottom step\" after they've come up (made it the top). Both boots on the tally desk when Ilse ran home in one of them. Ferris and the eleven crowns, and Tobin's partner Dall, who are set up and never come back. And the weed line, which is at a man's head outside the warehouse and far above their heads inside.","group":"Smaller continuity, all in the margin","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"Gli2vCm9fdUmDG0ELTIlL"},{"kind":"character","id":"cY_CqgHaFVrViHC17jmnd"},{"kind":"character","id":"QTT6KgihBjZcHYe5qBsBk"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"1KDdSdbuywTtrdenq5dj5"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":28,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794685593001,"version":2,"doneAt":1794685593001},{"id":"vR3JEIiG27GoqgeAvSg9Z","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Ch3: give her a reason. Orrin has just told her the magistrate stops listening after five minutes. A clerk's answer to that is a record.","group":"The ending (your question 4)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"QTT6KgihBjZcHYe5qBsBk"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":23,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683831454,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683831454},{"id":"vga5oR9SmtRtpiZH_iIY2","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"In Ch5, scene 3, why does Ilse go to the Exchange? Brennick has told her to go home and speak to no one, and she does. Something has to send her out again. A note in Vael's name that isn't from Vael would be neat, and would be Brennick's doing.","group":"Also worth looking at, under the same heading","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":14,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683827554,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683827554},{"id":"wrQ956IK7s39R8GB1pI4S","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"Kesh \"watched who did\" it and has seen Corran's real name \"on a letter, in a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight\". That's a promise to the reader, and the name arrives in the epilogue from nowhere. Let Kesh deliver it, or cut the promise.","group":"Also worth looking at, under the same heading","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"},{"kind":"character","id":"cY_CqgHaFVrViHC17jmnd"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":17,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683828855,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683828855},{"id":"yeOR0cz1EHWGTG2kh_xSz","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"The calendar of days holds up well, better than most first drafts I see. As I read it: Mon 10th: the Constant entry at the desk; Sefa at noon; the north quay at evening.\nTue 11th, small hours: the Gull burns (out on the ebb about one, burning about two); Orrin; Ilse in the tally room before four.\nTue day: Hanne (Vael's visit \"on Saturday\", three days ago, is right); Pell missing; Kesh in the rope walk; Orrin at Coil Lane that night; the notebook begins.\nWed: not seen, and that's fine.\nWed night into Thu: the flats, Pell, the letter, the crane.\nThu: back a little before noon; the magistrate put back to Friday; Brennick; Sefa's basket; Vael in the tally room; the flood that night.\nThe following Tuesday: the hearing.","group":"Days, tides and light (your question 2)","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"86XxjRM6tp1_kkH4xzJcc"},{"kind":"character","id":"QTT6KgihBjZcHYe5qBsBk"},{"kind":"character","id":"tJuw34VzaULYeRmFRFX0h"},{"kind":"character","id":"EhAf-qbdXAA5t1VnQtFGO"},{"kind":"character","id":"aXTH5WRX9XqGIdA_t1ure"},{"kind":"character","id":"Gli2vCm9fdUmDG0ELTIlL"},{"kind":"character","id":"pBeu-L_uv0sSY1kaWJd1t"},{"kind":"character","id":"c8DMEe1rTgl3pECficTQ8"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":7,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683824523,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683824523},{"id":"zhuOyPJI_Gvhu-YYq4MaA","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","text":"The marbled notebook isn't planted firmly enough yet. It appears once, at the end of Ch3, with \"She did not know why\", which tells the reader not to notice it (I've cut that line), and next at the bench. Three small moves would fix it:","group":"The ending (your question 4)","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Maren Hollis"},"done":true,"order":22,"createdAt":1794682885541,"updatedAt":1794683831021,"version":2,"doneAt":1794683831021}],"continuitySuppressions":[],"writingLogs":[{"id":"bTalPUBFYvTI6c1ef1TYT","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","date":"2026-11-14","words":12559,"createdAt":1794680379810,"updatedAt":1794697782221}],"sceneRevisions":[{"id":"-3pNfZANn1UvkVvxistg2","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","text":"The tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives watched her go and then stared at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap. Before she let herself think, she took the marbled book down and copied the letter into it, word for word, and drew the crossed keys at the bottom as carefully as she could. Then she thought.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with business with the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. The one who had asked Vael to look into the water gate in the first place — Hanne had said so — and who had been asking for years about the spring tides, every year the water a little higher. The one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\"\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned and looked into the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.","wordCount":1088,"createdAt":1794682772462},{"id":"-NYLBcPVgVFUYb_vyy3il","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"K3nyf6eQkfA7bwMjLc83B","text":"The harbour watch kept its house at the foot of Tarrant Stair, a squat stone building with barred windows that had been a customs post in the old days and still had the king's arms over the door, half worn away by salt. Inside it smelled of tallow and wet wool. There was a long bench, a table, a fire, and at the back an iron door with a grille.\n\nTobin was behind the iron door.\n\n\"He's not under arrest,\" said Captain Orrin. \"Not yet. He's under my eye. There's a difference, and I'd advise you to be grateful for it.\"\n\nDace Orrin was younger than Ilse had expected for a captain of the watch, perhaps thirty, with a long serious face and a scar through one eyebrow that gave him a look of permanent mild surprise. He had taken off his coat and was writing in a daybook at the table, and he did not stop writing while he talked to her.\n\n\"He was in his bed,\" Ilse said. \"He went to bed at ten. I heard him.\"\n\n\"Did you hear him get up?\"\n\n\"No. I was asleep.\"\n\n\"Then you heard him go to bed and you didn't hear him get up, and an hour after midnight his boat was seen going out on the ebb, and an hour after that it was burning on the bar, and he was in the water beside it.\" Orrin dipped his pen. \"Miss Marrow, I'm not saying he set it. I'm saying I've a burnt lighter across the main channel at the bottom of the tide and every ship due in on the morning flood will have to stand off until we clear it, and the owner of the lighter was the only man found in the water.\"\n\n\"It's his boat. Why would he burn his own boat?\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" said Orrin, without looking up. \"Debt. A woman. A quarrel with his partner. I've seen men burn their boats for all of those, and once for a dog.\" He wrote something. \"What's the matter with his hand?\"\n\n\"I don't know. He was hurt. He was hurt getting her off, or putting the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"He says he woke and the boat was gone and he went down to the quay and saw her going out, and took a skiff after her, and when he got to her she was already burning and there was nobody aboard. He says he burnt his hand on the tiller trying to bring her round.\" Orrin looked up at last. \"Does that sound like your brother?\"\n\nIt did. It sounded exactly like Tobin.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It sounds like a story to me,\" said Orrin. \"But I've been wrong.\" He closed the daybook. \"Go home, Miss Marrow. He'll be here in the morning. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"That's three days.\"\n\n\"It's two. Today's Tuesday.\" He stood, and she saw that he was tired, and that the hand he put out to take his coat off the chair was not steady either. \"If there's anything you know about how that boat got out to the bar, now is the time to tell me. Not Thursday. Now.\"\n\nShe thought of the southern man with the ring. She thought of the river door, bricked up, and the four sacks of Harl white, and Master Vael closing the ledger gently, like a book someone had fallen asleep over. She thought of Tobin saying *don't tell anyone*.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"Nothing.\"\n\nOrrin looked at her for a moment longer than was comfortable. Then he nodded, as though she had told him something after all, and opened the street door for her.\n\nOutside, Sefa was waiting on the step, her apron wrapped round her shoulders against the cold. The fog had gone. The sky over the flats was black and pricked with stars, and the tide was coming back in, filling the channels with a sound like a long slow pour of sand.\n\n\"Well?\" said Sefa.\n\n\"Thursday. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Then we've got until Thursday.\" Sefa put her arm through Ilse's and started walking her up Tarrant Stair. \"What do you need?\"\n\nIlse stopped on the stair.\n\n\"Sefa,\" she said. \"What were you doing on the roof at Orley's at one in the morning?\"\n\n\"Stirring the vat. I told you. It goes green.\"\n\n\"The vat's in the yard. Not on the roof.\"\n\nSefa looked at her, and then laughed, a short tired laugh with not much in it.\n\n\"There's a man,\" she said. \"He's a ropemaker. We go up on the roof. It's the only place Mistress Orley can't hear the door. Is that what you want to know?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Don't be. He's not much of a man. He talks about hemp.\" Sefa tugged her on up the stair. \"But I saw what I saw, Ilse. I saw the boat go out. There was a light on her when she went out, a little light at the stern, like a lantern. And there was somebody at the tiller. I couldn't see who. But they were steering. Somebody was steering her out to the bar.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"","wordCount":861,"createdAt":1794682764883},{"id":"-Trr_tmDeHQpnTUj3gAq5","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, the cellarman's careful round hand. She turned back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nAt midsummer it stopped. Before midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing that she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.\n","wordCount":448,"createdAt":1794680428021},{"id":"-ycsy2KI9uLX7aPIqdaMD","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"KRsrJ5iPhhiIQpc1GaDZW","text":"The captain's leave, as it turned out, could be had for a basket.\n\nSefa brought bread, and a pot of Mistress Orley's fish stew with a cloth tied over it, and a clean shirt of Tobin's that she had taken off the line at Coil Lane, and a little jar of goose grease for his hand. She set them down on the table in front of the sergeant with the cold and smiled at him, and kept smiling, until he got up and unlocked the iron door.\n\nTobin was sitting on the bench in the cell with his back against the wall. His left hand was bound up in grey rag. He looked up when she came in and his whole face changed, and then changed again, carefully, into something else.\n\n\"Sefa.\"\n\n\"You owe me a shilling,\" said Sefa. She sat down on the bench beside him and put the basket on his knees. \"Eat that. Mistress Orley made it and she'll ask.\"\n\nHe ate. He ate like a man who has had nothing but watch-house gruel for three days, which he had, and she watched him and did not say anything until the pot was empty.\n\n\"Where's Ilse?\"\n\n\"Out. She left me a note last night. *Gone to look at something.*\" Sefa took his bad hand and began to unwind the rag. \"She came back at noon looking like something the tide brought in, and went to the watch, and then she went home and put her good shawl on and went up the Mount.\"\n\n\"The Mount? Who does she know on the Mount?\"\n\n\"Nobody. That's what worries me.\" Sefa got the rag off. The burn ran across his palm and up the inside of his fingers, shiny and red. She opened the goose grease. \"Hold still.\"\n\nHe held still. She worked the grease into the burn, gently, and he did not make a sound, though his jaw went tight.\n\n\"I saw her go out,\" Sefa said. \"The *Gull*. I was on the roof at Orley's. Somebody was steering her. I told Ilse and I've told the captain.\"\n\n\"I know. He told me. Sefa. When I got to her, out at the bar. She was already burning. Nobody aboard. But there was a boat pulling away. A gig, four oars, going east, towards the drowned warehouses. I saw it against the fire. And there was a man in the stern of it, and he turned round to look at the *Gull*, and the light was on his face.\"\n\n\"Did you know him?\"\n\n\"I didn't think so. Not then.\" Tobin was quiet. \"But I've been sitting here three days with nothing to do but think, and I've been thinking about it. And I've seen him since. I've seen him in the Exchange. Talking to Master Vael by the clock, a week back, the day I came in for my pay. A big man, soft-spoken. Southern. With a ring.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" said Sefa.\n\nTobin stared at her. \"How do you know that name?\"\n\n\"Because there's a girl called Kesh who drinks at the Lantern and talks too much when she's had three, and she's been talking about a man called Corran since the summer.\" Sefa wound a clean strip of linen round his hand, firmly. \"Half the rope walk knows that name, Tobin. We just don't know who it belongs to.\"\n\n\"Tell Ilse,\" Tobin said. \"Tell her Vael knows him. Tell her tonight.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Sefa.\" He caught her hand with his good one. \"Tell her not to go up the Mount again.\"","wordCount":592,"createdAt":1794682773331},{"id":"0LxapV7W4yjJATOX28KTN","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","text":"She did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the west side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the lamps under the great clock. In their light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"with a pot of stew and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. But your brother did, and he was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the bottom step, holding his head. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin looked at him for a moment. Then he turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"The letter's ash,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"","wordCount":505,"createdAt":1794682776112},{"id":"1Jwfod0xpoGKRjTU2T0Da","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","text":"The wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a westerly behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the river door tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the river door had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer. A course at a time. From the other side.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Nobody's coming,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use.\" Brennick came down the last step and looked around the vault, at the salt, at the gates, at the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought about not giving it. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the river door. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.","wordCount":954,"createdAt":1794682775135},{"id":"1SiLTrlihTyuz3paSoUFS","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book toward her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and very tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"\n\n\n# Spring Tide\n\n## The River Door\n[\n  Place: #Cellar Three\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Merrit Vael @@Corran @@Aud Brennick\n  Items: ^Cellar page:lost\n]\n\nThe wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a westerly behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the river door tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the river door had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer. A course at a time. From the other side.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Orrin is here,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head. He looked up at her.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use.\" Brennick came down the last step and looked around the vault, at the salt, at the gates, at the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought about not giving it. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the river door. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Salt Exchange\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Tobin Marrow @@Sefa @@Dace Orrin @@Merrit Vael @@Corran @@Aud Brennick\n  Items: ^The letter:destroyed\n]\n\nShe did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the west side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the gas under the great clock. In its light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"with a pot of stew and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. But your brother did, and he was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the bottom step, holding his head. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin looked at him for a moment. Then he turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"The letter's ash,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"","wordCount":1898,"createdAt":1794680446273},{"id":"1pGUh2OY5jGh7E9_27q21","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to clear her brother by hanging a councillor.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday afternoon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate studied the page. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick with him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":726,"createdAt":1794685301297},{"id":"2revTPK4OLYa1M5krwXg6","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","text":"She did not find Pell. Pell, the north quay foreman told her, had not come in that morning, nor sent word, and his wife had been round twice asking for him. The foreman was not worried. Pell drank, he said, and when he drank he sometimes slept in the sail lofts.\n\nIlse tried the sail lofts. Pell was not there. She went back to the Exchange and did a day's tally with her copy of the culvert in her sleeve and the page from cellar three against her ribs, and she did not make a single mistake, which she was afterwards rather proud of.\n\nIt was dark when she came out. The streets of the old town were narrow and crooked and the lamps were few, and she had walked them all her life and knew every step, which is how she could tell, by the bottom of Weld Street, that she was being followed.\n\nShe did what her father had taught her. She did not hurry. She did not look round. She turned into the rope walk, a covered alley three hundred yards long where the ropemakers twisted hemp in the daytime, and which at night was empty and dark and smelled of tar. Halfway down there was a place where the roof had fallen in and you could step out of the walk into the yard of a cooper's. She stepped out, and waited behind a stack of staves, and counted.\n\nAt thirty, a figure came past the gap in the roof. It stopped. It looked into the cooper's yard.\n\n\"You're very good,\" it said. \"But you breathe like a bellows.\"\n\nIt was a woman. She was young, younger than Ilse, with cropped black hair and a sailor's coat too big for her, and she stood in the gap with her hands in her pockets as though she had all the time in the world.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"Kesh.\" The woman came into the yard and sat down on an upturned barrel. \"Just Kesh. You're the Marrow girl. Tally clerk. Sister of the man who burned his boat.\"\n\n\"He didn't burn it.\"\n\n\"I know he didn't. I watched who did.\" Kesh smiled. She had a gap between her front teeth. \"That's what I've come to sell you.\"\n\nIlse came out from behind the staves.\n\n\"Sell.\"\n\n\"I'm a woman of business. Everybody in Varrow is. You count salt, I carry things.\" Kesh took a hand out of her pocket, palm up. \"Five crowns.\"\n\n\"I haven't got five crowns.\"\n\n\"Then you can't afford what I know.\" Kesh shrugged and began to stand.\n\n\"Wait.\" Ilse put her hand to her sleeve, where the copy of the culvert was. \"I can't pay you. But I can tell you something you don't know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"There's a culvert,\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange. It runs from the old water gate out to the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Somebody's using it to bring white salt into cellar three that never went through the scales. Three or four sacks a time, since midsummer. And the master of the guild went and looked at the plans of it on Saturday.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for a while. Somewhere out in the old town a dog was barking, and a woman was shouting at the dog, and the dog was not listening.\n\n\"That's worth something,\" Kesh said at last. \"That's worth something to the right people. All right, Marrow. Here's mine for yours. The man who took your brother's boat out was a southerner. Big, soft-spoken, wears a seal ring. I've carried for him, once or twice. He calls himself Corran. That's not his name.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Because I've seen his real name,\" said Kesh. \"On a letter. In a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight.\" She stood and pulled the big coat around her. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. Low water's about four in the morning. If you want to see where your culvert comes out, meet me at the end of the east causeway at two, Wednesday night, and wear boots you don't love.\"\n\n\"Thursday's when the magistrate sits.\"\n\n\"Then you'll want to be back by breakfast,\" said Kesh, and went out through the gap in the roof, and was gone.","wordCount":713,"createdAt":1794685275163},{"id":"3ZRBLck4YuzUC25KnXySl","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","text":"The tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives turned to watch her go and then turned to stare at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap and thought.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with business with the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. The one who had asked Vael to look into the water gate in the first place — Hanne had said so — and who had been asking for years about the spring tides, every year the water a little higher. The one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\"\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned and looked into the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.","wordCount":1058,"createdAt":1794682772344},{"id":"4YNTlkK0GQvEMhk4d4jnd","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round in his hands. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not look surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me last night, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. She did not know why. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.\n\n\n# The East Flats\n\n## The Causeway\n[\n  Place: #East Causeway\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Kesh\n]\n\nThe east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, very far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had been the warehouses of the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at midnight in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern in her hand and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She looked up and found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, and turned until it was behind her, and looked along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"\n\n## Below the Weed Line\n[\n  Place: #The Drowned Warehouse\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Kesh @@Gerrit Pell:dead\n  Items: ^The letter:@Kesh\n]\n\nInside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse slowly. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three. And then it's in the cellar book. And then it's real.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. Nobody can ever say it wasn't. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. \"Duty-free Harl white, laundered through the Exchange. You could sell that anywhere.\" She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it by hand through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and looked at Pell for a long time without saying anything.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"Yesterday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drowned Warehouse\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Kesh\n]\n\nThey did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in all night. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had.\" She looked down at the water. \"I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face with the back of her hand. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea went from black to grey to a thin, dirty white. The tide turned. Ilse felt it turn, somehow, though she could see nothing change: some pressure went off the air. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.\n","wordCount":3002,"createdAt":1794680434061},{"id":"58tR6q_pA9lohSKoGDpdz","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Kghlu5z8Cey3C2HTmpZvF","text":"The east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, very far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had been the warehouses of the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at midnight in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern in her hand and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She looked up and found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, and turned until it was behind her, and looked along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"","wordCount":684,"createdAt":1794682769497},{"id":"5xDk6wTDT_7pLQptyfiCz","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday. He heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, and finally Ilse. She had brought her own ledger: a small book with a marbled cover, in which, every night since the night of the north quay, she had written down everything she had seen and when. The four sacks. The cellar book entries, copied out week by week, back to midsummer. The culvert. The letter, word for word, with the mark of the crossed keys drawn at the bottom as carefully as she could draw it. The magistrate read it through twice, and asked her whether she had written it at the time or afterward, and she told him at the time, every night, and showed him where the ink changed when her pen had needed cutting.\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. Corran's real name turned out to be Anselm Rook, a factor of the Harl salt houses, wanted in two other ports. Councillor Brennick was held for the assize.\n\nKesh did not come to the hearing. But on the morning after it, when Ilse came into the Exchange at six with her sleeves already pinned, there was a pair of sea boots standing on the tally desk, her father's, the one she had lost on the crane wall and the one she had not, and stuffed into the toe of the first was a scrap of paper that said, in a sprawling hand, *you owe me five crowns.*\n\nSefa came at noon with bread.\n\n\"Your counting face,\" she said, sitting on the step below the desk, which was still not allowed. \"The old one. The good one.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"It is.\" Sefa tore the loaf. \"What are you counting?\"\n\nIlse showed her. It was the cellar book for cellar three, dried out page by page in front of the fire in the tally room, cockled and stained and smelling of the sea. At the ninth, where the stub of the cut page was, she had pasted in a fresh leaf. On it, in her own hand, she had written the entry for the *Constant* as it should have been.\n\nForty-two sacks of Harl white. Received at the north quay. Weighed. Entered.\n\nAnd underneath, smaller, in the margin, where a clerk notes a correction:\n\n*Four sacks struck. See the magistrate's record. — I. M.*\n\n\"There,\" said Ilse. \"Now it adds.\"\n","wordCount":534,"createdAt":1794682776963},{"id":"61wEdu8OVHRAcq_WrE69b","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in all night. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had.\" She looked down at the water. \"I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face with the back of her hand. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea went from black to grey to a thin, dirty white. The tide turned. Ilse felt it turn, somehow, though she could see nothing change: some pressure went off the air. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.","wordCount":546,"createdAt":1794682771363},{"id":"6M15f0m2V__JdgYhHNFO8","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face. Then he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to hang the woman who accused her brother.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday, at noon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate looked at the page for a while. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick with him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":731,"createdAt":1794685300982},{"id":"6RmJa_ufhpLKbP1Pq8nRR","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, just above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth. Each sack was stamped with a mark Ilse could have drawn in her sleep: the Harl mark, the little ship under full sail. White salt. Something in the ship caught at her, the way a wrong figure catches in a column before you have found it, and she bent closer with the lantern, and could not find it, and let it go.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. Four sacks on a man's back at every low water is a living. Forty in a lighter up the old channel at the top of a spring tide, straight in through the gate, is a fortune. That's what the gate's being cleared for.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and said nothing.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"On Monday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, on Monday night, late. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the gate is yours at every spring flood until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1063,"createdAt":1794697780087},{"id":"7FRf2Yq4JEOHJGnfzq8eo","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"K3nyf6eQkfA7bwMjLc83B","text":"The harbour watch kept its house at the foot of Tarrant Stair, a squat stone building with barred windows that had been a customs post in the old days and still had the king's arms over the door, half worn away by salt. Inside it smelled of tallow and wet wool. There was a long bench, a table, a fire, and at the back an iron door with a grille.\n\nTobin was behind the iron door.\n\n\"He's not under arrest,\" said Captain Orrin. \"Not yet. He's under my eye. There's a difference, and I'd advise you to be grateful for it.\"\n\nDace Orrin was younger than Ilse had expected for a captain of the watch, perhaps thirty, with a long serious face and a scar through one eyebrow that gave him a look of permanent mild surprise. He had taken off his coat and was writing in a daybook at the table, and he did not stop writing while he talked to her.\n\n\"He was in his bed,\" Ilse said. \"He went to bed at ten. I heard him.\"\n\n\"Did you hear him get up?\"\n\n\"No. I was asleep.\"\n\n\"Then you heard him go to bed and you didn't hear him get up, and an hour after midnight his boat was seen going out on the ebb, and an hour after that it was burning on the bar, and he was in the water beside it.\" Orrin dipped his pen. \"Miss Marrow, I'm not saying he set it. I'm saying I've a burnt lighter across the main channel at the bottom of the tide and every ship due in on the morning flood will have to stand off until we clear it, and the owner of the lighter was the only man found in the water.\"\n\n\"It's his boat. Why would he burn his own boat?\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" said Orrin, without looking up. \"Debt. A woman. A quarrel with his partner. I've seen men burn their boats for all of those, and once for a dog.\" He wrote something. \"What's the matter with his hand?\"\n\n\"I don't know. He was hurt. He was hurt getting her off, or putting the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"He says he woke because her chain stopped knocking on the wall under your window, and he went down to the quay and saw her going out, and took a skiff after her, and when he got to her she was already burning and there was nobody aboard. He says he burnt his hand on the tiller trying to bring her round.\" Orrin looked up at last. \"Does that sound like your brother?\"\n\nIt did. It sounded exactly like Tobin.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It sounds like a story to me,\" said Orrin. \"But I've been wrong.\" He closed the daybook. \"Go home, Miss Marrow. He'll be here in the morning. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"That's three days.\"\n\n\"It's two. Today's Tuesday.\" He stood, and she saw that he was tired, and that the hand he put out to take his coat off the chair was not quite steady. \"If there's anything you know about how that boat got out to the bar, now is the time to tell me. Not Thursday. Now.\"\n\nShe thought of the southern man with the ring. She thought of the river door, bricked up, and the four sacks of Harl white, and Master Vael closing the ledger gently, like a book someone had fallen asleep over. She thought of Tobin saying *don't tell anyone*.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"Nothing.\"\n\nOrrin looked at her for a moment longer than was comfortable. Then he nodded, as though she had told him something after all, and opened the street door for her.\n\nOutside, Sefa was waiting on the step, her apron wrapped round her shoulders against the cold. The fog had gone. The sky over the flats was black and pricked with stars, and the tide was coming back in, filling the channels with a sound like a long slow pour of sand.\n\n\"Well?\" said Sefa.\n\n\"Thursday. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Then we've got until Thursday.\" Sefa put her arm through Ilse's and started walking her up Tarrant Stair. \"What do you need?\"\n\nIlse stopped on the stair.\n\n\"Sefa,\" she said. \"What were you doing on the roof at Orley's at one in the morning?\"\n\n\"Stirring the vat. I told you. It goes green.\"\n\n\"The vat's in the yard. Not on the roof.\"\n\nSefa laughed, a short tired laugh with not much in it.\n\n\"There's a man,\" she said. \"He's a ropemaker. We go up on the roof. It's the only place Mistress Orley can't hear the door. Is that what you want to know?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Don't be. He's not much of a man. He talks about hemp.\" Sefa tugged her on up the stair. \"But I saw what I saw, Ilse. I saw the boat go out. There was a light on her when she went out, a little light at the stern, like a lantern. And there was somebody at the tiller. I couldn't see who. But they were steering. Somebody was steering her out to the bar.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"","wordCount":862,"createdAt":1794685519872,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685519872},{"id":"7GqdQP-MvJLEGMmpQRR6S","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","text":"She did not find Pell. Pell, the north quay foreman told her, had not come in that morning, nor sent word, and his wife had been round twice asking for him. The foreman was not worried. Pell drank, he said, and when he drank he sometimes slept in the sail lofts.\n\nIlse tried the sail lofts. Pell was not there. She went back to the Exchange and did a day's tally with her copy of the culvert in her sleeve and the page from cellar three against her ribs, and she did not make a single mistake, which she was afterwards rather proud of.\n\nIt was dark when she came out. The streets of the old town were narrow and crooked and the lamps were few, and she had walked them all her life and knew every step, which is how she could tell, by the bottom of Weld Street, that she was being followed.\n\nShe did what her father had taught her. She did not hurry. She did not look round. She turned into the rope walk, a covered alley three hundred yards long where the ropemakers twisted hemp in the daytime, and which at night was empty and dark and smelled of tar. Halfway down there was a place where the roof had fallen in and you could step out of the walk into the yard of a cooper's. She stepped out, and waited behind a stack of staves, and counted.\n\nAt thirty, a figure came past the gap in the roof. It stopped. It looked into the cooper's yard.\n\n\"You're very good,\" it said. \"But you breathe like a bellows.\"\n\nIt was a woman. She was young, younger than Ilse, with cropped black hair and a sailor's coat too big for her, and she stood in the gap with her hands in her pockets as though she had all the time in the world.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"Kesh.\" The woman came into the yard and sat down on an upturned barrel. \"Just Kesh. You're the Marrow girl. Tally clerk. Sister of the man who burned his boat.\"\n\n\"He didn't burn it.\"\n\n\"I know he didn't. I watched who did.\" Kesh smiled. She had a gap between her front teeth. \"That's what I've come to sell you.\"\n\nIlse came out from behind the staves.\n\n\"Sell.\"\n\n\"I'm a woman of business. Everybody in Varrow is. You count salt, I carry things.\" Kesh took a hand out of her pocket, palm up. \"Five crowns.\"\n\n\"I haven't got five crowns.\"\n\n\"Then you can't afford what I know.\" Kesh shrugged and began to stand.\n\n\"Wait.\" Ilse put her hand to her sleeve, where the copy of the culvert was. \"I can't pay you. But I can tell you something you don't know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"There's a culvert,\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange. It runs from the old water gate out to the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Somebody's using it to bring white salt into cellar three that never went through the scales. Three or four sacks a time, since midsummer. And the master of the guild went and looked at the plans of it on Saturday.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for a while. Somewhere out in the old town a dog was barking, and a woman was shouting at the dog, and the dog was not listening.\n\n\"That's worth something,\" Kesh said at last. \"That's worth something to the right people. All right, Marrow. Here's mine for yours. The man who took your brother's boat out was a southerner. Big, soft-spoken, wears a seal ring. I've carried for him, once or twice. He calls himself Corran. That's not his name.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Because I've seen his real name,\" said Kesh. \"On a letter. In a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight.\" She stood and pulled the big coat around her. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. Bottom of the ebb is two hours before dawn. If you want to see where your culvert comes out, meet me at the end of the east causeway on Wednesday night, and wear boots you don't love.\"\n\n\"Thursday's when the magistrate sits.\"\n\n\"Then you'll want to be back by breakfast,\" said Kesh, and went out through the gap in the roof, and was gone.","wordCount":714,"createdAt":1794683215531,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683215531},{"id":"7LX9qxSuXSPCfVH2_pz1Q","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in all night. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had. I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea stayed black. The water went on rising under them, patient as a clerk, and the chain below their feet swung slower as it filled. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell, and not a line of light anywhere. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.","wordCount":535,"createdAt":1794685277963},{"id":"7wNDHIT_5G951bDY2QuWQ","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","text":"The bell at the Salt Exchange rang six times before the light came over the flats, and by the sixth stroke Ilse Marrow had her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut and the day's first ledger open on the slope of the tally desk.\n\nShe liked this hour. The great hall was empty except for the porters sweeping out yesterday's grit, and the windows along the east wall were still grey, and the only sound was the brooms and, under them, the long breath of the tide going out across the mud. In an hour the hall would be full of factors shouting prices and lightermen shouting back and the clerks of the six houses arguing over whose scales were honest. For now it was hers.\n\nThe ledger was the lading book for the *Constant*, a coaster out of Harl that had come in on the evening tide with forty-two hundredweight of white salt in sacks. Ilse ran her finger down the column. Forty-two sacks, each a hundredweight, each stamped with the Harl mark. Received at the north quay by the weigher, a man named Pell whose sevens looked like ones. Carried by lighter to the Exchange cellars. Entered.\n\nShe turned the page to the cellar book, where the same salt should have been entered again by the cellarman, and frowned.\n\nForty-six.\n\nShe checked the date, checked the mark, checked that she had not turned two pages at once. Forty-six sacks of Harl white, received into cellar three on the night of the ninth. Four more than had come off the ship.\n\n\"Morning, Miss Marrow.\"\n\nShe did not jump, though it was a near thing. Merrit Vael stood at the end of the desk with his hat in his hand. He was the master of the guild, a tall, soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and ink on his cuffs like any clerk, and he had a way of arriving in a room without having walked into it.\n\n\"Master Vael. You're early.\"\n\n\"So are you. So are you always, I'm told.\" He nodded at the open book. \"The *Constant*?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Anything wanting?\"\n\nShe could have said no. Afterwards she would wonder why she didn't, and the only answer she could find was that she had been a tally clerk for six years and her whole worth in the world was that she said what the numbers said.\n\n\"The cellar book has four more sacks than the lading book. Forty-six against forty-two.\"\n\nVael came around the end of the desk and looked. He looked for a long time, long enough that she could hear the porters' brooms again.\n\n\"Pell,\" he said at last. \"Pell writes his sixes like his twos. I've told him.\" He tapped the lading entry with one finger. \"That's a forty-six, Miss Marrow. Look at the tail on it.\"\n\nShe looked. It was a two. She had seen Pell's twos a thousand times and this was one of them, and the tail on it was the tail on all his twos, a little hook like a fishing barb.\n\n\"I'll check with the ship's master,\" she said.\n\n\"The *Constant* sailed on the morning ebb.\" Vael closed the ledger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over. \"Write it up as forty-six. I'll have a word with Pell about his hand.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\nHe smiled at her and put his hat on and went out through the side door, and Ilse sat looking at the closed cover for a while. Then she opened it again and copied both entries, the lading and the cellar, onto a scrap of tally paper, and folded the scrap twice, and put it inside her stays where it lay against her ribs all day like a cold coin.\n\nBy seven the hall was full. Factors from the inland houses stood under the great clock with their samples in little linen bags and tasted salt off the backs of their hands. A Harl agent argued with a Varrow weigher about moisture. Somebody's dog got in and was chased out. Ilse entered the *Grace of Tidings* and the *Meg* and a lighter-load of grey salt from the south pans that was mostly sand, and did not think about the *Constant* more than once an hour.\n\nAt noon Sefa came by with bread.\n\nSefa was apprenticed to a dyer in Weld Street and her hands were blue to the wrist and would be, she said, until she died, and probably after. She sat on the step below the tally desk, which was not allowed, and tore the loaf in half.\n\n\"You've got your counting face on,\" she said.\n\n\"I always have my counting face on. I count.\"\n\n\"Your other counting face. The one where something doesn't add.\" Sefa chewed. \"Is it a man?\"\n\n\"It's four sacks of salt.\"\n\n\"Oh,\" said Sefa. \"Well, that's much worse.\"\n\nIlse almost told her. She had the words in her mouth. But Master Vael crossed the far end of the hall just then, talking with a woman in a grey council cloak, and he glanced up at the tally desk, and Ilse swallowed the words with the bread.\n\n\"Tobin's in tonight,\" she said instead. \"He's bringing the *Gull* round from the south pans. Come and eat with us.\"\n\n\"Can't. Mistress Orley's got a vat of woad going and she says if it isn't stirred on the hour it'll go green, and if it goes green she'll have my hide for a dye bag.\" Sefa stood and brushed the crumbs off. \"Tell your brother he still owes me a shilling.\"\n\n\"He'll say he paid it.\"\n\n\"He'll be lying,\" said Sefa, and went.","wordCount":944,"createdAt":1794682762037},{"id":"8-_ChJcx2zNw-X5V_Vfrm","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"XGmpbItpsK6pu6YzV87iu","text":"Somebody was hammering on the door.\n\nIlse came up out of sleep the way you come up out of cold water, all at once and gasping. The room was dark. The fire was a red eye in the grate. Tobin's cot across the room was empty, the blanket thrown back.\n\n\"Ilse! Ilse, open the door!\"\n\nSefa. She knew the voice before she knew she was standing, and she had the bar off the door and Sefa was in the room, still in her dye apron, her hair coming down, her blue hands grabbing at Ilse's arms.\n\n\"It's the *Gull*,\" Sefa said. \"She's burning. She's out on the bar and she's burning. I saw it from the roof at Orley's, I was up stirring the vat, I saw the light—\"\n\n\"Tobin's here.\" Ilse turned to the cot as if he would be in it. \"He was here. We ate. He went to bed.\"\n\n\"Then where is he?\"\n\nThey ran. Down the stair, along Coil Lane, down the second stair to the quay, where the fog had thinned to rags and there were people already, lightermen in their shirts, a woman with a baby wrapped in a shawl, two boys from the rope walk. Everyone was looking the same way, out across the black water toward the bar, where a long orange smear of fire lay on the dark like a wound.\n\n\"That's the *Gull*,\" said one of the lightermen. \"That's Marrow's boat.\"\n\n\"Who took her out?\" Ilse's voice came out high and strange. \"Who took her out? She was empty. She was moored right there.\"\n\nThe place where the *Gull* had been moored was empty water. The painter was gone from the ring.\n\n\"Saw her go out on the ebb, an hour back,\" said the lighterman, a heavy man named Coby whom Ilse knew a little. \"Thought it was your brother. Didn't think anything of it. Lot of the lads go out to the bar on the ebb to fish the channel.\"\n\n\"In the fog?\"\n\nCoby shrugged, and did not look at her.\n\nOut on the bar the fire leaned over and went down, slowly, the way a candle goes when it falls into its own wax. Then there was only a red glow on the water, and then nothing at all, and the bell buoy ringing.\n\n\"Get a boat,\" Ilse said. \"Somebody get a boat out. He might be in the water.\"\n\nNobody moved. It was Sefa who said it, gently, her blue hand on Ilse's back.\n\n\"Ilse. It's slack water at the bar. If he's in it, he's—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"—he's swimming. Tobin swims like a seal. You know he does.\"\n\nTwo of the lightermen were already unshipping oars. A boat went out, and then another, their lanterns swinging. Ilse stood at the edge of the quay and watched them go into the dark. She found that she had the scrap of tally paper in her hand, though she did not remember taking it out. She had slept with it under her pillow.\n\nThe boats were gone an hour. When they came back, the first held three men and a coil of burnt rope. The second held Tobin.\n\nHe was sitting up in the stern, soaked to the skin and black with soot, his left hand wrapped in somebody's shirt. He was alive. Ilse's knees went, and Sefa held her up, and for a moment the whole quay swam.\n\nThen she saw the two men in the boat behind him, in the dark blue coats of the harbour watch, and the way one of them had his hand on Tobin's collar.","wordCount":601,"createdAt":1794682763896},{"id":"84L_eYXeU4IkYTPHftAT1","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. Nobody can ever say it wasn't. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. \"Duty-free Harl white, laundered through the Exchange. You could sell that anywhere.\" She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it by hand through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and looked at Pell for a long time without saying anything.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"Yesterday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1020,"createdAt":1794682770501},{"id":"8DDNUnFa-UiV3RPN3z4ID","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","text":"The archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river door was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the river was turned, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man. At the bottom of a spring tide. If he didn't mind rats.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse looked at the drawing for a long time. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1005,"createdAt":1794682766821},{"id":"8x24nPxDSNPBS32qRpMo2","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","text":"The wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a westerly behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the river door tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the river door had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer. A course at a time. From the other side.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Nobody's coming,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head. He looked up at her.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use.\" Brennick came down the last step and looked around the vault, at the salt, at the gates, at the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought about not giving it. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the river door. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.","wordCount":959,"createdAt":1794682775096},{"id":"B4US8aAqtOd-uPxJW-6w1","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book towards her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and very tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"","wordCount":388,"createdAt":1794683238662,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683240312},{"id":"BHNDUokTT76q_mo3dcyRe","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","text":"The bell at the Salt Exchange rang six times before the light came over the flats, and by the sixth stroke Ilse Marrow had her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut and the day's first ledger open on the slope of the tally desk.\n\nShe liked this hour. The great hall was empty except for the porters sweeping out yesterday's grit, and the windows along the east wall were still grey, and the only sound was the brooms and, under them, the long breath of the tide going out across the mud. In an hour the hall would be full of factors shouting prices and lightermen shouting back and the clerks of the six houses arguing over whose scales were honest. For now it was hers.\n\nThe ledger was the lading book for the *Constant*, a coaster out of Harl that had come in on the evening tide with forty-two hundredweight of white salt in sacks. Ilse ran her finger down the column. Forty-two sacks, each a hundredweight, each stamped with the Harl mark. Received at the north quay by the weigher, a man named Pell whose sevens looked like ones. Carried by lighter to the Exchange cellars. Entered.\n\nShe turned the page to the cellar book, where the same salt should have been entered again by the cellarman, and frowned.\n\nForty-six.\n\nShe checked the date, checked the mark, checked that she had not turned two pages at once. Forty-six sacks of Harl white, received into cellar three on the night of the ninth. Four more than had come off the ship.\n\n\"Morning, Miss Marrow.\"\n\nShe did not jump, though it was a near thing. Merrit Vael stood at the end of the desk with his hat in his hand. He was the master of the guild, a tall, soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and ink on his cuffs like any clerk, and he had a way of arriving in a room without having walked into it.\n\n\"Master Vael. You're early.\"\n\n\"So are you. So are you always, I'm told.\" He nodded at the open book. \"The *Constant*?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Anything wanting?\"\n\nShe could have said no. Afterwards she would wonder why she didn't, and the only answer she could find was that she had been a tally clerk for six years and her whole worth in the world was that she said what the numbers said.\n\n\"The cellar book has four more sacks than the lading book. Forty-six against forty-two.\"\n\nVael came around the end of the desk and looked. He looked for a long time, long enough that she could hear the porters' brooms again.\n\n\"Pell,\" he said at last. \"Pell writes his sixes like his twos. I've told him.\" He tapped the lading entry with one finger. \"That's a forty-six, Miss Marrow. Look at the tail on it.\"\n\nShe looked. It was a two. She had seen Pell's twos a thousand times and this was one of them, and the tail on it was the tail on all his twos, a little hook like a fishing barb.\n\n\"I'll check with the ship's master,\" she said.\n\n\"The *Constant* sailed on the morning ebb.\" Vael closed the ledger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over. \"Write it up as forty-six. I'll have a word with Pell about his hand.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\nHe smiled at her and put his hat on and went out through the side door, and Ilse sat looking at the closed cover for a while. Then she opened it again and copied both entries, the lading and the cellar, onto a scrap of tally paper, and folded the scrap twice, and put it inside her stays where it lay against her ribs all day like a cold coin.\n\nBy seven the hall was full. Factors from the inland houses stood under the great clock with their samples in little linen bags and tasted salt off the backs of their hands. A Harl agent argued with a Varrow weigher about moisture. Somebody's dog got in and was chased out. Ilse entered the *Grace of Tidings* and the *Meg* and a lighter-load of grey salt from the south pans that was mostly sand, and did not think about the *Constant* more than once an hour.\n\nAt noon Sefa came by with bread.\n\nSefa was apprenticed to a dyer in Weld Street and her hands were blue to the wrist and would be, she said, until she died, and probably after. She sat on the step below the tally desk, which was not allowed, and tore the loaf in half.\n\n\"You've got your counting face on,\" she said.\n\n\"I always have my counting face on. I count.\"\n\n\"Your other counting face. The one where something doesn't add.\" Sefa chewed. \"Is it a man?\"\n\n\"It's four sacks of salt.\"\n\n\"Oh,\" said Sefa. \"Well, that's much worse.\"\n\nIlse almost told her. She had the words in her mouth. But Master Vael crossed the far end of the hall just then, talking with a woman in a grey council cloak, and he glanced up at the tally desk, and Ilse swallowed the words with the bread.\n\n\"Tobin's in tonight,\" she said instead. \"He's bringing the *Gull* round from the south pans. Come and eat with us.\"\n\n\"Can't. Mistress Orley's got a vat of woad going and she says if it isn't stirred on the hour it'll go green, and if it goes green she'll have my hide for a dye bag.\" Sefa stood and brushed the crumbs off. \"Tell your brother he still owes me a shilling.\"\n\n\"He'll say he paid it.\"\n\n\"He'll be lying,\" said Sefa, and went.\n\n[\n  Place: #North Quay\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Tobin Marrow\n]\n\nThe fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all and had never once in his life managed to. In the lamplight his face was grey.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin turned the pipe over and knocked it against the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down toward the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.\n","wordCount":1861,"createdAt":1794680379829},{"id":"Bkd2W--a2nJ22sWVKUMyg","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Kghlu5z8Cey3C2HTmpZvF","text":"The east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, very far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had been the warehouses of the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at two in the morning in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, put it at her back, and sighted along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"","wordCount":679,"createdAt":1794685276096},{"id":"C1Df02P25AIOE4IQKgT8x","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and said nothing for a long time.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"On Monday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":997,"createdAt":1794683220497,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683227096},{"id":"C8sk0NctUn4L_i3hpa0Mk","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. In the lamplight he was grey. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin knocked the pipe out on the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down toward the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.","wordCount":894,"createdAt":1794682763149},{"id":"Cl4sx944PRZCwPK5zgLo7","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in for hours. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had. I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea stayed black. The water went on rising under them, patient as a clerk, and the chain below their feet swung slower as it filled. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell, and not a line of light anywhere. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.","wordCount":535,"createdAt":1794685278074},{"id":"DAZY2C9LQbfGJGY-Y8vj2","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"KRsrJ5iPhhiIQpc1GaDZW","text":"The captain's leave, as it turned out, could be had for a basket.\n\nSefa brought bread, and a pot of Mistress Orley's fish stew with a cloth tied over it, and a clean shirt of Tobin's that she had taken off the line at Coil Lane, and a little jar of goose grease for his hand. She set them down on the table in front of the sergeant with the cold and smiled at him, and kept smiling, until he got up and unlocked the iron door.\n\nTobin was sitting on the bench in the cell with his back against the wall. His left hand was bound up in grey rag. He looked up when she came in and his whole face changed, and then changed again, carefully, into something else.\n\n\"Sefa.\"\n\n\"You owe me a shilling,\" said Sefa. She sat down on the bench beside him and put the basket on his knees. \"Eat that. Mistress Orley made it and she'll ask.\"\n\nHe ate. He ate like a man who has had nothing but watch-house gruel for three days, which he had, and she watched him and did not say anything until the pot was empty.\n\n\"Where's Ilse?\"\n\n\"Out. She left me a note last night. *Gone to look at something.*\" Sefa took his bad hand and began to unwind the rag. \"She came back at noon looking like something the tide brought in, and went to the watch, and then she went home and put her good shawl on and went up the Mount.\"\n\n\"The Mount? Who does she know on the Mount?\"\n\n\"Nobody. That's what worries me.\" Sefa got the rag off. The burn ran across his palm and up the inside of his fingers, shiny and red. She opened the goose grease. \"Hold still.\"\n\nHe held still. She worked the grease into the burn, gently, and he did not make a sound, though his jaw went tight.\n\n\"I saw her go out,\" Sefa said. \"The *Gull*. I was on the roof at Orley's. Somebody was steering her. I told Ilse and I've told the captain.\"\n\n\"I know. He told me. Sefa. When I got to her, out at the bar. She was already burning. Nobody aboard. But there was a boat pulling away. A gig, four oars, going east, toward the drowned warehouses. I saw it against the fire. And there was a man in the stern of it, and he turned round to look at the *Gull*, and the light was on his face.\"\n\n\"Did you know him?\"\n\n\"I didn't think so. Not then.\" Tobin was quiet. \"But I've been sitting here three days with nothing to do but think, and I've been thinking about it. And I've seen him since. I've seen him in the Exchange. Talking to Master Vael by the clock, a week back, the day I came in for my pay. A big man, soft-spoken. Southern. With a ring.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" said Sefa.\n\nTobin stared at her. \"How do you know that name?\"\n\n\"Because there's a girl called Kesh who drinks at the Lantern and talks too much when she's had three, and she's been talking about a man called Corran since the summer.\" Sefa wound a clean strip of linen round his hand, firmly. \"Half the rope walk knows that name, Tobin. We just don't know who it belongs to.\"\n\n\"Tell Ilse,\" Tobin said. \"Tell her Vael knows him. Tell her tonight.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Sefa.\" He caught her hand with his good one. \"Tell her not to go up the Mount again.\"","wordCount":592,"createdAt":1794682773273},{"id":"Dq9uh7SANAj04m1oSKEBd","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, Mott the cellarman's careful round hand. She turned back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nAt midsummer it stopped. Before midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing that she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.","wordCount":449,"createdAt":1794682765815},{"id":"E4wWwYcEwSZVmVNKjS-dc","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, the cellarman's careful round hand. She turned back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nAt midsummer it stopped. Before midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing that she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.\n\n\n# The Archive\n\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Hanne Sallow\n  Items: ^Culvert plan:@Ilse Marrow\n]\n\nThe archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river gate was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the flats were drained, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man. At the bottom of a spring tide. If he didn't mind rats.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse looked at the drawing for a long time. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1470,"createdAt":1794680428176},{"id":"E6NuhvJyJp6cOpmXPBgo4","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, the cellarman's careful round hand. She turned back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nAt midsummer it stopped. Before midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing that she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.\n\n\n# The Archive\n\n## Hanne\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Hanne Sallow\n  Items: ^Culvert plan:@Ilse Marrow\n]\n\nThe archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river gate was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the flats were drained, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man. At the bottom of a spring tide. If he didn't mind rats.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse looked at the drawing for a long time. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"\n\n## The Rope Walk\n[\n  Place: #The Rope Walk\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Kesh\n]\n\nShe did not find Pell. Pell, the north quay foreman told her, had not come in that morning, nor sent word, and his wife had been round twice asking for him. The foreman was not worried. Pell drank, he said, and when he drank he sometimes slept in the sail lofts.\n\nIlse looked in the sail lofts. Pell was not there. She went back to the Exchange and did a day's tally with her copy of the culvert in her sleeve and the page from cellar three against her ribs, and she did not make a single mistake, which she was afterwards rather proud of.\n\nIt was dark when she came out. The streets of the old town were narrow and crooked and the lamps were few, and she had walked them all her life and knew every step, which is why she knew, by the bottom of Weld Street, that she was being followed.\n\nShe did what her father had taught her. She did not hurry. She did not look round. She turned into the rope walk, which was a long covered alley three hundred yards long where the ropemakers twisted hemp in the daytime, and which at night was empty and dark and smelled of tar. Halfway down there was a place where the roof had fallen in and you could step out of the walk into the yard of a cooper's. She stepped out, and waited behind a stack of staves, and counted.\n\nAt thirty, a figure came past the gap in the roof. It stopped. It looked into the cooper's yard.\n\n\"You're very good,\" it said. \"But you breathe like a bellows.\"\n\nIt was a woman. She was young, younger than Ilse, with cropped black hair and a sailor's coat too big for her, and she stood in the gap with her hands in her pockets as though she had all the time in the world.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"Kesh.\" The woman came into the yard and sat down on an upturned barrel. \"Just Kesh. You're the Marrow girl. Tally clerk. Sister of the man who burned his boat.\"\n\n\"He didn't burn it.\"\n\n\"I know he didn't. I watched who did.\" Kesh smiled. She had a gap between her front teeth. \"That's what I've come to sell you.\"\n\nIlse came out from behind the staves.\n\n\"Sell.\"\n\n\"I'm a woman of business. Everybody in Varrow is. You count salt, I carry things.\" Kesh took a hand out of her pocket and turned it palm up. \"Five crowns.\"\n\n\"I haven't got five crowns.\"\n\n\"Then you can't afford what I know.\" Kesh shrugged and began to stand.\n\n\"Wait.\" Ilse put her hand to her sleeve, where the copy of the culvert was. \"I can't pay you. But I can tell you something you don't know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"There's a culvert,\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange. It runs from the old water gate out to the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Somebody's using it to bring white salt into cellar three that never went through the scales. Three or four sacks a time, since midsummer. And the master of the guild went and looked at the plans of it on Saturday.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for a while. Somewhere out in the old town a dog was barking, and a woman was shouting at the dog, and the dog was not listening.\n\n\"That's worth something,\" Kesh said at last. \"That's worth something to the right people. All right, Marrow. Here's mine for yours. The man who took your brother's boat out was a southerner. Big, soft-spoken, wears a seal ring. I've carried for him, once or twice. He calls himself Corran. That's not his name.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Because I've seen his real name,\" said Kesh. \"On a letter. In a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight.\" She stood and pulled the big coat around her. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. Bottom of the ebb is two hours before dawn. If you want to see where your culvert comes out, meet me at the end of the east causeway on Wednesday night, and wear boots you don't love.\"\n\n\"Thursday's when the magistrate sits.\"\n\n\"Then you'll want to be back by breakfast,\" said Kesh, and went out through the gap in the roof, and was gone.\n\n[\n  Place: #Coil Lane\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Dace Orrin\n  Items: ^Marbled notebook:@Ilse Marrow\n]\n\nCaptain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round in his hands. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not look surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me last night, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. She did not know why. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.\n","wordCount":2914,"createdAt":1794680428035},{"id":"EBIbiX5J8jEZLAFCDltj0","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","text":"She did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the west side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the lamps under the great clock. In their light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"with a pot of stew and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. But your brother did, and he was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the top step of the cellar stair. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"The letter's ash,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"","wordCount":498,"createdAt":1794683253512,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683255162},{"id":"ErtlUNeqt3EdC0GFa7oir","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday. He heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, and finally Ilse. She had brought her own ledger: a small book with a marbled cover, in which, since the night Captain Orrin came to Coil Lane, she had written down everything she had seen and when. The four sacks. The cellar book entries, copied out week by week, back to midsummer. The culvert. The letter, word for word, with the mark of the crossed keys drawn at the bottom as carefully as she could draw it. The magistrate read it through twice, and asked her whether she had written it at the time or afterwards, and she told him at the time, every night, and showed him where the ink changed when her pen had needed cutting.\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. Corran's real name turned out to be Anselm Rook, a factor of the Harl salt houses, wanted in two other ports. Councillor Brennick was held for the assize.\n\nKesh did not come to the hearing. But on the morning after it, when Ilse came into the Exchange at six with her sleeves already pinned, there was a pair of sea boots standing on the tally desk, her father's, the one she had lost on the crane wall and the one she had not, and stuffed into the toe of the first was a scrap of paper that said, in a sprawling hand, *you owe me five crowns.*\n\nSefa came at noon with bread.\n\n\"Your counting face,\" she said, sitting on the step below the desk, which was still not allowed. \"The old one. The good one.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"It is.\" Sefa tore the loaf. \"What are you counting?\"\n\nIlse showed her. It was the cellar book for cellar three, dried out page by page in front of the fire in the tally room, cockled and stained and smelling of the sea. At the ninth, where the stub of the cut page was, she had pasted in a fresh leaf. On it, in her own hand, she had written the entry for the *Constant* as it should have been.\n\nForty-two sacks of Harl white. Received at the north quay. Weighed. Entered.\n\nAnd underneath, smaller, in the margin, where a clerk notes a correction:\n\n*Four sacks struck. See the magistrate's record. — I. M.*\n\n\"There,\" said Ilse. \"Now it adds.\"","wordCount":534,"createdAt":1794682777048},{"id":"F5Z65wqgNYxbcyFkZYjGF","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book toward her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and very tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"\n\n\n# Spring Tide\n\n## The River Door\n[\n  Place: #Cellar Three\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Merrit Vael @@Corran @@Aud Brennick\n  Items: ^Cellar page:lost\n]\n\nThe wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a westerly behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the river door tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the river door had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer. A course at a time. From the other side.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Orrin is here,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head. He looked up at her.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use.\" Brennick came down the last step and looked around the vault, at the salt, at the gates, at the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought about not giving it. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the river door. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.\n\n## Over the Seawall\n[\n  Place: #The Salt Exchange\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Tobin Marrow @@Sefa @@Dace Orrin @@Merrit Vael @@Corran @@Aud Brennick\n  Items: ^The letter:destroyed\n]\n\nShe did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the west side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the gas under the great clock. In its light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"with a pot of stew and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. But your brother did, and he was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the bottom step, holding his head. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin looked at him for a moment. Then he turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"The letter's ash,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Salt Exchange\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Sefa\n]\n\nThey pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday. He heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, and finally Ilse. She had brought her own ledger: a small book with a marbled cover, in which, every night since the night of the north quay, she had written down everything she had seen and when. The four sacks. The cellar book entries, copied out week by week, back to midsummer. The culvert. The letter, word for word, with the mark of the crossed keys drawn at the bottom as carefully as she could draw it. The magistrate read it through twice, and asked her whether she had written it at the time or afterward, and she told him at the time, every night, and showed him where the ink changed when her pen had needed cutting.\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. Corran's real name turned out to be Anselm Rook, a factor of the Harl salt houses, wanted in two other ports. Councillor Brennick was held for the assize.\n\nKesh did not come to the hearing. But on the morning after it, when Ilse came into the Exchange at six with her sleeves already pinned, there was a pair of sea boots standing on the tally desk, her father's, the one she had lost on the crane wall and the one she had not, and stuffed into the toe of the first was a scrap of paper that said, in a sprawling hand, *you owe me five crowns.*\n\nSefa came at noon with bread.\n\n\"Your counting face,\" she said, sitting on the step below the desk, which was still not allowed. \"The old one. The good one.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"It is.\" Sefa tore the loaf. \"What are you counting?\"\n\nIlse showed her. It was the cellar book for cellar three, dried out page by page in front of the fire in the tally room, cockled and stained and smelling of the sea. At the ninth, where the stub of the cut page was, she had pasted in a fresh leaf. On it, in her own hand, she had written the entry for the *Constant* as it should have been.\n\nForty-two sacks of Harl white. Received at the north quay. Weighed. Entered.\n\nAnd underneath, smaller, in the margin, where a clerk notes a correction:\n\n*Four sacks struck. See the magistrate's record. — I. M.*\n\n\"There,\" said Ilse. \"Now it adds.\"\n","wordCount":2446,"createdAt":1794680446212},{"id":"F7uH09HFylHK8AZvSrn0f","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in all night. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had.\" She looked down at the water. \"I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face with the back of her hand. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea went from black to grey to a thin, dirty white. The tide turned. Ilse felt it turn, somehow, though she could see nothing change: some pressure went off the air. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.\n","wordCount":546,"createdAt":1794680440103},{"id":"FeBh_4_7oHW5vwVb6HTpl","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","text":"The tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives watched her go and then stared at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap. Before she let herself think, she took the marbled book down and copied the letter into it, word for word, and drew the crossed keys at the bottom as carefully as she could. Then she thought.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with an interest in the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. The one who had asked Vael to look into the water gate in the first place — Hanne had said so — and who had been asking for years about the spring tides, every year the water a little higher. The one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\"\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned to the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.","wordCount":1087,"createdAt":1794683232046,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683235347},{"id":"GgvfnXgyrM7IOkUbFAHuL","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to clear her brother by hanging a councillor.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday, at noon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate looked at the page for a while. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick with him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":731,"createdAt":1794685301141},{"id":"GnmQRFz9V19KxBx8t7Si9","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Kghlu5z8Cey3C2HTmpZvF","text":"The east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, very far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had been the warehouses of the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at midnight in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She looked up and found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, and turned until it was behind her, and looked along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"","wordCount":681,"createdAt":1794682769554},{"id":"HjBj-MrNheqolX2n8Dnhw","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"K3nyf6eQkfA7bwMjLc83B","text":"The harbour watch kept its house at the foot of Tarrant Stair, a squat stone building with barred windows that had been a customs post in the old days and still had the king's arms over the door, half worn away by salt. Inside it smelled of tallow and wet wool. There was a long bench, a table, a fire, and at the back an iron door with a grille.\n\nTobin was behind the iron door.\n\n\"He's not under arrest,\" said Captain Orrin. \"Not yet. He's under my eye. There's a difference, and I'd advise you to be grateful for it.\"\n\nDace Orrin was younger than Ilse had expected for a captain of the watch, perhaps thirty, with a long serious face and a scar through one eyebrow that gave him a look of permanent mild surprise. He had taken off his coat and was writing in a daybook at the table, and he did not stop writing while he talked to her.\n\n\"He was in his bed,\" Ilse said. \"He went to bed at ten. I heard him.\"\n\n\"Did you hear him get up?\"\n\n\"No. I was asleep.\"\n\n\"Then you heard him go to bed and you didn't hear him get up, and an hour after midnight his boat was seen going out on the ebb, and an hour after that it was burning on the bar, and he was in the water beside it.\" Orrin dipped his pen. \"Miss Marrow, I'm not saying he set it. I'm saying I've a burnt lighter across the main channel at the bottom of the tide and every ship due in on the morning flood will have to stand off until we clear it, and the owner of the lighter was the only man found in the water.\"\n\n\"It's his boat. Why would he burn his own boat?\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" said Orrin, without looking up. \"Debt. A woman. A quarrel with his partner. I've seen men burn their boats for all of those, and once for a dog.\" He wrote something. \"What's the matter with his hand?\"\n\n\"I don't know. He was hurt. He was hurt getting her off, or putting the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"He says he woke and the boat was gone and he went down to the quay and saw her going out, and took a skiff after her, and when he got to her she was already burning and there was nobody aboard. He says he burnt his hand on the tiller trying to bring her round.\" Orrin looked up at last. \"Does that sound like your brother?\"\n\nIt did. It sounded exactly like Tobin.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It sounds like a story to me,\" said Orrin. \"But I've been wrong.\" He closed the daybook. \"Go home, Miss Marrow. He'll be here in the morning. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"That's three days.\"\n\n\"It's two. Today's Tuesday.\" He stood, and she saw that he was tired, and that the hand he put out to take his coat off the chair was not quite steady. \"If there's anything you know about how that boat got out to the bar, now is the time to tell me. Not Thursday. Now.\"\n\nShe thought of the southern man with the ring. She thought of the river door, bricked up, and the four sacks of Harl white, and Master Vael closing the ledger gently, like a book someone had fallen asleep over. She thought of Tobin saying *don't tell anyone*.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"Nothing.\"\n\nOrrin looked at her for a moment longer than was comfortable. Then he nodded, as though she had told him something after all, and opened the street door for her.\n\nOutside, Sefa was waiting on the step, her apron wrapped round her shoulders against the cold. The fog had gone. The sky over the flats was black and pricked with stars, and the tide was coming back in, filling the channels with a sound like a long slow pour of sand.\n\n\"Well?\" said Sefa.\n\n\"Thursday. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Then we've got until Thursday.\" Sefa put her arm through Ilse's and started walking her up Tarrant Stair. \"What do you need?\"\n\nIlse stopped on the stair.\n\n\"Sefa,\" she said. \"What were you doing on the roof at Orley's at one in the morning?\"\n\n\"Stirring the vat. I told you. It goes green.\"\n\n\"The vat's in the yard. Not on the roof.\"\n\nSefa laughed, a short tired laugh with not much in it.\n\n\"There's a man,\" she said. \"He's a ropemaker. We go up on the roof. It's the only place Mistress Orley can't hear the door. Is that what you want to know?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Don't be. He's not much of a man. He talks about hemp.\" Sefa tugged her on up the stair. \"But I saw what I saw, Ilse. I saw the boat go out. There was a light on her when she went out, a little light at the stern, like a lantern. And there was somebody at the tiller. I couldn't see who. But they were steering. Somebody was steering her out to the bar.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"","wordCount":856,"createdAt":1794683208898,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683208898},{"id":"HmqGQE0Tb3zvVOmD3HG2y","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","text":"She did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the west side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the lamps under the great clock. In its light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"with a pot of stew and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. But your brother did, and he was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the bottom step, holding his head. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin looked at him for a moment. Then he turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"The letter's ash,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"","wordCount":505,"createdAt":1794682776071},{"id":"IW3UjMvEW_CQk92uB7PW_","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. In the lamplight he was grey. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin knocked the pipe out on the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down towards the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.","wordCount":884,"createdAt":1794683203951,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683207247},{"id":"J1TMe0J6ki3rqGfNFXyK8","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, Mott the cellarman's careful round hand. She went back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nBefore midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. A page in a book could be written over by whoever had the key to the cupboard. A page inside her stays could not. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.","wordCount":468,"createdAt":1794685273296},{"id":"K7xr9csK-Wjul4K_NXO98","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. In the lamplight he was grey. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all and had never once in his life managed to. In the lamplight his face was grey.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin turned the pipe over and knocked it against the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down toward the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.","wordCount":913,"createdAt":1794682763029},{"id":"Kn-jP6hqW2b8F8bd9N65k","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay with Dall, who had crewed her for six years, and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs. Neither of them said anything for a while. Then Dall said that she had been a good old boat, and Tobin said he supposed they would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, who began with Ferris on the south pans and the eleven crowns owing on the *Gull*'s mast and then set them aside himself, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and who told the court, among a great deal else, that he had written both the notes himself, at Councillor Brennick's table, since a cellarman who copies forty hands a week into a cellar book can copy two more, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to clear her brother by hanging a councillor.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday afternoon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate studied the page. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Your Tuesday has gone brown at the edges,\" he said at last, \"the way gall ink goes when it has had a week in the air. Your Thursday night is still black. A woman may change her pen when she likes, Miss Marrow. She cannot make her ink older.\"\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the witnesses' bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick after him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":849,"createdAt":1794697782108},{"id":"KqzOwbNs3bZCQ5zChQzY0","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round in his hands. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not look surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me last night, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. She did not know why. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.\n\n\n# The East Flats\n\n## The Causeway\n[\n  Place: #East Causeway\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Kesh\n]\n\nThe east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, very far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had been the warehouses of the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at midnight in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern in her hand and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She looked up and found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, and turned until it was behind her, and looked along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drowned Warehouse\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Kesh @@Gerrit Pell:dead\n  Items: ^The letter:@Kesh\n]\n\nInside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse slowly. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three. And then it's in the cellar book. And then it's real.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. Nobody can ever say it wasn't. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. \"Duty-free Harl white, laundered through the Exchange. You could sell that anywhere.\" She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it by hand through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and looked at Pell for a long time without saying anything.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"Yesterday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":2441,"createdAt":1794680434124},{"id":"LQMKkJSenKh4ps25cHvad","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it by hand through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and looked at Pell for a long time without saying anything.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"Yesterday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1002,"createdAt":1794682770612},{"id":"MH37coaVrgRvOCUvZyARR","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","text":"The archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river door was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the river was turned, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. The gate's at the cellar end, the slip beside it where the barges came up, and the culvert runs out from behind the gate. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man could walk it at any low water, if he knew it and didn't mind rats. At the top of a spring tide, with the gate open, you could float a barge up the old channel to it, same as my grandfather did.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse bent over the drawing. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1055,"createdAt":1794685274229},{"id":"MKMSXHeb0hcAATb3JveLe","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book toward her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and very tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"","wordCount":388,"createdAt":1794682774163},{"id":"MvhhgNRxkED9ft3jGGDlk","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","text":"The archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river door was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the river was turned, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man. At the bottom of a spring tide. If he didn't mind rats.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse bent over the drawing. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1001,"createdAt":1794683212213,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683213865},{"id":"N5Os6LbVOZy0MMXy0WtPR","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The note came under the door at Coil Lane a little after eight, folded small, in a hand she knew from years of chits: *Tally room. Nine o'clock. Bring what you took from cellar three. — M. V.*\n\nShe sat with it a long while. Brennick had said speak to no one, and the man who had written this was the man the letter said could manage her. But only someone who had opened the cellar book since Tuesday knew there was anything missing from it to bring; and she wanted, more than she had wanted anything all week, to see his face when she told him the letter was already with the council. A clerk checks a figure against the hand that wrote it.\n\nThen she put her shawl on again, and on her way down Weld Street she left two lines for Sefa at Orley's: *Gone to the tally room, to Vael. If I'm not back by ten, tell Captain Orrin.*\n\nThe Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down. \"You sent for me,\" she said, and held out the note.\n\nVael looked at it. Then he put his hand inside his coat and took out its twin, folded the same way. *Tally room. Nine o'clock. — I. M.* It was not her hand. It was not a bad copy of it.\n\n\"Then neither of us did,\" he said. \"Sit down anyway.\"\n\nShe sat.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book towards her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"","wordCount":614,"createdAt":1794697781032},{"id":"NTmFmKav-zmY27gs2gBLB","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to clear her brother by hanging a councillor.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday afternoon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate looked at the page for a while. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick with him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":730,"createdAt":1794685301220},{"id":"NVgJggw58KxABgcLBbzKt","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"XGmpbItpsK6pu6YzV87iu","text":"Somebody was hammering on the door.\n\nIlse came up out of sleep the way you come up out of cold water, all at once and gasping. The room was dark. The fire was a red eye in the grate. Tobin's cot across the room was empty, the blanket thrown back.\n\n\"Ilse! Ilse, open the door!\"\n\nSefa. She knew the voice before she knew she was standing, and she had the bar off the door and Sefa was in the room, still in her dye apron, her hair coming down, her blue hands grabbing at Ilse's arms.\n\n\"It's the *Gull*,\" Sefa said. \"She's burning. She's out on the bar and she's burning. I saw it from the roof at Orley's, I was up stirring the vat, I saw the light—\"\n\n\"Tobin's here.\" Ilse turned to the cot as if he would be in it. \"He was here. We ate. He went to bed.\"\n\n\"Then where is he?\"\n\nThey ran. Down the stair, along Coil Lane, down the second stair to the quay, where the fog had thinned to rags and there were people already, lightermen in their shirts, a woman with a baby wrapped in a shawl, two boys from the rope walk. Everyone was looking the same way, out across the black water towards the bar, where a long orange smear of fire lay on the dark like a wound.\n\n\"That's the *Gull*,\" said one of the lightermen. \"That's Marrow's boat.\"\n\n\"Who took her out?\" Ilse's voice came out high and strange. \"Who took her out? She was empty. She was moored right there.\"\n\nThe place where the *Gull* had been moored was empty water. The painter was gone from the ring.\n\n\"Saw her go out on the ebb, an hour back,\" said the lighterman, a heavy man named Coby whom Ilse knew a little. \"Thought it was your brother. Didn't think anything of it. Lot of the lads go out to the bar on the ebb to fish the channel.\"\n\n\"In the fog?\"\n\nCoby shrugged, and did not look at her.\n\nOut on the bar the fire leaned over and went down, slowly, the way a candle goes when it falls into its own wax. Then there was only a red glow on the water, and then nothing at all, and the bell buoy ringing.\n\n\"Get a boat,\" Ilse said. \"Somebody get a boat out. He might be in the water.\"\n\nNobody moved. It was Sefa who said it, gently, her blue hand on Ilse's back.\n\n\"Ilse. It's slack water at the bar. If he's in it, he's—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"—he's swimming. Tobin swims like a seal. You know he does.\"\n\nThen two of the lightermen were unshipping oars. A boat went out, and then another, their lanterns swinging. Ilse stood at the edge of the quay and watched them go into the dark. She found that she had the scrap of tally paper in her hand, though she did not remember taking it out. She had slept with it under her pillow.\n\nThe boats were gone an hour. When they came back, the first held three men and a coil of burnt rope. The second held Tobin.\n\nHe was sitting up in the stern, soaked to the skin and black with soot, his left hand wrapped in somebody's shirt. He was alive. Ilse's knees went, and Sefa held her up, and for a moment the whole quay swam.\n\nThen she saw the two men in the boat behind him, in the dark blue coats of the harbour watch, and the way one of them had his hand on Tobin's collar.","wordCount":601,"createdAt":1794682764045},{"id":"OieaZUSOFdzOX9QDYqEFS","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to clear her brother by hanging a councillor.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday afternoon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate studied the page. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the witnesses' bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick with him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":727,"createdAt":1794685301374},{"id":"Ox39FUiCg_psvi2HiJOYq","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday. He heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, and finally Ilse. She had brought her own ledger: a small book with a marbled cover, in which, since the night Captain Orrin came to Coil Lane, she had written down everything she had seen and when. The four sacks. The cellar book entries, copied out week by week, back to midsummer. The culvert. The letter, word for word, with the mark of the crossed keys drawn at the bottom as carefully as she could draw it. The magistrate read it through twice, and asked her whether she had written it at the time or afterwards, and she told him at the time, or as near it as she could, and showed him where the ink changed when her pen had needed cutting.\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. Corran's real name turned out to be Anselm Rook, a factor of the Harl salt houses, wanted in two other ports. Councillor Brennick was held for the assize.","wordCount":309,"createdAt":1794683364243,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683365890},{"id":"QGdMeQLsWM7dX39_iS_Rx","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","text":"The archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river gate was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the flats were drained, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man. At the bottom of a spring tide. If he didn't mind rats.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse looked at the drawing for a long time. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1005,"createdAt":1794682766697},{"id":"QTzLUnzy-xRGtrUrLoVlb","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","text":"The bell at the Salt Exchange rang six times before the light came over the flats, and by the sixth stroke Ilse Marrow had her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut and the day's first ledger open on the slope of the tally desk.\n\nShe liked this hour. The great hall was empty except for the porters sweeping out yesterday's grit, and the windows along the east wall were still grey, and the only sound was the brooms and, under them, the long breath of the tide going out across the mud. In an hour the hall would be full of factors shouting prices and lightermen shouting back and the clerks of the six houses arguing over whose scales were honest. For now it was hers.\n\nThe ledger was the lading book for the *Constant*, a coaster out of Harl that had come in on the evening tide with forty-two hundredweight of white salt in sacks. Ilse ran her finger down the column. Forty-two sacks, each a hundredweight, each stamped with the Harl mark. Received at the north quay by the weigher, a man named Pell whose sevens looked like ones. Carried by lighter to the Exchange cellars. Entered.\n\nShe opened the cellar book, where the same salt should have been entered again by the cellarman, and frowned.\n\nForty-six.\n\nShe checked the date, checked the mark, checked that she had not turned two pages at once. Forty-six sacks of Harl white, received into cellar three on the night of the ninth. Four more than had come off the ship.\n\n\"Morning, Miss Marrow.\"\n\nShe did not jump, though it was a near thing. Merrit Vael stood at the end of the desk with his hat in his hand. He was the master of the guild, a tall, soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and ink on his cuffs like any clerk, and he had a way of arriving in a room without having walked into it.\n\n\"Master Vael. You're early.\"\n\n\"So are you. So are you always, I'm told.\" He nodded at the open book. \"The *Constant*?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Anything wanting?\"\n\nShe could have said no. Afterwards she would wonder why she didn't, and the only answer she could find was that she had been a tally clerk for six years and her whole worth in the world was that she said what the numbers said.\n\n\"The cellar book has four more sacks than the lading book. Forty-six against forty-two.\"\n\nVael came around the end of the desk and looked for a long time, long enough that she could hear the porters' brooms again.\n\n\"Pell,\" he said at last. \"Pell writes his sixes like his twos. I've told him.\" He tapped the lading entry with one finger. \"That's a forty-six, Miss Marrow. Look at the tail on it.\"\n\nIt was a two. She had seen Pell's twos a thousand times and this was one of them, and the tail on it was the tail on all his twos, a little hook like a fishing barb.\n\n\"I'll check with the ship's master,\" she said.\n\n\"The *Constant* sailed on the morning ebb.\" Vael closed the ledger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over. \"Write it up as forty-six. I'll have a word with Pell about his hand.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\nHe smiled at her and put his hat on and went out through the side door, and Ilse sat looking at the closed cover for a while. Then she opened it again and copied both entries, the lading and the cellar, onto a scrap of tally paper, and folded the scrap twice, and put it inside her stays where it lay against her ribs all day like a cold coin.\n\nBy seven the hall was full. Factors from the inland houses stood under the great clock with their samples in little linen bags and tasted salt off the backs of their hands. A Harl agent argued with a Varrow weigher about moisture. Somebody's dog got in and was chased out. Ilse entered the *Grace of Tidings* and the *Meg* and a lighter-load of grey salt from the south pans that was mostly sand, and did not think about the *Constant* more than once an hour.\n\nAt noon Sefa came by with bread.\n\nSefa was apprenticed to a dyer in Weld Street and her hands were blue to the wrist and would be, she said, until she died, and probably after. She sat on the step below the tally desk, which was not allowed, and tore the loaf in half.\n\n\"You've got your counting face on,\" she said.\n\n\"I always have my counting face on. I count.\"\n\n\"Your other counting face. The one where something doesn't add.\" Sefa chewed. \"Is it a man?\"\n\n\"It's four sacks of salt.\"\n\n\"Oh,\" said Sefa. \"Well, that's much worse.\"\n\nIlse almost told her. But Master Vael crossed the far end of the hall just then, talking with a woman in a grey council cloak, and he glanced up at the tally desk, and Ilse swallowed the words with the bread.\n\n\"Tobin's in tonight,\" she said instead. \"He's bringing the *Gull* round from the south pans. Come and eat with us.\"\n\n\"Can't. Mistress Orley's got a vat of woad going and she says if it isn't stirred on the hour it'll go green, and if it goes green she'll have my hide for a dye bag.\" Sefa stood and brushed the crumbs off. \"Tell your brother he still owes me a shilling.\"\n\n\"He'll say he paid it.\"\n\n\"He'll be lying,\" said Sefa, and went.","wordCount":930,"createdAt":1794683202299,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683202299},{"id":"RUMLosr8VQl4Akfdc54HR","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all and had never once in his life managed to. In the lamplight his face was grey.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin turned the pipe over and knocked it against the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down toward the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.\n","wordCount":907,"createdAt":1794680400138},{"id":"RdEGhrZQv2I6eFJS2FZ0r","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round in his hands. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not look surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me last night, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. She did not know why. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.\n\n\n# The East Flats\n\n[\n  Place: #East Causeway\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Kesh\n]\n\nThe east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, very far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had been the warehouses of the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at midnight in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern in her hand and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She looked up and found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, and turned until it was behind her, and looked along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"","wordCount":1391,"createdAt":1794680434184},{"id":"RlpyiDKJEvq39zH9qVCw_","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday. He heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, and finally Ilse. She had brought her own ledger: a small book with a marbled cover, in which, since the night Captain Orrin came to Coil Lane, she had written down everything she had seen and when. The four sacks. The cellar book entries, copied out week by week, back to midsummer. The culvert. The letter, word for word, with the mark of the crossed keys drawn at the bottom as carefully as she could draw it. The magistrate read it through twice, and asked her whether she had written it at the time or afterwards, and she told him at the time, or as near it as she could, and showed him where the ink changed when her pen had needed cutting.\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. Corran's real name turned out to be Anselm Rook, a factor of the Harl salt houses, wanted in two other ports. Councillor Brennick was held for the assize.\n\nKesh did not come to the hearing. But on the morning after it, when Ilse came into the Exchange at six with her sleeves already pinned, there was a pair of sea boots standing on the tally desk, her father's, the one she had lost on the crane wall and the one she had not, and stuffed into the toe of the first was a scrap of paper that said, in a sprawling hand, *you owe me five crowns.*\n\nSefa came at noon with bread.\n\n\"Your counting face,\" she said, sitting on the step below the desk, which was still not allowed. \"The old one. The good one.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"It is.\" Sefa tore the loaf. \"What are you counting?\"\n\nIlse showed her. It was the cellar book for cellar three, dried out page by page in front of the fire in the tally room, cockled and stained and smelling of the sea. At the ninth, where the stub of the cut page was, she had pasted in a fresh leaf. On it, in her own hand, she had written the entry for the *Constant* as it should have been.\n\nForty-two sacks of Harl white. Received at the north quay. Weighed. Entered.\n\nAnd underneath, smaller, in the margin, where a clerk notes a correction:\n\n*Four sacks struck. See the magistrate's record. — I. M.*\n\n\"There,\" said Ilse. \"Now it adds.\"","wordCount":539,"createdAt":1794683303136},{"id":"RnFkA0DPhrTw6VHV8Ff3d","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not look surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me last night, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. She did not know why. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.","wordCount":691,"createdAt":1794682768626},{"id":"SSu-0eKvn1jD9aCLjFs1i","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round in his hands. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not look surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me last night, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. She did not know why. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.\n","wordCount":694,"createdAt":1794680434054},{"id":"UFy-oldjVcEeqIW2pPXhA","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all and had never once in his life managed to. In the lamplight his face was grey.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin turned the pipe over and knocked it against the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down toward the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.\n\n\n# The Bar\n\n## Fire on the Water\n[\n  Place: #North Quay\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Sefa @@Tobin Marrow\n  Items: ^The Gull:destroyed\n]\n\nSomebody was hammering on the door.\n\nIlse came up out of sleep the way you come up out of cold water, all at once and gasping. The room was dark. The fire was a red eye in the grate. Tobin's cot across the room was empty, the blanket thrown back.\n\n\"Ilse! Ilse, open the door!\"\n\nSefa. She knew the voice before she knew she was standing, and she had the bar off the door and Sefa was in the room, still in her dye apron, her hair coming down, her blue hands grabbing at Ilse's arms.\n\n\"It's the *Gull*,\" Sefa said. \"She's burning. She's out on the bar and she's burning. I saw it from the roof at Orley's, I was up stirring the vat, I saw the light—\"\n\n\"Tobin's here.\" Ilse turned to the cot as if he would be in it. \"He was here. We ate. He went to bed.\"\n\n\"Then where is he?\"\n\nThey ran. Down the stair, along Coil Lane, down the second stair to the quay, where the fog had thinned to rags and there were people already, lightermen in their shirts, a woman with a baby wrapped in a shawl, two boys from the rope walk. Everyone was looking the same way, out across the black water toward the bar, where a long orange smear of fire lay on the dark like a wound.\n\n\"That's the *Gull*,\" said one of the lightermen. \"That's Marrow's boat.\"\n\n\"Who took her out?\" Ilse's voice came out high and strange. \"Who took her out? She was empty. She was moored right there.\"\n\nThe place where the *Gull* had been moored was empty water. The painter was gone from the ring.\n\n\"Saw her go out on the ebb, an hour back,\" said the lighterman, a heavy man named Coby whom Ilse knew a little. \"Thought it was your brother. Didn't think anything of it. Lot of the lads go out to the bar on the ebb to fish the channel.\"\n\n\"In the fog?\"\n\nCoby shrugged, and did not look at her.\n\nOut on the bar the fire leaned over and went down, slowly, the way a candle goes when it falls into its own wax. Then there was only a red glow on the water, and then nothing at all, and the bell buoy ringing.\n\n\"Get a boat,\" Ilse said. \"Somebody get a boat out. He might be in the water.\"\n\nNobody moved. It was Sefa who said it, gently, her blue hand on Ilse's back.\n\n\"Ilse. It's slack water at the bar. If he's in it, he's—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"—he's swimming. Tobin swims like a seal. You know he does.\"\n\nTwo of the lightermen were already unshipping oars. A boat went out, and then another, their lanterns swinging. Ilse stood at the edge of the quay and watched them go into the dark. She found that she had the scrap of tally paper in her hand, though she did not remember taking it out. She had slept with it under her pillow.\n\nThe boats were gone an hour. When they came back, the first held three men and a coil of burnt rope. The second held Tobin.\n\nHe was sitting up in the stern, soaked to the skin and black with soot, his left hand wrapped in somebody's shirt. He was alive. Ilse's knees went, and Sefa held her up, and for a moment the whole quay swam.\n\nThen she saw the two men in the boat behind him, in the dark blue coats of the harbour watch, and the way one of them had his hand on Tobin's collar.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Watch House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Dace Orrin @@Tobin Marrow @@Sefa\n]\n\nThe harbour watch kept its house at the foot of Tarrant Stair, a squat stone building with barred windows that had been a customs post in the old days and still had the king's arms over the door, half worn away by salt. Inside it smelled of tallow and wet wool. There was a long bench, a table, a fire, and at the back an iron door with a grille.\n\nTobin was behind the iron door.\n\n\"He's not under arrest,\" said Captain Orrin. \"Not yet. He's under my eye. There's a difference, and I'd advise you to be grateful for it.\"\n\nDace Orrin was younger than Ilse had expected for a captain of the watch, perhaps thirty, with a long serious face and a scar through one eyebrow that gave him a look of permanent mild surprise. He had taken off his coat and was writing in a daybook at the table, and he did not stop writing while he talked to her.\n\n\"He was in his bed,\" Ilse said. \"He went to bed at ten. I heard him.\"\n\n\"Did you hear him get up?\"\n\n\"No. I was asleep.\"\n\n\"Then you heard him go to bed and you didn't hear him get up, and an hour after midnight his boat was seen going out on the ebb, and an hour after that it was burning on the bar, and he was in the water beside it.\" Orrin dipped his pen. \"Miss Marrow, I'm not saying he set it. I'm saying I've a burnt lighter across the main channel at the bottom of the tide and every ship due in on the morning flood will have to stand off until we clear it, and the owner of the lighter was the only man found in the water.\"\n\n\"It's his boat. Why would he burn his own boat?\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" said Orrin, without looking up. \"Debt. A woman. A quarrel with his partner. I've seen men burn their boats for all of those, and once for a dog.\" He wrote something. \"What's the matter with his hand?\"\n\n\"I don't know. He was hurt. He was hurt getting her off, or putting the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"He says he woke and the boat was gone and he went down to the quay and saw her going out, and took a skiff after her, and when he got to her she was already burning and there was nobody aboard. He says he burnt his hand on the tiller trying to bring her round.\" Orrin looked up at last. \"Does that sound like your brother?\"\n\nIt did. It sounded exactly like Tobin. It was the most Tobin thing she had ever heard.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It sounds like a story to me,\" said Orrin. \"But I've been wrong.\" He closed the daybook. \"Go home, Miss Marrow. He'll be here in the morning. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"That's three days.\"\n\n\"It's two. Today's Tuesday.\" He stood, and she saw that he was tired, and that the hand he put out to take his coat off the chair was not steady either. \"If there's anything you know about how that boat got out to the bar, now is the time to tell me. Not Thursday. Now.\"\n\nShe thought of the southern man with the ring. She thought of the river door, bricked up, and the four sacks of Harl white, and Master Vael closing the ledger gently, like a book someone had fallen asleep over. She thought of Tobin saying *don't tell anyone*.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"Nothing.\"\n\nOrrin looked at her for a moment longer than was comfortable. Then he nodded, as though she had told him something after all, and opened the street door for her.\n\nOutside, Sefa was waiting on the step, her apron wrapped round her shoulders against the cold. The fog had gone. The sky over the flats was black and pricked with stars, and the tide was coming back in, filling the channels with a sound like a long slow pour of sand.\n\n\"Well?\" said Sefa.\n\n\"Thursday. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Then we've got until Thursday.\" Sefa put her arm through Ilse's and started walking her up Tarrant Stair. \"What do you need?\"\n\nIlse stopped on the stair.\n\n\"Sefa,\" she said. \"What were you doing on the roof at Orley's at one in the morning?\"\n\n\"Stirring the vat. I told you. It goes green.\"\n\n\"The vat's in the yard. Not on the roof.\"\n\nSefa looked at her, and then laughed, a short tired laugh with not much in it.\n\n\"There's a man,\" she said. \"He's a ropemaker. We go up on the roof. It's the only place Mistress Orley can't hear the door. Is that what you want to know?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Don't be. He's not much of a man. He talks about hemp.\" Sefa tugged her on up the stair. \"But I saw what I saw, Ilse. I saw the boat go out. There was a light on her when she went out, a little light at the stern, like a lantern. And there was somebody at the tiller. I couldn't see who. But they were steering. Somebody was steering her out to the bar.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"","wordCount":2415,"createdAt":1794680400199},{"id":"UziW-SJ03e3QZyLHv-7MH","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"KRsrJ5iPhhiIQpc1GaDZW","text":"The captain's leave, as it turned out, could be had for a basket.\n\nSefa brought bread, and a pot of Mistress Orley's fish stew with a cloth tied over it, and a clean shirt of Tobin's that she had taken off the line at Coil Lane, and a little jar of goose grease for his hand. She set them down on the table in front of the sergeant with the cold and smiled at him, and kept smiling, until he got up and unlocked the iron door.\n\nTobin was sitting on the bench in the cell with his back against the wall. His left hand was bound up in grey rag. He looked up when she came in and his whole face changed, and then changed again, carefully, into something else.\n\n\"Sefa.\"\n\n\"You owe me a shilling,\" said Sefa. She sat down on the bench beside him and put the basket on his knees. \"Eat that. Mistress Orley made it and she'll ask.\"\n\nHe ate. He ate like a man who has had nothing but watch-house gruel for three days, which he had, and she watched him and did not say anything until the pot was empty.\n\n\"Where's Ilse?\"\n\n\"Out. She left me a note last night. *Gone to look at something.*\" Sefa took his bad hand and began to unwind the rag. \"She came back at noon looking like something the tide brought in, and went to the watch, and then she went home and put her good shawl on and went up the Mount.\"\n\n\"The Mount? Who does she know on the Mount?\"\n\n\"Nobody. That's what worries me.\" Sefa got the rag off. The burn ran across his palm and up the inside of his fingers, shiny and red. She opened the goose grease. \"Hold still.\"\n\nHe held still. She worked the grease into the burn, gently, and he did not make a sound, though his jaw went tight.\n\n\"I saw her go out,\" Sefa said. \"The *Gull*. I was on the roof at Orley's. Somebody was steering her. I told Ilse and I've told the captain.\"\n\n\"I know. He told me.\" Tobin looked at his hand. \"Sefa. When I got to her, out at the bar. She was already burning. Nobody aboard. But there was a boat pulling away. A gig, four oars, going east, toward the drowned warehouses. I saw it against the fire. And there was a man in the stern of it, and he turned round to look at the *Gull*, and the light was on his face.\"\n\n\"Did you know him?\"\n\n\"I didn't think so. Not then.\" Tobin was quiet. \"But I've been sitting here three days with nothing to do but think, and I've been thinking about it. And I've seen him since. I've seen him in the Exchange. Talking to Master Vael by the clock, a week back, the day I came in for my pay. A big man, soft-spoken. Southern. With a ring.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" said Sefa.\n\nTobin stared at her. \"How do you know that name?\"\n\n\"Because there's a girl called Kesh who drinks at the Lantern and talks too much when she's had three, and she's been talking about a man called Corran since the summer.\" Sefa wound a clean strip of linen round his hand, firmly. \"Half the rope walk knows that name, Tobin. We just don't know who it belongs to.\"\n\n\"Tell Ilse,\" Tobin said. \"Tell her Vael knows him. Tell her tonight.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Sefa.\" He caught her hand with his good one. \"Tell her not to go up the Mount again.\"","wordCount":597,"createdAt":1794682773231},{"id":"V6t007XGLyDJiHbvOInNv","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"KRsrJ5iPhhiIQpc1GaDZW","text":"The captain's leave, as it turned out, could be had for a basket.\n\nSefa brought bread, and a pot of Mistress Orley's fish stew with a cloth tied over it, and a clean shirt of Tobin's that she had taken off the line at Coil Lane, and a little jar of goose grease for his hand. She set them down on the table in front of the sergeant with the cold and smiled at him, and kept smiling, until he got up and unlocked the iron door.\n\nTobin was sitting on the bench in the cell with his back against the wall. His left hand was bound up in grey rag. He looked up when she came in and his whole face changed, and then changed again, carefully, into something else.\n\n\"Sefa.\"\n\n\"You owe me a shilling,\" said Sefa. She sat down on the bench beside him and put the basket on his knees. \"Eat that. Mistress Orley made it and she'll ask.\"\n\nHe ate. He ate like a man who has had nothing but watch-house gruel for three days, which he had, and she watched him and did not say anything until the pot was empty.\n\n\"Where's Ilse?\"\n\n\"Out. She left me a note last night. *Gone to look at something.*\" Sefa took his bad hand and began to unwind the rag. \"She came back at noon looking like something the tide brought in, and went to the watch, and then she went home and put her good shawl on and went up the Mount.\"\n\n\"The Mount? Who does she know on the Mount?\"\n\n\"Nobody. That's what worries me.\" Sefa got the rag off. The burn ran across his palm and up the inside of his fingers, shiny and red. She opened the goose grease. \"Hold still.\"\n\nHe held still. She worked the grease into the burn, gently, and he did not make a sound, though his jaw went tight.\n\n\"I saw her go out,\" Sefa said. \"The *Gull*. I was on the roof at Orley's. Somebody was steering her. I told Ilse and I've told the captain.\"\n\n\"I know. He told me. Sefa. When I got to her, out at the bar. She was already burning. Nobody aboard. But there was a boat pulling away. A gig, four oars, going east, towards the drowned warehouses. I saw it against the fire. And there was a man in the stern of it, and he turned round to look at the *Gull*, and the light was on his face.\"\n\n\"Did you know him?\"\n\n\"I didn't think so. Not then.\" Tobin was quiet. \"But I've been sitting here three days with nothing to do but think. And I've seen him since. I've seen him in the Exchange. Talking to Master Vael by the clock, a week back, the day I came in for my pay. A big man, soft-spoken. Southern. With a ring.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" said Sefa.\n\nTobin stared at her. \"How do you know that name?\"\n\n\"Because there's a girl called Kesh who drinks at the Lantern and talks too much when she's had three, and she's been talking about a man called Corran since the summer.\" Sefa wound a clean strip of linen round his hand, firmly. \"Half the rope walk knows that name, Tobin. We just don't know who it belongs to.\"\n\n\"Tell Ilse,\" Tobin said. \"Tell her Vael knows him. Tell her tonight.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Sefa.\" He caught her hand with his good one. \"Tell her not to go up the Mount again.\"","wordCount":586,"createdAt":1794683237013,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683237013},{"id":"VKl-JU1WyhGyL-WSi8kDC","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all and had never once in his life managed to. In the lamplight his face was grey.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin turned the pipe over and knocked it against the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down toward the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.","wordCount":907,"createdAt":1794682762963},{"id":"VNZPnL7-hShIanWCFpkeh","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday. He heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, and finally Ilse. She had brought her own ledger: a small book with a marbled cover, in which, since the night Captain Orrin came to Coil Lane, she had written down everything she had seen and when. The four sacks. The cellar book entries, copied out week by week, back to midsummer. The culvert. The letter, word for word, with the mark of the crossed keys drawn at the bottom as carefully as she could draw it. The magistrate read it through twice, and asked her whether she had written it at the time or afterward, and she told him at the time, every night, and showed him where the ink changed when her pen had needed cutting.\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. Corran's real name turned out to be Anselm Rook, a factor of the Harl salt houses, wanted in two other ports. Councillor Brennick was held for the assize.\n\nKesh did not come to the hearing. But on the morning after it, when Ilse came into the Exchange at six with her sleeves already pinned, there was a pair of sea boots standing on the tally desk, her father's, the one she had lost on the crane wall and the one she had not, and stuffed into the toe of the first was a scrap of paper that said, in a sprawling hand, *you owe me five crowns.*\n\nSefa came at noon with bread.\n\n\"Your counting face,\" she said, sitting on the step below the desk, which was still not allowed. \"The old one. The good one.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"It is.\" Sefa tore the loaf. \"What are you counting?\"\n\nIlse showed her. It was the cellar book for cellar three, dried out page by page in front of the fire in the tally room, cockled and stained and smelling of the sea. At the ninth, where the stub of the cut page was, she had pasted in a fresh leaf. On it, in her own hand, she had written the entry for the *Constant* as it should have been.\n\nForty-two sacks of Harl white. Received at the north quay. Weighed. Entered.\n\nAnd underneath, smaller, in the margin, where a clerk notes a correction:\n\n*Four sacks struck. See the magistrate's record. — I. M.*\n\n\"There,\" said Ilse. \"Now it adds.\"","wordCount":534,"createdAt":1794682776998},{"id":"VXb3V-6GQ3pwW1oesiQLB","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book toward her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and very tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"\n\n\n# Spring Tide\n\n[\n  Place: #Cellar Three\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Merrit Vael @@Corran @@Aud Brennick\n  Items: ^Cellar page:lost\n]\n\nThe wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a westerly behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the river door tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the river door had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer. A course at a time. From the other side.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Orrin is here,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head. He looked up at her.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use.\" Brennick came down the last step and looked around the vault, at the salt, at the gates, at the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought about not giving it. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the river door. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.","wordCount":1367,"createdAt":1794680446329},{"id":"VjCIi4gyzvuYHfKRUEeV6","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay with Dall, who had crewed her for six years, and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs. Neither of them said anything for a while. Then Dall said that she had been a good old boat, and Tobin said he supposed they would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, who began with Ferris on the south pans and the eleven crowns owing on the *Gull*'s mast and then set them aside himself; and Vael; and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and who told the court, among a great deal else, that he had written both the notes himself, at Councillor Brennick's table, since a cellarman who copies forty hands a week into a cellar book can copy two more, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to clear her brother by hanging a councillor.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday afternoon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate studied the page. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Your Tuesday has gone brown at the edges,\" he said at last, \"the way gall ink goes when it has had a week in the air. Your Thursday night is still black. A woman may change her pen when she likes, Miss Marrow. She cannot make her ink older.\"\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the witnesses' bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick after him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":849,"createdAt":1794697782218},{"id":"WLQB6Z46iv5z-3frTfeTr","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book toward her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and very tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"\n","wordCount":388,"createdAt":1794680446203},{"id":"WwXSM7AdQ6_yFemmPWp99","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, the cellarman's careful round hand. She turned back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nAt midsummer it stopped. Before midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing that she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.","wordCount":448,"createdAt":1794682765763},{"id":"YKCub8VtRbL3vPhswnoud","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to clear her brother by hanging a councillor.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday afternoon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate studied the page. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the witnesses' bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick after him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":727,"createdAt":1794685521537,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685526487},{"id":"_XdprPDNimg5aayI-4Rrr","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","text":"The wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a north-easter behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the water gate tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the water gate had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer, a course at a time, from the culvert side. Enough for a man with a sack on his back. The rest went this week. Enough for a lighter's load.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing. That isn't Harl salt, Miss Marrow. It's Ost white, from across the water, that no one in Varrow may buy and everyone would, dressed up in a Harl coat.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"I had the man who brings it in under the great clock a week ago, talking prices, so that I could get a look at his ring. All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Nobody's coming,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use. Ten years I asked your guild and my council to open that gate before the sea took the lower town, and ten years you sent it to a committee. So I opened it myself, and found that it paid. The sea's coming in anyway, Merrit. I thought I might as well be rich when it does.\" Brennick came down the last step and took in the vault: the salt, the gates, the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought of the marbled book on the shelf in Coil Lane, the letter in it in her own hand and the keys at the foot of it, which nobody in this cellar knew about. Then she thought about not giving the page. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the water gate. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.","wordCount":1120,"createdAt":1794685329580,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685329580},{"id":"_iR2goB3_AD-08NZy173M","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. \"Duty-free Harl white, laundered through the Exchange. You could sell that anywhere.\" She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it by hand through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and looked at Pell for a long time without saying anything.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"Yesterday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1014,"createdAt":1794682770558},{"id":"_m6c0oHnlA_HCDs-iMjOs","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not seem surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me this morning, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. She did not know why. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.","wordCount":691,"createdAt":1794682768736},{"id":"aLrg5BJcDiX5NmZzVl1PR","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in all night. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had. I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face with the back of her hand. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea went from black to grey to a thin, dirty white. The tide turned. Ilse felt it turn, somehow, though she could see nothing change: some pressure went off the air. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.","wordCount":540,"createdAt":1794682771407},{"id":"bH3Fs2EysEHXJR7_Mv0dr","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","text":"The tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives watched her go and then stared at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap and thought.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with business with the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. The one who had asked Vael to look into the water gate in the first place — Hanne had said so — and who had been asking for years about the spring tides, every year the water a little higher. The one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\"\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned and looked into the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.","wordCount":1054,"createdAt":1794682772400},{"id":"baqzFtDoTyCJwBsZNJCMb","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in all night. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had. I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea went from black to grey to a thin, dirty white. The tide turned. Ilse felt it turn, somehow, though she could see nothing change: some pressure went off the air. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.","wordCount":534,"createdAt":1794683228746,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683230395},{"id":"bb6SZABpYGsl6DHaW6ZCU","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","text":"She did not find Pell. Pell, the north quay foreman told her, had not come in that morning, nor sent word, and his wife had been round twice asking for him. The foreman was not worried. Pell drank, he said, and when he drank he sometimes slept in the sail lofts.\n\nIlse tried the sail lofts. Pell was not there. She went back to the Exchange and did a day's tally with her copy of the culvert in her sleeve and the page from cellar three against her ribs, and she did not make a single mistake, which she was afterwards rather proud of.\n\nIt was dark when she came out. The streets of the old town were narrow and crooked and the lamps were few, and she had walked them all her life and knew every step, which is how she could tell, by the bottom of Weld Street, that she was being followed.\n\nShe did what her father had taught her. She did not hurry. She did not look round. She turned into the rope walk, which was a long covered alley three hundred yards long where the ropemakers twisted hemp in the daytime, and which at night was empty and dark and smelled of tar. Halfway down there was a place where the roof had fallen in and you could step out of the walk into the yard of a cooper's. She stepped out, and waited behind a stack of staves, and counted.\n\nAt thirty, a figure came past the gap in the roof. It stopped. It looked into the cooper's yard.\n\n\"You're very good,\" it said. \"But you breathe like a bellows.\"\n\nIt was a woman. She was young, younger than Ilse, with cropped black hair and a sailor's coat too big for her, and she stood in the gap with her hands in her pockets as though she had all the time in the world.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"Kesh.\" The woman came into the yard and sat down on an upturned barrel. \"Just Kesh. You're the Marrow girl. Tally clerk. Sister of the man who burned his boat.\"\n\n\"He didn't burn it.\"\n\n\"I know he didn't. I watched who did.\" Kesh smiled. She had a gap between her front teeth. \"That's what I've come to sell you.\"\n\nIlse came out from behind the staves.\n\n\"Sell.\"\n\n\"I'm a woman of business. Everybody in Varrow is. You count salt, I carry things.\" Kesh took a hand out of her pocket and turned it palm up. \"Five crowns.\"\n\n\"I haven't got five crowns.\"\n\n\"Then you can't afford what I know.\" Kesh shrugged and began to stand.\n\n\"Wait.\" Ilse put her hand to her sleeve, where the copy of the culvert was. \"I can't pay you. But I can tell you something you don't know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"There's a culvert,\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange. It runs from the old water gate out to the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Somebody's using it to bring white salt into cellar three that never went through the scales. Three or four sacks a time, since midsummer. And the master of the guild went and looked at the plans of it on Saturday.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for a while. Somewhere out in the old town a dog was barking, and a woman was shouting at the dog, and the dog was not listening.\n\n\"That's worth something,\" Kesh said at last. \"That's worth something to the right people. All right, Marrow. Here's mine for yours. The man who took your brother's boat out was a southerner. Big, soft-spoken, wears a seal ring. I've carried for him, once or twice. He calls himself Corran. That's not his name.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Because I've seen his real name,\" said Kesh. \"On a letter. In a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight.\" She stood and pulled the big coat around her. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. Bottom of the ebb is two hours before dawn. If you want to see where your culvert comes out, meet me at the end of the east causeway on Wednesday night, and wear boots you don't love.\"\n\n\"Thursday's when the magistrate sits.\"\n\n\"Then you'll want to be back by breakfast,\" said Kesh, and went out through the gap in the roof, and was gone.","wordCount":720,"createdAt":1794682767743},{"id":"bdzvJ5d5xp8F0WcFojiB2","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, Mott the cellarman's careful round hand. She went back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nBefore midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing that she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.","wordCount":445,"createdAt":1794682765918},{"id":"c79od-wdqh5sl6cwQtaAA","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in for hours. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had. I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea stayed black. The water went on rising under them, patient as a clerk, and the chain below their feet swung slower as the warehouse filled. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell, and not a line of light anywhere. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.","wordCount":536,"createdAt":1794685533139,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685534788},{"id":"cTwKHaNSo7FA-9rtD8IhG","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"K3nyf6eQkfA7bwMjLc83B","text":"The harbour watch kept its house at the foot of Tarrant Stair, a squat stone building with barred windows that had been a customs post in the old days and still had the king's arms over the door, half worn away by salt. Inside it smelled of tallow and wet wool. There was a long bench, a table, a fire, and at the back an iron door with a grille.\n\nTobin was behind the iron door.\n\n\"He's not under arrest,\" said Captain Orrin. \"Not yet. He's under my eye. There's a difference, and I'd advise you to be grateful for it.\"\n\nDace Orrin was younger than Ilse had expected for a captain of the watch, perhaps thirty, with a long serious face and a scar through one eyebrow that gave him a look of permanent mild surprise. He had taken off his coat and was writing in a daybook at the table, and he did not stop writing while he talked to her.\n\n\"He was in his bed,\" Ilse said. \"He went to bed at ten. I heard him.\"\n\n\"Did you hear him get up?\"\n\n\"No. I was asleep.\"\n\n\"Then you heard him go to bed and you didn't hear him get up, and an hour after midnight his boat was seen going out on the ebb, and an hour after that it was burning on the bar, and he was in the water beside it.\" Orrin dipped his pen. \"Miss Marrow, I'm not saying he set it. I'm saying I've a burnt lighter across the main channel at the bottom of the tide and every ship due in on the morning flood will have to stand off until we clear it, and the owner of the lighter was the only man found in the water.\"\n\n\"It's his boat. Why would he burn his own boat?\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" said Orrin, without looking up. \"Debt. A woman. A quarrel with his partner. I've seen men burn their boats for all of those, and once for a dog.\" He wrote something. \"What's the matter with his hand?\"\n\n\"I don't know. He was hurt. He was hurt getting her off, or putting the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"He says he woke and the boat was gone and he went down to the quay and saw her going out, and took a skiff after her, and when he got to her she was already burning and there was nobody aboard. He says he burnt his hand on the tiller trying to bring her round.\" Orrin looked up at last. \"Does that sound like your brother?\"\n\nIt did. It sounded exactly like Tobin.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It sounds like a story to me,\" said Orrin. \"But I've been wrong.\" He closed the daybook. \"Go home, Miss Marrow. He'll be here in the morning. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"That's three days.\"\n\n\"It's two. Today's Tuesday.\" He stood, and she saw that he was tired, and that the hand he put out to take his coat off the chair was not quite steady. \"If there's anything you know about how that boat got out to the bar, now is the time to tell me. Not Thursday. Now.\"\n\nShe thought of the southern man with the ring. She thought of the river door, bricked up, and the four sacks of Harl white, and Master Vael closing the ledger gently, like a book someone had fallen asleep over. She thought of Tobin saying *don't tell anyone*.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"Nothing.\"\n\nOrrin looked at her for a moment longer than was comfortable. Then he nodded, as though she had told him something after all, and opened the street door for her.\n\nOutside, Sefa was waiting on the step, her apron wrapped round her shoulders against the cold. The fog had gone. The sky over the flats was black and pricked with stars, and the tide was coming back in, filling the channels with a sound like a long slow pour of sand.\n\n\"Well?\" said Sefa.\n\n\"Thursday. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Then we've got until Thursday.\" Sefa put her arm through Ilse's and started walking her up Tarrant Stair. \"What do you need?\"\n\nIlse stopped on the stair.\n\n\"Sefa,\" she said. \"What were you doing on the roof at Orley's at one in the morning?\"\n\n\"Stirring the vat. I told you. It goes green.\"\n\n\"The vat's in the yard. Not on the roof.\"\n\nSefa looked at her, and then laughed, a short tired laugh with not much in it.\n\n\"There's a man,\" she said. \"He's a ropemaker. We go up on the roof. It's the only place Mistress Orley can't hear the door. Is that what you want to know?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Don't be. He's not much of a man. He talks about hemp.\" Sefa tugged her on up the stair. \"But I saw what I saw, Ilse. I saw the boat go out. There was a light on her when she went out, a little light at the stern, like a lantern. And there was somebody at the tiller. I couldn't see who. But they were steering. Somebody was steering her out to the bar.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"","wordCount":861,"createdAt":1794682764979},{"id":"ciPbBm-WlJw6GWUBAe06V","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","text":"She did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the west side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the lamps under the great clock. In their light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"with a pot of stew and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. But your brother did, and he was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the top step of the cellar stair. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin looked at him for a moment. Then he turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"The letter's ash,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"","wordCount":506,"createdAt":1794682776143},{"id":"dxl-OF-6NoQzCRVWb6Ugu","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and looked at Pell for a long time without saying anything.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"Yesterday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1000,"createdAt":1794682770683},{"id":"e0a1XZAeIrXQWhOFm2E95","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","text":"The bell at the Salt Exchange rang six times before the light came over the flats, and by the sixth stroke Ilse Marrow had her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut and the day's first ledger open on the slope of the tally desk.\n\nShe liked this hour. The great hall was empty except for the porters sweeping out yesterday's grit, and the windows along the east wall were still grey, and the only sound was the brooms and, under them, the long breath of the tide going out across the mud. In an hour the hall would be full of factors shouting prices and lightermen shouting back and the clerks of the six houses arguing over whose scales were honest. For now it was hers.\n\nThe ledger was the lading book for the *Constant*, a coaster out of Harl that had come in on the evening tide with forty-two hundredweight of white salt in sacks. Ilse ran her finger down the column. Forty-two sacks, each a hundredweight, each stamped with the Harl mark. Received at the north quay by the weigher, a man named Pell whose sevens looked like ones. Carried by lighter to the Exchange cellars. Entered.\n\nShe opened the cellar book, where the same salt should have been entered again by the cellarman, and frowned.\n\nForty-six.\n\nShe checked the date, checked the mark, checked that she had not turned two pages at once. Forty-six sacks of Harl white, received into cellar three on the night of the ninth. Four more than had come off the ship.\n\n\"Morning, Miss Marrow.\"\n\nShe did not jump, though it was a near thing. Merrit Vael stood at the end of the desk with his hat in his hand. He was the master of the guild, a tall, soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and ink on his cuffs like any clerk, and he had a way of arriving in a room without having walked into it.\n\n\"Master Vael. You're early.\"\n\n\"So are you. So are you always, I'm told.\" He nodded at the open book. \"The *Constant*?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Anything wanting?\"\n\nShe could have said no. Afterwards she would wonder why she didn't, and the only answer she could find was that she had been a tally clerk for six years and her whole worth in the world was that she said what the numbers said.\n\n\"The cellar book has four more sacks than the lading book. Forty-six against forty-two.\"\n\nVael came around the end of the desk and looked. He looked for a long time, long enough that she could hear the porters' brooms again.\n\n\"Pell,\" he said at last. \"Pell writes his sixes like his twos. I've told him.\" He tapped the lading entry with one finger. \"That's a forty-six, Miss Marrow. Look at the tail on it.\"\n\nShe looked. It was a two. She had seen Pell's twos a thousand times and this was one of them, and the tail on it was the tail on all his twos, a little hook like a fishing barb.\n\n\"I'll check with the ship's master,\" she said.\n\n\"The *Constant* sailed on the morning ebb.\" Vael closed the ledger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over. \"Write it up as forty-six. I'll have a word with Pell about his hand.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\nHe smiled at her and put his hat on and went out through the side door, and Ilse sat looking at the closed cover for a while. Then she opened it again and copied both entries, the lading and the cellar, onto a scrap of tally paper, and folded the scrap twice, and put it inside her stays where it lay against her ribs all day like a cold coin.\n\nBy seven the hall was full. Factors from the inland houses stood under the great clock with their samples in little linen bags and tasted salt off the backs of their hands. A Harl agent argued with a Varrow weigher about moisture. Somebody's dog got in and was chased out. Ilse entered the *Grace of Tidings* and the *Meg* and a lighter-load of grey salt from the south pans that was mostly sand, and did not think about the *Constant* more than once an hour.\n\nAt noon Sefa came by with bread.\n\nSefa was apprenticed to a dyer in Weld Street and her hands were blue to the wrist and would be, she said, until she died, and probably after. She sat on the step below the tally desk, which was not allowed, and tore the loaf in half.\n\n\"You've got your counting face on,\" she said.\n\n\"I always have my counting face on. I count.\"\n\n\"Your other counting face. The one where something doesn't add.\" Sefa chewed. \"Is it a man?\"\n\n\"It's four sacks of salt.\"\n\n\"Oh,\" said Sefa. \"Well, that's much worse.\"\n\nIlse almost told her. She had the words in her mouth. But Master Vael crossed the far end of the hall just then, talking with a woman in a grey council cloak, and he glanced up at the tally desk, and Ilse swallowed the words with the bread.\n\n\"Tobin's in tonight,\" she said instead. \"He's bringing the *Gull* round from the south pans. Come and eat with us.\"\n\n\"Can't. Mistress Orley's got a vat of woad going and she says if it isn't stirred on the hour it'll go green, and if it goes green she'll have my hide for a dye bag.\" Sefa stood and brushed the crumbs off. \"Tell your brother he still owes me a shilling.\"\n\n\"He'll say he paid it.\"\n\n\"He'll be lying,\" said Sefa, and went.","wordCount":941,"createdAt":1794682762115},{"id":"e3sZfkJLxiJrcWLAMi86h","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Kghlu5z8Cey3C2HTmpZvF","text":"The east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had been the warehouses of the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at two in the morning in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, put it at her back, and sighted along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"","wordCount":678,"createdAt":1794685276174},{"id":"ePuMpwyG5hv0ppfoG66yR","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","text":"The wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a north-easter behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the water gate tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the water gate had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer, a course at a time, from the culvert side. Enough for a man with a sack on his back. The rest went this week. Enough for a lighter's load.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing. That isn't Harl salt, Miss Marrow. It's Ost white, from across the water, that no one in Varrow may buy and everyone would, dressed up in a Harl coat. I had the man who brings it in under the great clock a week ago, talking prices, so that I could get a look at his ring.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Nobody's coming,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use. Ten years I asked your guild and my council to open that gate before the sea took the lower town, and ten years you sent it to a committee. So I opened it myself, and found that it paid. The sea's coming in anyway, Merrit. I thought I might as well be rich when it does.\" Brennick came down the last step and took in the vault: the salt, the gates, the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought of the marbled book on the shelf in Coil Lane, the letter in it in her own hand and the keys at the foot of it, which nobody in this cellar knew about. Then she thought about not giving the page. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the water gate. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.","wordCount":1120,"createdAt":1794685538087,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685538087},{"id":"epGHWjUJ1OWhFaMEu5_XA","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. In the lamplight he was grey. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin turned the pipe over and knocked it against the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down toward the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.","wordCount":897,"createdAt":1794682763086},{"id":"f4tyKiMK0jzFrnlOGGGHJ","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse slowly. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three. And then it's in the cellar book. And then it's real.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. Nobody can ever say it wasn't. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. \"Duty-free Harl white, laundered through the Exchange. You could sell that anywhere.\" She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it by hand through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and looked at Pell for a long time without saying anything.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"Yesterday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1032,"createdAt":1794680757272,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794680757272},{"id":"f9oJzaJmWb-uep0lwEDlb","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","text":"The archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river door was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the river was turned, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man could walk it at any low water, if he knew it and didn't mind rats. At the top of a spring tide, with the gate open, you could float a barge up the old channel to it, same as my grandfather did.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse bent over the drawing. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1031,"createdAt":1794685274292},{"id":"fmVMiwBwbaVXQ5EK6z4Gl","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"XGmpbItpsK6pu6YzV87iu","text":"Somebody was hammering on the door.\n\nIlse came up out of sleep the way you come up out of cold water, all at once and gasping. The room was dark. The fire was a red eye in the grate. Tobin's cot across the room was empty, the blanket thrown back.\n\n\"Ilse! Ilse, open the door!\"\n\nSefa. She knew the voice before she knew she was standing, and she had the bar off the door and Sefa was in the room, still in her dye apron, her hair coming down, her blue hands grabbing at Ilse's arms.\n\n\"It's the *Gull*,\" Sefa said. \"She's burning. She's out on the bar and she's burning. I saw it from the roof at Orley's, I was up stirring the vat, I saw the light—\"\n\n\"Tobin's here.\" Ilse turned to the cot as if he would be in it. \"He was here. We ate. He went to bed.\"\n\n\"Then where is he?\"\n\nThey ran. Down the stair, along Coil Lane, down the second stair to the quay, where the fog had thinned to rags and there were people already, lightermen in their shirts, a woman with a baby wrapped in a shawl, two boys from the rope walk. Everyone was looking the same way, out across the black water towards the bar, where a long orange smear of fire lay on the dark like a wound.\n\n\"That's the *Gull*,\" said one of the lightermen. \"That's Marrow's boat.\"\n\n\"Who took her out?\" Ilse's voice came out high and strange. \"Who took her out? She was empty. She was moored right there.\"\n\nThe place where the *Gull* had been moored was empty water. The painter was gone from the ring.\n\n\"Saw her go out on the ebb, an hour back,\" said the lighterman, a heavy man named Coby whom Ilse knew a little. \"Thought it was your brother. Didn't think anything of it. Lot of the lads go out to the bar on the ebb to fish the channel.\"\n\n\"In the fog?\"\n\nCoby shrugged, and did not look at her.\n\nOut on the bar the fire leaned over and went down, slowly, the way a candle goes when it falls into its own wax. Then there was only a red glow on the water, and then nothing at all, and the bell buoy ringing.\n\n\"Get a boat,\" Ilse said. \"Somebody get a boat out. He might be in the water.\"\n\nNobody moved. It was Sefa who said it, gently, her blue hand on Ilse's back.\n\n\"Ilse. It's slack water at the bar. If he's in it, he's—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"—he's swimming. Tobin swims like a seal. You know he does.\"\n\nTwo of the lightermen were already unshipping oars. A boat went out, and then another, their lanterns swinging. Ilse stood at the edge of the quay and watched them go into the dark. She found that she had the scrap of tally paper in her hand, though she did not remember taking it out. She had slept with it under her pillow.\n\nThe boats were gone an hour. When they came back, the first held three men and a coil of burnt rope. The second held Tobin.\n\nHe was sitting up in the stern, soaked to the skin and black with soot, his left hand wrapped in somebody's shirt. He was alive. Ilse's knees went, and Sefa held her up, and for a moment the whole quay swam.\n\nThen she saw the two men in the boat behind him, in the dark blue coats of the harbour watch, and the way one of them had his hand on Tobin's collar.","wordCount":601,"createdAt":1794682763966},{"id":"gd6Avn2obNx2KLCRBkZzi","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"K3nyf6eQkfA7bwMjLc83B","text":"The harbour watch kept its house at the foot of Tarrant Stair, a squat stone building with barred windows that had been a customs post in the old days and still had the king's arms over the door, half worn away by salt. Inside it smelled of tallow and wet wool. There was a long bench, a table, a fire, and at the back an iron door with a grille.\n\nTobin was behind the iron door.\n\n\"He's not under arrest,\" said Captain Orrin. \"Not yet. He's under my eye. There's a difference, and I'd advise you to be grateful for it.\"\n\nDace Orrin was younger than Ilse had expected for a captain of the watch, perhaps thirty, with a long serious face and a scar through one eyebrow that gave him a look of permanent mild surprise. He had taken off his coat and was writing in a daybook at the table, and he did not stop writing while he talked to her.\n\n\"He was in his bed,\" Ilse said. \"He went to bed at ten. I heard him.\"\n\n\"Did you hear him get up?\"\n\n\"No. I was asleep.\"\n\n\"Then you heard him go to bed and you didn't hear him get up, and an hour after midnight his boat was seen going out on the ebb, and an hour after that it was burning on the bar, and he was in the water beside it.\" Orrin dipped his pen. \"Miss Marrow, I'm not saying he set it. I'm saying I've a burnt lighter across the main channel at the bottom of the tide and every ship due in on the morning flood will have to stand off until we clear it, and the owner of the lighter was the only man found in the water.\"\n\n\"It's his boat. Why would he burn his own boat?\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" said Orrin, without looking up. \"Debt. A woman. A quarrel with his partner. I've seen men burn their boats for all of those, and once for a dog.\" He wrote something. \"What's the matter with his hand?\"\n\n\"I don't know. He was hurt. He was hurt getting her off, or putting the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"He says he woke and the boat was gone and he went down to the quay and saw her going out, and took a skiff after her, and when he got to her she was already burning and there was nobody aboard. He says he burnt his hand on the tiller trying to bring her round.\" Orrin looked up at last. \"Does that sound like your brother?\"\n\nIt did. It sounded exactly like Tobin. It was the most Tobin thing she had ever heard.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It sounds like a story to me,\" said Orrin. \"But I've been wrong.\" He closed the daybook. \"Go home, Miss Marrow. He'll be here in the morning. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"That's three days.\"\n\n\"It's two. Today's Tuesday.\" He stood, and she saw that he was tired, and that the hand he put out to take his coat off the chair was not steady either. \"If there's anything you know about how that boat got out to the bar, now is the time to tell me. Not Thursday. Now.\"\n\nShe thought of the southern man with the ring. She thought of the river door, bricked up, and the four sacks of Harl white, and Master Vael closing the ledger gently, like a book someone had fallen asleep over. She thought of Tobin saying *don't tell anyone*.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"Nothing.\"\n\nOrrin looked at her for a moment longer than was comfortable. Then he nodded, as though she had told him something after all, and opened the street door for her.\n\nOutside, Sefa was waiting on the step, her apron wrapped round her shoulders against the cold. The fog had gone. The sky over the flats was black and pricked with stars, and the tide was coming back in, filling the channels with a sound like a long slow pour of sand.\n\n\"Well?\" said Sefa.\n\n\"Thursday. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Then we've got until Thursday.\" Sefa put her arm through Ilse's and started walking her up Tarrant Stair. \"What do you need?\"\n\nIlse stopped on the stair.\n\n\"Sefa,\" she said. \"What were you doing on the roof at Orley's at one in the morning?\"\n\n\"Stirring the vat. I told you. It goes green.\"\n\n\"The vat's in the yard. Not on the roof.\"\n\nSefa looked at her, and then laughed, a short tired laugh with not much in it.\n\n\"There's a man,\" she said. \"He's a ropemaker. We go up on the roof. It's the only place Mistress Orley can't hear the door. Is that what you want to know?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Don't be. He's not much of a man. He talks about hemp.\" Sefa tugged her on up the stair. \"But I saw what I saw, Ilse. I saw the boat go out. There was a light on her when she went out, a little light at the stern, like a lantern. And there was somebody at the tiller. I couldn't see who. But they were steering. Somebody was steering her out to the bar.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"","wordCount":871,"createdAt":1794682764830},{"id":"h19YzanZpmKuqJXdppXKT","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","text":"The wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a westerly behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the river door tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the river door had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer. A course at a time. From the other side.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Nobody's coming,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use.\" Brennick came down the last step and took in the vault: the salt, the gates, the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought about not giving it. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the river door. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.","wordCount":951,"createdAt":1794683241962,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683251861},{"id":"h6wwJboWdbgkhOihk-KRo","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in all night. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had.\" She looked down at the water. \"I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face with the back of her hand. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea went from black to grey to a thin, dirty white. The tide turned. Ilse felt it turn, somehow, though she could see nothing change: some pressure went off the air. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.\n\n\n# The Mount\n\n[\n  Place: #Brennick's House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Aud Brennick @@Kesh\n  Items: ^The letter:@Aud Brennick\n]\n\nThe tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in like a hand. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives turned to watch her go and then turned to stare at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap and thought.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with business with the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. The one who had asked Vael to look into the water gate in the first place — Hanne had said so — and who had been asking for years about the spring tides, every year the water a little higher. The one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\"\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned and looked into the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.","wordCount":1625,"createdAt":1794680440246},{"id":"hDQWA2A7M0qxMublDc2SR","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","text":"The bell at the Salt Exchange rang six times before the light came over the flats, and by the sixth stroke Ilse Marrow had her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut and the day's first ledger open on the slope of the tally desk.\n\nShe liked this hour. The great hall was empty except for the porters sweeping out yesterday's grit, and the windows along the east wall were still grey, and the only sound was the brooms and, under them, the long breath of the tide going out across the mud. In an hour the hall would be full of factors shouting prices and lightermen shouting back and the clerks of the six houses arguing over whose scales were honest. For now it was hers.\n\nThe ledger was the lading book for the *Constant*, a coaster out of Harl that had come in on the evening tide with forty-two hundredweight of white salt in sacks. Ilse ran her finger down the column. Forty-two sacks, each a hundredweight, each stamped with the Harl mark. Received at the north quay by the weigher, a man named Pell whose sevens looked like ones. Carried by lighter to the Exchange cellars. Entered.\n\nShe opened the cellar book, where the same salt should have been entered again by the cellarman, and frowned.\n\nForty-six.\n\nShe checked the date, checked the mark, checked that she had not turned two pages at once. Forty-six sacks of Harl white, received into cellar three on the night of the ninth. Four more than had come off the ship.\n\n\"Morning, Miss Marrow.\"\n\nShe did not jump, though it was a near thing. Merrit Vael stood at the end of the desk with his hat in his hand. He was the master of the guild, a tall, soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and ink on his cuffs like any clerk, and he had a way of arriving in a room without having walked into it.\n\n\"Master Vael. You're early.\"\n\n\"So are you. So are you always, I'm told.\" He nodded at the open book. \"The *Constant*?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Anything wanting?\"\n\nShe could have said no. Afterwards she would wonder why she didn't, and the only answer she could find was that she had been a tally clerk for six years and her whole worth in the world was that she said what the numbers said.\n\n\"The cellar book has four more sacks than the lading book. Forty-six against forty-two.\"\n\nVael came around the end of the desk and looked for a long time, long enough that she could hear the porters' brooms again.\n\n\"Pell,\" he said at last. \"Pell writes his sixes like his twos. I've told him.\" He tapped the lading entry with one finger. \"That's a forty-six, Miss Marrow. Look at the tail on it.\"\n\nShe looked. It was a two. She had seen Pell's twos a thousand times and this was one of them, and the tail on it was the tail on all his twos, a little hook like a fishing barb.\n\n\"I'll check with the ship's master,\" she said.\n\n\"The *Constant* sailed on the morning ebb.\" Vael closed the ledger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over. \"Write it up as forty-six. I'll have a word with Pell about his hand.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\nHe smiled at her and put his hat on and went out through the side door, and Ilse sat looking at the closed cover for a while. Then she opened it again and copied both entries, the lading and the cellar, onto a scrap of tally paper, and folded the scrap twice, and put it inside her stays where it lay against her ribs all day like a cold coin.\n\nBy seven the hall was full. Factors from the inland houses stood under the great clock with their samples in little linen bags and tasted salt off the backs of their hands. A Harl agent argued with a Varrow weigher about moisture. Somebody's dog got in and was chased out. Ilse entered the *Grace of Tidings* and the *Meg* and a lighter-load of grey salt from the south pans that was mostly sand, and did not think about the *Constant* more than once an hour.\n\nAt noon Sefa came by with bread.\n\nSefa was apprenticed to a dyer in Weld Street and her hands were blue to the wrist and would be, she said, until she died, and probably after. She sat on the step below the tally desk, which was not allowed, and tore the loaf in half.\n\n\"You've got your counting face on,\" she said.\n\n\"I always have my counting face on. I count.\"\n\n\"Your other counting face. The one where something doesn't add.\" Sefa chewed. \"Is it a man?\"\n\n\"It's four sacks of salt.\"\n\n\"Oh,\" said Sefa. \"Well, that's much worse.\"\n\nIlse almost told her. She had the words in her mouth. But Master Vael crossed the far end of the hall just then, talking with a woman in a grey council cloak, and he glanced up at the tally desk, and Ilse swallowed the words with the bread.\n\n\"Tobin's in tonight,\" she said instead. \"He's bringing the *Gull* round from the south pans. Come and eat with us.\"\n\n\"Can't. Mistress Orley's got a vat of woad going and she says if it isn't stirred on the hour it'll go green, and if it goes green she'll have my hide for a dye bag.\" Sefa stood and brushed the crumbs off. \"Tell your brother he still owes me a shilling.\"\n\n\"He'll say he paid it.\"\n\n\"He'll be lying,\" said Sefa, and went.","wordCount":939,"createdAt":1794682762221},{"id":"hFKnwFjUXBxUsyuf-Ojy9","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, just above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth. Each sack was stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times: the Harl mark, the little ship under full sail. White salt. Something in the ship caught at her, the way a wrong figure catches in a column before you have found it, and she bent closer with the lantern, and could not find it, and let it go.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. Four sacks on a man's back at every low water is a living. Forty in a lighter up the old channel at the top of a spring tide, straight in through the gate, is a fortune. That's what the gate's being cleared for.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and said nothing.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"On Monday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, on Monday night, late. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the gate is yours at every spring flood until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1062,"createdAt":1794685531475,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685531475},{"id":"hJUdxiOa2eERFwwFPH2MD","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Kghlu5z8Cey3C2HTmpZvF","text":"The east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, very far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had been the warehouses of the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at midnight in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, put it at her back, and sighted along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"","wordCount":676,"createdAt":1794683218846,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683218846},{"id":"hPYuJexG1BJ3nxIBNvNhm","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not seem surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me this morning, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.","wordCount":686,"createdAt":1794683217179,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683217179},{"id":"hfr8X4kKgtEE5aYfKpeeu","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","text":"The bell at the Salt Exchange rang six times before the light came over the flats, and by the sixth stroke Ilse Marrow had her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut and the day's first ledger open on the slope of the tally desk.\n\nShe liked this hour. The great hall was empty except for the porters sweeping out yesterday's grit, and the windows along the east wall were still grey, and the only sound was the brooms and, under them, the long breath of the tide going out across the mud. In an hour the hall would be full of factors shouting prices and lightermen shouting back and the clerks of the six houses arguing over whose scales were honest. For now it was hers.\n\nThe ledger was the lading book for the *Constant*, a coaster out of Harl that had come in on the evening tide with forty-two hundredweight of white salt in sacks. Ilse ran her finger down the column. Forty-two sacks, each a hundredweight, each stamped with the Harl mark. Received at the north quay by the weigher, a man named Pell whose twos were a byword in the tally room. Carried by lighter to the Exchange cellars. Entered.\n\nShe opened the cellar book, where the same salt should have been entered again by the cellarman, and frowned.\n\nForty-six.\n\nShe checked the date, checked the mark, checked that she had not turned two pages at once. Forty-six sacks of Harl white, received into cellar three on the night of the ninth. Four more than had come off the ship.\n\n\"Morning, Miss Marrow.\"\n\nShe did not jump, though it was a near thing. Merrit Vael stood at the end of the desk with his hat in his hand. He was the master of the guild, a tall, soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and ink on his cuffs like any clerk, and he had a way of arriving in a room without having walked into it.\n\n\"Master Vael. You're early.\"\n\n\"So are you. So are you always, I'm told.\" He nodded at the open book. \"The *Constant*?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Anything wanting?\"\n\nShe could have said no. Afterwards she would wonder why she didn't, and the only answer she could find was that she had been a tally clerk for six years and her whole worth in the world was that she said what the numbers said.\n\n\"The cellar book has four more sacks than the lading book. Forty-six against forty-two.\"\n\nVael came around the end of the desk and looked for a long time, long enough that she could hear the porters' brooms again.\n\n\"Pell,\" he said at last. \"Pell writes his sixes like his twos. I've told him.\" He tapped the lading entry with one finger. \"That's a forty-six, Miss Marrow. Look at the tail on it.\"\n\nIt was a two. She had seen Pell's twos a thousand times and this was one of them, and the tail on it was the tail on all his twos, a little hook like a fishing barb.\n\n\"I'll check with the ship's master,\" she said.\n\n\"The *Constant* sailed on the morning ebb.\" Vael closed the ledger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over. \"Write it up as forty-six. I'll have a word with Pell about his hand.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\nHe smiled at her and put his hat on and went out through the side door, and Ilse sat looking at the closed cover for a while. Then she opened it again and copied both entries, the lading and the cellar, onto a scrap of tally paper, and folded the scrap twice, and put it inside her stays where it lay against her ribs all day like a cold coin.\n\nBy seven the hall was full. Factors from the inland houses stood under the great clock with their samples in little linen bags and tasted salt off the backs of their hands. A Harl agent argued with a Varrow weigher about moisture. Somebody's dog got in and was chased out. Ilse entered the *Grace of Tidings* and the *Meg* and a lighter-load of grey salt from the south pans that was mostly sand, and did not think about the *Constant* more than once an hour.\n\nAt noon Sefa came by with bread.\n\nSefa was apprenticed to a dyer in Weld Street and her hands were blue to the wrist and would be, she said, until she died, and probably after. She sat on the step below the tally desk, which was not allowed, and tore the loaf in half.\n\n\"You've got your counting face on,\" she said.\n\n\"I always have my counting face on. I count.\"\n\n\"Your other counting face. The one where something doesn't add.\" Sefa chewed. \"Is it a man?\"\n\n\"It's four sacks of salt.\"\n\n\"Oh,\" said Sefa. \"Well, that's much worse.\"\n\nIlse almost told her. But Master Vael crossed the far end of the hall just then, talking with a woman in a grey council cloak, and he glanced up at the tally desk, and Ilse swallowed the words with the bread.\n\n\"Tobin's in tonight,\" she said instead. \"He's bringing the *Gull* round from the south pans. Come and eat with us.\"\n\n\"Can't. Mistress Orley's got a vat of woad going and she says if it isn't stirred on the hour it'll go green, and if it goes green she'll have my hide for a dye bag.\" Sefa stood and brushed the crumbs off. \"Tell your brother he still owes me a shilling.\"\n\n\"He'll say he paid it.\"\n\n\"He'll be lying,\" said Sefa, and went.","wordCount":934,"createdAt":1794685528139,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685529804},{"id":"ht5h3JjVESkm3I2pU0XJd","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","text":"She did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the west side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the gas under the great clock. In its light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"with a pot of stew and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. But your brother did, and he was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the bottom step, holding his head. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin looked at him for a moment. Then he turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"The letter's ash,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"","wordCount":505,"createdAt":1794682776029},{"id":"i2WEE2HuA2boXIRrimcqS","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N5Fb_YPhoAqUWmyzlORZt","text":"Kesh did not come to the hearing. But on the morning after it, when Ilse came into the Exchange at six with her sleeves already pinned, there was a pair of sea boots standing on the tally desk, her father's, the one she had lost on the crane wall and the one she had not, and stuffed into the toe of the first was a scrap of paper that said, in a sprawling hand, *you owe me five crowns.*\n\nSefa came at noon with bread.\n\n\"Your counting face,\" she said, sitting on the step below the desk, which was still not allowed. \"The old one. The good one.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"It is.\" Sefa tore the loaf. \"What are you counting?\"\n\nIlse showed her. It was the cellar book for cellar three, dried out page by page in front of the fire in the tally room, cockled and stained and smelling of the sea. At the ninth, where the stub of the cut page was, she had pasted in a fresh leaf. On it, in her own hand, she had written the entry for the *Constant* as it should have been.\n\nForty-two sacks of Harl white. Received at the north quay. Weighed. Entered.\n\nAnd underneath, smaller, in the margin, where a clerk notes a correction:\n\n*Four sacks struck. See the magistrate's record. — I. M.*\n\n\"There,\" said Ilse. \"Now it adds.\"","wordCount":230,"createdAt":1794683367557,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683367557},{"id":"iRe80iqdUShFZMgzx_rFX","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","text":"The tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in like a hand. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives turned to watch her go and then turned to stare at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap and thought.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with business with the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. The one who had asked Vael to look into the water gate in the first place — Hanne had said so — and who had been asking for years about the spring tides, every year the water a little higher. The one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\"\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned and looked into the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.","wordCount":1061,"createdAt":1794682772296},{"id":"itTVqK9tAMKvZ-7VOcSPc","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","text":"She did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the east side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the lamps under the great clock. In their light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"with your note, and a pot of stew, and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. But I know a clerk's hand when I see one, and your brother was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the top step of the cellar stair. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"I burned the letter before you were home,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"","wordCount":513,"createdAt":1794697800551,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794697800551},{"id":"j2Rr3EaKPSR6kSUVjS5FD","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, the cellarman's careful round hand. She turned back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nAt midsummer it stopped. Before midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing that she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.\n\n\n# The Archive\n\n## Hanne\n[\n  Place: #The Archive\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Hanne Sallow\n  Items: ^Culvert plan:@Ilse Marrow\n]\n\nThe archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river gate was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the flats were drained, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man. At the bottom of a spring tide. If he didn't mind rats.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse looked at the drawing for a long time. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Rope Walk\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Kesh\n]\n\nShe did not find Pell. Pell, the north quay foreman told her, had not come in that morning, nor sent word, and his wife had been round twice asking for him. The foreman was not worried. Pell drank, he said, and when he drank he sometimes slept in the sail lofts.\n\nIlse looked in the sail lofts. Pell was not there. She went back to the Exchange and did a day's tally with her copy of the culvert in her sleeve and the page from cellar three against her ribs, and she did not make a single mistake, which she was afterwards rather proud of.\n\nIt was dark when she came out. The streets of the old town were narrow and crooked and the lamps were few, and she had walked them all her life and knew every step, which is why she knew, by the bottom of Weld Street, that she was being followed.\n\nShe did what her father had taught her. She did not hurry. She did not look round. She turned into the rope walk, which was a long covered alley three hundred yards long where the ropemakers twisted hemp in the daytime, and which at night was empty and dark and smelled of tar. Halfway down there was a place where the roof had fallen in and you could step out of the walk into the yard of a cooper's. She stepped out, and waited behind a stack of staves, and counted.\n\nAt thirty, a figure came past the gap in the roof. It stopped. It looked into the cooper's yard.\n\n\"You're very good,\" it said. \"But you breathe like a bellows.\"\n\nIt was a woman. She was young, younger than Ilse, with cropped black hair and a sailor's coat too big for her, and she stood in the gap with her hands in her pockets as though she had all the time in the world.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"Kesh.\" The woman came into the yard and sat down on an upturned barrel. \"Just Kesh. You're the Marrow girl. Tally clerk. Sister of the man who burned his boat.\"\n\n\"He didn't burn it.\"\n\n\"I know he didn't. I watched who did.\" Kesh smiled. She had a gap between her front teeth. \"That's what I've come to sell you.\"\n\nIlse came out from behind the staves.\n\n\"Sell.\"\n\n\"I'm a woman of business. Everybody in Varrow is. You count salt, I carry things.\" Kesh took a hand out of her pocket and turned it palm up. \"Five crowns.\"\n\n\"I haven't got five crowns.\"\n\n\"Then you can't afford what I know.\" Kesh shrugged and began to stand.\n\n\"Wait.\" Ilse put her hand to her sleeve, where the copy of the culvert was. \"I can't pay you. But I can tell you something you don't know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"There's a culvert,\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange. It runs from the old water gate out to the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Somebody's using it to bring white salt into cellar three that never went through the scales. Three or four sacks a time, since midsummer. And the master of the guild went and looked at the plans of it on Saturday.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for a while. Somewhere out in the old town a dog was barking, and a woman was shouting at the dog, and the dog was not listening.\n\n\"That's worth something,\" Kesh said at last. \"That's worth something to the right people. All right, Marrow. Here's mine for yours. The man who took your brother's boat out was a southerner. Big, soft-spoken, wears a seal ring. I've carried for him, once or twice. He calls himself Corran. That's not his name.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Because I've seen his real name,\" said Kesh. \"On a letter. In a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight.\" She stood and pulled the big coat around her. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. Bottom of the ebb is two hours before dawn. If you want to see where your culvert comes out, meet me at the end of the east causeway on Wednesday night, and wear boots you don't love.\"\n\n\"Thursday's when the magistrate sits.\"\n\n\"Then you'll want to be back by breakfast,\" said Kesh, and went out through the gap in the roof, and was gone.","wordCount":2202,"createdAt":1794680428113},{"id":"j3b7Y3Jcx8_I9WMXEerqY","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, Mott the cellarman's careful round hand. She went back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nAt midsummer it stopped. Before midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing that she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.","wordCount":449,"createdAt":1794682765867},{"id":"jCFyv2TgFACOiz-65f0yw","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","text":"The archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river door was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the flats were drained, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man. At the bottom of a spring tide. If he didn't mind rats.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse looked at the drawing for a long time. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1005,"createdAt":1794682766746},{"id":"jWFQpEWSCxGFGb6SPjrYz","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, just above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth. Each sack was stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times: the Harl mark, the little ship under full sail. White salt. Something in the ship caught at her, the way a wrong figure catches in a column before you have found it, and she bent closer with the lantern, and could not find it, and let it go.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. Four sacks on a man's back at every low water is a living. Forty in a lighter up the old channel at the top of a spring tide, straight in through the gate, is a fortune. That's what the gate's being cleared for.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and said nothing for a long time.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"On Monday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, on Monday night, late. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the gate is yours at every spring flood until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1066,"createdAt":1794685277029},{"id":"jYDlQm2puZCMJ7WcHjuf0","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round in his hands. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not look surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me last night, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. She did not know why. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.","wordCount":694,"createdAt":1794682768563},{"id":"kDaI4vrJgiddn2ANygpNi","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"5F_Nstvdk8EfrpeSqEVsZ","text":"She did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, Mott the cellarman's careful round hand. She went back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nBefore midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.","wordCount":444,"createdAt":1794683210565,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794683210565},{"id":"l_hj-LcrmjJugqfApDV1K","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","text":"The tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives watched her go and then stared at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap. For a while she only held it. Then, because she was a clerk and it was the one thing she was sure how to do, she took down the marbled book and entered it: the letter, word for word, every stop where the writer had put one, and under it, small and careful, the two keys in their circle. She wrote the time beside it, as near as she could put it. Only when the ink was dry did she let herself think.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with an interest in the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. One of the six had written this, and she did not know which, and she did not need to. She needed one who could not have.\n\nShe made herself go at it the way she would go at a column that would not add. Whoever wrote the letter wanted the vote on the gate held off until the new year. Aud Brennick had been asking for that vote for ten years, loudly, in the council house and on the quays, every spring when the water came a little higher. She had asked Vael to look into the gate this very summer; Hanne had said so. The one councillor in Varrow who had spent ten years trying to bring the vote on could not be the one keeping it off. It was the first thing all week that had added up cleanly, and Ilse was so glad of it that she did not check the sum a second time.\n\nAnd Brennick was the one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\" She did not say Hanne's name. Hanne was old, and three shawls deep, and had trusted her with a drawer that stuck.\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned to the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.","wordCount":1277,"createdAt":1794685539740,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685539740},{"id":"lk1dI2CAv0tqhLo88HI-5","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay with Dall, who had crewed her for six years, and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs. Neither of them said anything for a while. Then Dall said that she had been a good old boat, and Tobin said he supposed they would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, who began with Ferris on the south pans and the eleven crowns owing on the *Gull*'s mast and then, to the magistrate's visible surprise, set them aside himself, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and who told the court, among a great deal else, that he had written both the notes himself, at Councillor Brennick's table, since a cellarman who copies forty hands a week into a cellar book can copy two more, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to clear her brother by hanging a councillor.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday afternoon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate studied the page. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Your Tuesday has gone brown at the edges,\" he said at last, \"the way gall ink goes when it has had a week in the air. Your Thursday night is still black. A woman may change her pen when she likes, Miss Marrow. She cannot make her ink older.\"\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the witnesses' bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick after him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":854,"createdAt":1794697781983},{"id":"mIhsib0JGO-dS_zhWadgM","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in all night. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had.\" She looked down at the water. \"I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face with the back of her hand. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea went from black to grey to a thin, dirty white. The tide turned. Ilse felt it turn, somehow, though she could see nothing change: some pressure went off the air. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.\n\n\n# The Mount\n\n## The Councillor's House\n[\n  Place: #Brennick's House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Aud Brennick @@Kesh\n  Items: ^The letter:@Aud Brennick\n]\n\nThe tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in like a hand. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives turned to watch her go and then turned to stare at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap and thought.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with business with the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. The one who had asked Vael to look into the water gate in the first place — Hanne had said so — and who had been asking for years about the spring tides, every year the water a little higher. The one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\"\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned and looked into the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.\n\n## Visiting Hours\n[\n  Place: #The Watch House\n  Characters: @@Sefa @@Tobin Marrow\n]\n\nThe captain's leave, as it turned out, could be had for a basket.\n\nSefa brought bread, and a pot of Mistress Orley's fish stew with a cloth tied over it, and a clean shirt of Tobin's that she had taken off the line at Coil Lane, and a little jar of goose grease for his hand. She set them down on the table in front of the sergeant with the cold and smiled at him, and kept smiling, until he got up and unlocked the iron door.\n\nTobin was sitting on the bench in the cell with his back against the wall. His left hand was bound up in grey rag. He looked up when she came in and his whole face changed, and then changed again, carefully, into something else.\n\n\"Sefa.\"\n\n\"You owe me a shilling,\" said Sefa. She sat down on the bench beside him and put the basket on his knees. \"Eat that. Mistress Orley made it and she'll ask.\"\n\nHe ate. He ate like a man who has had nothing but watch-house gruel for three days, which he had, and she watched him and did not say anything until the pot was empty.\n\n\"Where's Ilse?\"\n\n\"Out. She left me a note last night. *Gone to look at something.*\" Sefa took his bad hand and began to unwind the rag. \"She came back at noon looking like something the tide brought in, and went to the watch, and then she went home and put her good shawl on and went up the Mount.\"\n\n\"The Mount? Who does she know on the Mount?\"\n\n\"Nobody. That's what worries me.\" Sefa got the rag off. The burn ran across his palm and up the inside of his fingers, shiny and red. She opened the goose grease. \"Hold still.\"\n\nHe held still. She worked the grease into the burn, gently, and he did not make a sound, though his jaw went tight.\n\n\"I saw her go out,\" Sefa said. \"The *Gull*. I was on the roof at Orley's. Somebody was steering her. I told Ilse and I've told the captain.\"\n\n\"I know. He told me.\" Tobin looked at his hand. \"Sefa. When I got to her, out at the bar. She was already burning. Nobody aboard. But there was a boat pulling away. A gig, four oars, going east, toward the drowned warehouses. I saw it against the fire. And there was a man in the stern of it, and he turned round to look at the *Gull*, and the light was on his face.\"\n\n\"Did you know him?\"\n\n\"I didn't think so. Not then.\" Tobin was quiet. \"But I've been sitting here three days with nothing to do but think, and I've been thinking about it. And I've seen him since. I've seen him in the Exchange. Talking to Master Vael by the clock, a week back, the day I came in for my pay. A big man, soft-spoken. Southern. With a ring.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" said Sefa.\n\nTobin stared at her. \"How do you know that name?\"\n\n\"Because there's a girl called Kesh who drinks at the Lantern and talks too much when she's had three, and she's been talking about a man called Corran since the summer.\" Sefa wound a clean strip of linen round his hand, firmly. \"Half the rope walk knows that name, Tobin. We just don't know who it belongs to.\"\n\n\"Tell Ilse,\" Tobin said. \"Tell her Vael knows him. Tell her tonight.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Sefa.\" He caught her hand with his good one. \"Tell her not to go up the Mount again.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Salt Exchange\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Merrit Vael\n]\n\nThe Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book toward her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and very tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"\n","wordCount":2638,"createdAt":1794680440113},{"id":"n7qLEg8QnfVWxHciai_z3","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The note came under the door at Coil Lane a little after eight, folded small, in a hand she knew from years of chits: *Tally room. Nine o'clock. Come alone. — M. V.* She sat with it a long while. Then she put her shawl on again.\n\nThe Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down. \"You sent for me,\" she said, and held out the note.\n\nVael looked at it. Then he put his hand inside his coat and took out its twin, folded the same way. *Tally room. Nine o'clock. — I. M.* It was not her hand. It was not a bad copy of it.\n\n\"Then neither of us did,\" he said. \"Sit down anyway.\"\n\nShe sat.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book towards her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and very tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"","wordCount":499,"createdAt":1794685516560,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685536438},{"id":"nRrMYA0Gp9JUug55hAJpK","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. In the lamplight he was grey. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin knocked the pipe out on the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down towards the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.","wordCount":894,"createdAt":1794682763233},{"id":"oP1Nir-6-qv6fjAVJJQhf","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all and had never once in his life managed to. In the lamplight his face was grey.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin turned the pipe over and knocked it against the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down toward the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.\n\n\n# The Bar\n\n[\n  Place: #North Quay\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Sefa @@Tobin Marrow\n  Items: ^The Gull:destroyed\n]\n\nSomebody was hammering on the door.\n\nIlse came up out of sleep the way you come up out of cold water, all at once and gasping. The room was dark. The fire was a red eye in the grate. Tobin's cot across the room was empty, the blanket thrown back.\n\n\"Ilse! Ilse, open the door!\"\n\nSefa. She knew the voice before she knew she was standing, and she had the bar off the door and Sefa was in the room, still in her dye apron, her hair coming down, her blue hands grabbing at Ilse's arms.\n\n\"It's the *Gull*,\" Sefa said. \"She's burning. She's out on the bar and she's burning. I saw it from the roof at Orley's, I was up stirring the vat, I saw the light—\"\n\n\"Tobin's here.\" Ilse turned to the cot as if he would be in it. \"He was here. We ate. He went to bed.\"\n\n\"Then where is he?\"\n\nThey ran. Down the stair, along Coil Lane, down the second stair to the quay, where the fog had thinned to rags and there were people already, lightermen in their shirts, a woman with a baby wrapped in a shawl, two boys from the rope walk. Everyone was looking the same way, out across the black water toward the bar, where a long orange smear of fire lay on the dark like a wound.\n\n\"That's the *Gull*,\" said one of the lightermen. \"That's Marrow's boat.\"\n\n\"Who took her out?\" Ilse's voice came out high and strange. \"Who took her out? She was empty. She was moored right there.\"\n\nThe place where the *Gull* had been moored was empty water. The painter was gone from the ring.\n\n\"Saw her go out on the ebb, an hour back,\" said the lighterman, a heavy man named Coby whom Ilse knew a little. \"Thought it was your brother. Didn't think anything of it. Lot of the lads go out to the bar on the ebb to fish the channel.\"\n\n\"In the fog?\"\n\nCoby shrugged, and did not look at her.\n\nOut on the bar the fire leaned over and went down, slowly, the way a candle goes when it falls into its own wax. Then there was only a red glow on the water, and then nothing at all, and the bell buoy ringing.\n\n\"Get a boat,\" Ilse said. \"Somebody get a boat out. He might be in the water.\"\n\nNobody moved. It was Sefa who said it, gently, her blue hand on Ilse's back.\n\n\"Ilse. It's slack water at the bar. If he's in it, he's—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"—he's swimming. Tobin swims like a seal. You know he does.\"\n\nTwo of the lightermen were already unshipping oars. A boat went out, and then another, their lanterns swinging. Ilse stood at the edge of the quay and watched them go into the dark. She found that she had the scrap of tally paper in her hand, though she did not remember taking it out. She had slept with it under her pillow.\n\nThe boats were gone an hour. When they came back, the first held three men and a coil of burnt rope. The second held Tobin.\n\nHe was sitting up in the stern, soaked to the skin and black with soot, his left hand wrapped in somebody's shirt. He was alive. Ilse's knees went, and Sefa held her up, and for a moment the whole quay swam.\n\nThen she saw the two men in the boat behind him, in the dark blue coats of the harbour watch, and the way one of them had his hand on Tobin's collar.","wordCount":1525,"createdAt":1794680400263},{"id":"p43TZsKCBQPYDpEXrvnJJ","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","text":"She did not find Pell. Pell, the north quay foreman told her, had not come in that morning, nor sent word, and his wife had been round twice asking for him. The foreman was not worried. Pell drank, he said, and when he drank he sometimes slept in the sail lofts.\n\nIlse tried the sail lofts. Pell was not there. She went back to the Exchange and did a day's tally with her copy of the culvert in her sleeve and the page from cellar three against her ribs, and she did not make a single mistake, which she was afterwards rather proud of.\n\nIt was dark when she came out. The streets of the old town were narrow and crooked and the lamps were few, and she had walked them all her life and knew every step, which is how she could tell, by the bottom of Weld Street, that she was being followed.\n\nShe did what her father had taught her. She did not hurry. She did not look round. She turned into the rope walk, a covered alley three hundred yards long where the ropemakers twisted hemp in the daytime, and which at night was empty and dark and smelled of tar. Halfway down there was a place where the roof had fallen in and you could step out of the walk into the yard of a cooper's. She stepped out, and waited behind a stack of staves, and counted.\n\nAt thirty, a figure came past the gap in the roof. It stopped. It looked into the cooper's yard.\n\n\"You're very good,\" it said. \"But you breathe like a bellows.\"\n\nIt was a woman. She was young, younger than Ilse, with cropped black hair and a sailor's coat too big for her, and she stood in the gap with her hands in her pockets as though she had all the time in the world.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"Kesh.\" The woman came into the yard and sat down on an upturned barrel. \"Just Kesh. You're the Marrow girl. Tally clerk. Sister of the man who burned his boat.\"\n\n\"He didn't burn it.\"\n\n\"I know he didn't. I watched who did.\" Kesh smiled. She had a gap between her front teeth. \"That's what I've come to sell you.\"\n\nIlse came out from behind the staves.\n\n\"Sell.\"\n\n\"I'm a woman of business. Everybody in Varrow is. You count salt, I carry things.\" Kesh took a hand out of her pocket and turned it palm up. \"Five crowns.\"\n\n\"I haven't got five crowns.\"\n\n\"Then you can't afford what I know.\" Kesh shrugged and began to stand.\n\n\"Wait.\" Ilse put her hand to her sleeve, where the copy of the culvert was. \"I can't pay you. But I can tell you something you don't know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"There's a culvert,\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange. It runs from the old water gate out to the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Somebody's using it to bring white salt into cellar three that never went through the scales. Three or four sacks a time, since midsummer. And the master of the guild went and looked at the plans of it on Saturday.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for a while. Somewhere out in the old town a dog was barking, and a woman was shouting at the dog, and the dog was not listening.\n\n\"That's worth something,\" Kesh said at last. \"That's worth something to the right people. All right, Marrow. Here's mine for yours. The man who took your brother's boat out was a southerner. Big, soft-spoken, wears a seal ring. I've carried for him, once or twice. He calls himself Corran. That's not his name.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Because I've seen his real name,\" said Kesh. \"On a letter. In a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight.\" She stood and pulled the big coat around her. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. Bottom of the ebb is two hours before dawn. If you want to see where your culvert comes out, meet me at the end of the east causeway on Wednesday night, and wear boots you don't love.\"\n\n\"Thursday's when the magistrate sits.\"\n\n\"Then you'll want to be back by breakfast,\" said Kesh, and went out through the gap in the roof, and was gone.","wordCount":717,"createdAt":1794682767824},{"id":"qEbc6f2EK-C9EM1Uj-lN8","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","text":"She did not find Pell. Pell, the north quay foreman told her, had not come in that morning, nor sent word, and his wife had been round twice asking for him. The foreman was not worried. Pell drank, he said, and when he drank he sometimes slept in the sail lofts.\n\nIlse tried the sail lofts. Pell was not there. She went back to the Exchange and did a day's tally with her copy of the culvert in her sleeve and the page from cellar three against her ribs, and she did not make a single mistake, which she was afterwards rather proud of.\n\nIt was dark when she came out. The streets of the old town were narrow and crooked and the lamps were few, and she had walked them all her life and knew every step, which is why she knew, by the bottom of Weld Street, that she was being followed.\n\nShe did what her father had taught her. She did not hurry. She did not look round. She turned into the rope walk, which was a long covered alley three hundred yards long where the ropemakers twisted hemp in the daytime, and which at night was empty and dark and smelled of tar. Halfway down there was a place where the roof had fallen in and you could step out of the walk into the yard of a cooper's. She stepped out, and waited behind a stack of staves, and counted.\n\nAt thirty, a figure came past the gap in the roof. It stopped. It looked into the cooper's yard.\n\n\"You're very good,\" it said. \"But you breathe like a bellows.\"\n\nIt was a woman. She was young, younger than Ilse, with cropped black hair and a sailor's coat too big for her, and she stood in the gap with her hands in her pockets as though she had all the time in the world.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"Kesh.\" The woman came into the yard and sat down on an upturned barrel. \"Just Kesh. You're the Marrow girl. Tally clerk. Sister of the man who burned his boat.\"\n\n\"He didn't burn it.\"\n\n\"I know he didn't. I watched who did.\" Kesh smiled. She had a gap between her front teeth. \"That's what I've come to sell you.\"\n\nIlse came out from behind the staves.\n\n\"Sell.\"\n\n\"I'm a woman of business. Everybody in Varrow is. You count salt, I carry things.\" Kesh took a hand out of her pocket and turned it palm up. \"Five crowns.\"\n\n\"I haven't got five crowns.\"\n\n\"Then you can't afford what I know.\" Kesh shrugged and began to stand.\n\n\"Wait.\" Ilse put her hand to her sleeve, where the copy of the culvert was. \"I can't pay you. But I can tell you something you don't know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"There's a culvert,\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange. It runs from the old water gate out to the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Somebody's using it to bring white salt into cellar three that never went through the scales. Three or four sacks a time, since midsummer. And the master of the guild went and looked at the plans of it on Saturday.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for a while. Somewhere out in the old town a dog was barking, and a woman was shouting at the dog, and the dog was not listening.\n\n\"That's worth something,\" Kesh said at last. \"That's worth something to the right people. All right, Marrow. Here's mine for yours. The man who took your brother's boat out was a southerner. Big, soft-spoken, wears a seal ring. I've carried for him, once or twice. He calls himself Corran. That's not his name.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Because I've seen his real name,\" said Kesh. \"On a letter. In a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight.\" She stood and pulled the big coat around her. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. Bottom of the ebb is two hours before dawn. If you want to see where your culvert comes out, meet me at the end of the east causeway on Wednesday night, and wear boots you don't love.\"\n\n\"Thursday's when the magistrate sits.\"\n\n\"Then you'll want to be back by breakfast,\" said Kesh, and went out through the gap in the roof, and was gone.","wordCount":719,"createdAt":1794682767682},{"id":"qRBr62R89MtkZ54mY9B9o","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"qYnfShj4K9z6ioHZ4HcO9","text":"The note came under the door at Coil Lane a little after eight, folded small, in a hand she knew from a thousand chits: *Tally room. Nine o'clock. Come alone. — M. V.* She sat with it a long while. Then she put her shawl on again.\n\nThe Exchange was closed when Ilse got there, but the side door was on the latch, and the lamp was lit in the tally room.\n\nMerrit Vael was sitting at her desk.\n\nHe had the cellar book for cellar three open in front of him, at the ninth, at the place where the page had been. He had laid his hand flat over the stub, as though he could feel through his palm where it had gone.\n\n\"Sit down, Miss Marrow,\" he said.\n\nShe did not sit down. \"You sent for me,\" she said, and held out the note.\n\nVael looked at it. Then he put his hand inside his coat and took out its twin, folded the same way. *Tally room. Nine o'clock. — I. M.* It was not her hand. It was not a bad copy of it.\n\n\"Then neither of us did,\" he said. \"Sit down anyway.\"\n\nShe sat.\n\n\"I've been master of this guild for eleven years,\" Vael said. He did not look up. \"In that time I have never known a page to go missing from a cellar book. Not one. Not through damp, not through fire, not through the carelessness of a clerk. And the night after I tell you to write up the *Constant* as Pell's error, a page goes. The very page.\" He turned the book towards her. \"I'm not angry. I want you to understand that. I'm frightened. Where is it?\"\n\n\"Safe.\"\n\n\"Is it.\" He sat back. In the lamplight he looked older than she had ever seen him, and very tired. \"Miss Marrow. I told you to write it up as forty-six because I have been watching the cellar book for cellar three since the first week of July, and I did not want whoever is writing those entries to know that anyone else had noticed. I have been building a case. A slow one. With care.\" He spread his hands. \"And then Pell disappeared, and your brother's boat burned, and the case is no longer slow.\"\n\n\"Pell is dead,\" said Ilse.\n\nVael closed his eyes.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"In the culvert. At the warehouse end. I found him last night.\"\n\n\"With anything on him?\"\n\nShe did not answer, and he opened his eyes and looked at her, and she saw him understand.\n\n\"You found a letter,\" he said. \"Where is it?\"\n\n\"I took it to the council.\"\n\n\"To the council.\"\n\n\"To Councillor Brennick.\"\n\nFor a moment Merrit Vael did not move at all. Then he stood up, so suddenly that the stool went over behind him, and the lamp jumped on the desk and the shadows jumped with it.\n\n\"Miss Marrow,\" he said, very quietly, \"who do you suppose cleared the water gate?\"","wordCount":499,"createdAt":1794685278895},{"id":"rVnQHYfpILXX-psKK28g_","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","text":"The wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a westerly behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the river door tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the river door had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer. A course at a time. From the other side.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Orrin is here,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head. He looked up at her.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use.\" Brennick came down the last step and looked around the vault, at the salt, at the gates, at the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought about not giving it. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the river door. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.","wordCount":960,"createdAt":1794682580908,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794682580908},{"id":"rfpXf9IgG3NiaDAwpy4x_","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"izTsfEfRR7Sd1wycY_p_B","text":"The tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives watched her go and then stared at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap. Before she let herself think, she took the marbled book down and copied the letter into it, word for word, and drew the crossed keys at the bottom as carefully as she could. Then she thought.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with an interest in the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. The one who had asked Vael to look into the water gate in the first place — Hanne had said so — and who had been asking for years about the spring tides, every year the water a little higher. The one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\"\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned and looked into the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.","wordCount":1089,"createdAt":1794682772497},{"id":"sPCB2z0iNPP8Kjopjimsc","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"K3nyf6eQkfA7bwMjLc83B","text":"The harbour watch kept its house at the foot of Tarrant Stair, a squat stone building with barred windows that had been a customs post in the old days and still had the king's arms over the door, half worn away by salt. Inside it smelled of tallow and wet wool. There was a long bench, a table, a fire, and at the back an iron door with a grille.\n\nTobin was behind the iron door.\n\n\"He's not under arrest,\" said Captain Orrin. \"Not yet. He's under my eye. There's a difference, and I'd advise you to be grateful for it.\"\n\nDace Orrin was younger than Ilse had expected for a captain of the watch, perhaps thirty, with a long serious face and a scar through one eyebrow that gave him a look of permanent mild surprise. He had taken off his coat and was writing in a daybook at the table, and he did not stop writing while he talked to her.\n\n\"He was in his bed,\" Ilse said. \"He went to bed at ten. I heard him.\"\n\n\"Did you hear him get up?\"\n\n\"No. I was asleep.\"\n\n\"Then you heard him go to bed and you didn't hear him get up, and an hour after midnight his boat was seen going out on the ebb, and an hour after that it was burning on the bar, and he was in the water beside it.\" Orrin dipped his pen. \"Miss Marrow, I'm not saying he set it. I'm saying I've a burnt lighter across the main channel at the bottom of the tide and every ship due in on the morning flood will have to stand off until we clear it, and the owner of the lighter was the only man found in the water.\"\n\n\"It's his boat. Why would he burn his own boat?\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" said Orrin, without looking up. \"Debt. A woman. A quarrel with his partner. I've seen men burn their boats for all of those, and once for a dog.\" He wrote something. \"What's the matter with his hand?\"\n\n\"I don't know. He was hurt. He was hurt getting her off, or putting the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"He says he woke because her chain stopped knocking on the wall under your window, and the boat was gone, and he went down to the quay and saw her going out, and took a skiff after her, and when he got to her she was already burning and there was nobody aboard. He says he burnt his hand on the tiller trying to bring her round.\" Orrin looked up at last. \"Does that sound like your brother?\"\n\nIt did. It sounded exactly like Tobin.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It sounds like a story to me,\" said Orrin. \"But I've been wrong.\" He closed the daybook. \"Go home, Miss Marrow. He'll be here in the morning. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"That's three days.\"\n\n\"It's two. Today's Tuesday.\" He stood, and she saw that he was tired, and that the hand he put out to take his coat off the chair was not quite steady. \"If there's anything you know about how that boat got out to the bar, now is the time to tell me. Not Thursday. Now.\"\n\nShe thought of the southern man with the ring. She thought of the river door, bricked up, and the four sacks of Harl white, and Master Vael closing the ledger gently, like a book someone had fallen asleep over. She thought of Tobin saying *don't tell anyone*.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"Nothing.\"\n\nOrrin looked at her for a moment longer than was comfortable. Then he nodded, as though she had told him something after all, and opened the street door for her.\n\nOutside, Sefa was waiting on the step, her apron wrapped round her shoulders against the cold. The fog had gone. The sky over the flats was black and pricked with stars, and the tide was coming back in, filling the channels with a sound like a long slow pour of sand.\n\n\"Well?\" said Sefa.\n\n\"Thursday. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Then we've got until Thursday.\" Sefa put her arm through Ilse's and started walking her up Tarrant Stair. \"What do you need?\"\n\nIlse stopped on the stair.\n\n\"Sefa,\" she said. \"What were you doing on the roof at Orley's at one in the morning?\"\n\n\"Stirring the vat. I told you. It goes green.\"\n\n\"The vat's in the yard. Not on the roof.\"\n\nSefa laughed, a short tired laugh with not much in it.\n\n\"There's a man,\" she said. \"He's a ropemaker. We go up on the roof. It's the only place Mistress Orley can't hear the door. Is that what you want to know?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Don't be. He's not much of a man. He talks about hemp.\" Sefa tugged her on up the stair. \"But I saw what I saw, Ilse. I saw the boat go out. There was a light on her when she went out, a little light at the stern, like a lantern. And there was somebody at the tiller. I couldn't see who. But they were steering. Somebody was steering her out to the bar.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"","wordCount":867,"createdAt":1794685272363},{"id":"sbAduLFUVNYSN9vvJ6QW5","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Rm3tWdajSrsgtCkk8_zkn","text":"The wind got up while they were still in the tally room. Ilse heard it come over the roof of the Exchange in one long push, like a hand going over a cat, and the windows of the great hall rattled in their leads, and somewhere a shutter that nobody had fastened began to bang.\n\n\"Spring tide,\" said Vael. \"And a north-easter behind it. It'll come over the seawall tonight. It does, one year in five.\" He righted the stool and did not sit on it. \"If you wanted to flood cellar three — if you wanted every sack in it wet and every book in this room ruined and the culvert full of mud for a twelvemonth — you would open the water gate tonight, at the top of the flood, and let the sea do the rest.\"\n\n\"And no one would ever know how much salt had been in it.\"\n\n\"No one would ever know anything.\" He picked up the lamp. \"Will you come and look? I would rather not go alone. I am fifty-eight years old and I have never in my life been brave.\"\n\nThey went down by the cellar stair. Cellar three was the oldest of the six, a long vault of black brick with the salt stacked along both walls in its sacks, white and grey, the white on pallets to keep it off the damp. The air tasted of it. At the far end, where the vault narrowed, there should have been a wall of newer, paler brick where the river door had been closed up when Ilse was a child.\n\nThere was not. There was an arch, and in the arch a pair of old oak gates, black with age and wet, and between the gates a gap the width of a man, and through the gap the sound of water.\n\n\"Cleared by the eleventh,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"The bricks came out in the summer, a course at a time, from the culvert side. Enough for a man with a sack on his back. The rest went this week. Enough for a lighter's load.\" Vael held up the lamp. \"Look at the hinges. Someone's greased them.\"\n\nOn the floor in front of the gates the salt was trodden into a path, and the path led to the stacks of white, and there, between two pallets, were the four sacks of Harl white from the *Constant* that were not from the *Constant* at all. Ilse knew them by their stamp, which was a good stamp, but not quite right: the Harl ship on it had one mast too many.\n\n\"They forged the mark,\" she said.\n\n\"They forged everything. The mark, the entries, the weighing. That isn't Harl salt, Miss Marrow. It's Ost white, from across the water, that no one in Varrow may buy and everyone would, dressed up in a Harl coat.\" Vael set the lamp down on a pallet. \"I had the man who brings it in under the great clock a week ago, talking prices, so that I could get a look at his ring. All they needed was a clerk who would write what she was given. Pell was frightened into it. Mott the cellarman was paid. And you—\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Vael. \"You weren't. That's why the letter calls you a concern.\"\n\nThe water beyond the gates was louder now. It came up the old slip in surges, with the wind behind each one, and each surge reached a little further across the stones before it slid back.\n\n\"We should go,\" said Ilse. \"We should go to Orrin, now, with this—\"\n\n\"Nobody's coming,\" said a voice behind them, and the lamp on the pallet swung its light round as somebody kicked it, and went out.\n\nIn the dark, a hand closed on Ilse's arm, hard, and she heard Vael cry out, and heard him fall. Then there was another light, a bull's-eye lantern, opened full in her face, and behind it a big soft-spoken shape that said, quite gently, \"Don't scream, Miss Marrow. There's nobody in the building to hear.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" she said.\n\n\"If you like.\" The light moved off her face and she saw him. He was large and fair and tired-looking, a man you would pass in the street, with a heavy southern coat and, on the hand that held the lantern, a ring with a ship on it. \"I'm sorry about your brother's boat. That wasn't my idea.\"\n\n\"Whose was it?\"\n\nHe did not answer. Behind him, coming down the cellar stair with a lamp of her own, her grey cloak held up off the wet stones, was Councillor Brennick.\n\n\"Mine,\" she said. \"I'm afraid it was. Hello, Merrit.\"\n\nVael was on the floor by the pallets, holding his head.\n\n\"Aud,\" he said. \"I did think it might be you. I hoped I was wrong.\"\n\n\"You always hope that. It's why you've never been any use. Ten years I asked your guild and my council to open that gate before the sea took the lower town, and ten years you sent it to a committee. So I opened it myself, and found that it paid. The sea's coming in anyway, Merrit. I thought I might as well be rich when it does.\" Brennick came down the last step and took in the vault: the salt, the gates, the water sliding in under them. \"The clerk had the page from the cellar book, Corran. She didn't give it to me. I watched her hand go to it. It's in her bodice.\"\n\nCorran held out his hand.\n\nIlse thought of the marbled book on the shelf in Coil Lane, the letter in it in her own hand and the keys at the foot of it, which nobody in this cellar knew about. Then she thought about not giving the page. She thought about it very hard, standing in the dark in cellar three with the sea coming in under the water gate. And then she thought about Pell face down in the culvert, and took the page out of her stays and held it out, and when Corran reached for it she let it go a moment early, so that it fell between them into the water on the floor.\n\nHe bent to catch it.\n\nThe next surge came in through the gates with a sound like a door slamming, and it was not like the others. It came up to Ilse's knees and over the pallets and knocked Corran off his feet and took his lantern and drowned it, and in the black that followed Ilse heard Brennick shout, and heard the oak gates groan back on their greased hinges as the sea opened them wide.","wordCount":1120,"createdAt":1794685279829},{"id":"soVGi1A1SOy29WgQyxfN_","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","text":"The archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river door was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the river was turned, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"The gate's at the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man could walk it at any low water, if he knew it and didn't mind rats. At the top of a spring tide, with the gate open, you could float a barge up the old channel to it, same as my grandfather did.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse bent over the drawing. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1033,"createdAt":1794685274375},{"id":"u6YiMBzMrsoUVp23iHl0W","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"N3PO36ObVDJehsDCRmE2s","text":"They did not make it to the causeway.\n\nThey made it out of the warehouse door, and twenty yards across the mud, and then the channel they had crossed on the way in was not a channel any more but a river, waist-deep and racing, with the moon in it broken into a thousand pieces. Beyond it the next channel was filling, and beyond that the next. The flats were going under while they watched, not like water rising in a bath, but all at once and everywhere, the sea coming up out of the mud itself.\n\n\"Back,\" said Kesh. \"Back inside. Up.\"\n\n\"Up where?\"\n\nKesh pointed. The crane.\n\nThey climbed the inside of the warehouse wall by the beams that had fallen against it, and by the iron brackets that had once held the upper floors, and by Kesh's rope, which she threw over a bracket and hauled Ilse up on, twice, when her father's boots would not grip. Ilse lost one boot on the way up and did not go back for it. By the time they reached the top of the wall, where the crane's beam came out through a gap in the brickwork, the water was over the sacks of Harl white and swirling round the mouth of the culvert where Pell was, and still coming.\n\nThey sat on the crane beam with their backs to the wall, sixty feet above the flats, and watched the sea come in. It came in all night. It filled the warehouse to the weed line and then above it, and the chain of the crane swung below their feet with its end in the water. It was very cold. Kesh gave Ilse her coat and sat in her shirt with her arms round her knees and did not seem to feel it.\n\n\"Why are you doing this?\" Ilse said, sometime in the hour before dawn, when she could no longer feel her feet and had stopped trying. \"Really. Not for five crowns.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for so long that Ilse thought she had fallen asleep.\n\n\"I carried for Corran,\" she said at last. \"In the summer. Before I knew what it was. Three loads, out here, at the bottom of the tide. He paid me well. And then he stopped paying me, and told me if I talked I'd end up like the last carrier he'd had.\" She looked down at the water. \"I didn't know what he meant then. I thought he was just talking.\"\n\n\"And now you do.\"\n\n\"Now I do.\" Kesh wiped her face with the back of her hand. \"Pell wasn't a bad man. He drank and he couldn't write a straight two. But he wasn't a bad man.\"\n\nThe sky over the sea went from black to grey to a thin, dirty white. The tide turned. Ilse felt it turn, somehow, though she could see nothing change: some pressure went off the air. Far off across the water, a bell was ringing in the town.\n\nShe counted the strokes, from habit. Six. The six o'clock bell. At the Exchange the porters would be sweeping and the tally desk would be empty.\n\nAnd at the foot of Tarrant Stair, the watch would be getting Tobin ready to go before the magistrate.\n\n\n# The Mount\n\n## The Councillor's House\n[\n  Place: #Brennick's House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Aud Brennick @@Kesh\n  Items: ^The letter:@Aud Brennick\n]\n\nThe tide let them go a little before noon.\n\nThey came down off the crane when the water had dropped below the weed line, and waded, and then walked, and then, for the last half mile, ran, Ilse in one boot and one stocking with the oilcloth packet buttoned inside Kesh's coat. At the end of the causeway Kesh stopped.\n\n\"This is where I leave you,\" she said.\n\n\"Come with me. To the watch. Tell Orrin what you saw.\"\n\n\"Orrin's watch has three men in it I've paid and one I'm paying now.\" Kesh pulled her coat off Ilse's shoulders, and Ilse felt the cold come in like a hand. \"Keep the letter. You're a clerk. People believe clerks.\" She hesitated. \"Pell. Somebody ought to go out and get him. Before the next tide takes him further in.\"\n\n\"I'll tell them.\"\n\n\"Don't tell them I was there.\" And Kesh was gone up Gull Street at a run, barefoot, her coat flapping, and two fishwives turned to watch her go and then turned to stare at Ilse, who was standing at the end of the causeway in the middle of the day covered in mud to the waist, with one boot.\n\nShe went to the watch house first. She did not think about it. Her feet took her.\n\nCaptain Orrin was not there. A sergeant with a cold told her that the magistrate had not sat that morning, on account of the *Gull* still lying across the channel and half the bench being shipowners who had business with the harbour; that the hearing had been put back to Friday; and that her brother was in good health, and had eaten his breakfast, and was not, at present, receiving visitors except by the captain's leave.\n\n\"Tell the captain,\" said Ilse, \"that there's a dead man in the drowned warehouse on the east flats. The one with the crane. It's Gerrit Pell, the north quay weigher.\"\n\nThe sergeant looked at her mud, and at her one boot, and wrote it down very slowly.\n\nThen she went home and washed and put on her other dress, the brown one, and her mother's good shawl, and sat on the edge of Tobin's cot with the letter in her lap and thought.\n\nShe could take it to the watch. But Kesh said the watch was bought, or some of it, and Orrin had looked at her too long in that cold room as though he could see the scrap of tally paper through her stays. She could take it to the guild. But the guild was Merrit Vael, and M. said he could manage her. She could take it to the magistrate on Friday, in open court, in front of half the bench who were shipowners with business with the harbour.\n\nOr she could take it to the council.\n\nNot the council. One councillor. The one who had asked Vael to look into the water gate in the first place — Hanne had said so — and who had been asking for years about the spring tides, every year the water a little higher. The one in the grey cloak who had been talking to Vael in the hall on Monday, and who, Ilse had thought at the time, had not looked as though she much liked what she was hearing.\n\nAud Brennick's house was on the Mount, at the top of the town, where the streets were wide and the houses had gardens and you could not smell the flats at all. A maid let her in. She was shown into a long room with a fire at each end and a carpet so thick that her feet made no sound on it, and asked to wait, and waited.\n\nCouncillor Brennick came in after a quarter of an hour. She was a handsome woman of fifty with iron-grey hair cut short and the kind of stillness that comes from never having had to hurry. She did not sit.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. My maid says you have something for the council.\"\n\nIlse held it out. \"I found it on a dead man.\"\n\nBrennick took the oilcloth and unfolded it and read the letter. She read it once. Her face did not change at all. Then she folded it again, along its old folds, and held it in both hands.\n\n\"Where?\"\n\nIlse told her. She told her about the four sacks and the cellar book and the culvert and the warehouse and Pell. She did not tell her about Kesh. She did not tell her about the page she had cut out of the cellar book, which was still inside her stays, though she could not have said why she kept that back, except that her hand had gone to it, once, while she was talking, and she had taken it away again.\n\nBrennick listened to the end.\n\n\"You've been very brave,\" she said, \"and very foolish, and I think more the first than the second. Does anyone else know of this?\"\n\n\"No one. My brother knows about the man on the south pans. That's all.\"\n\n\"And Master Vael?\"\n\n\"He knows I saw the four sacks. He told me to write it up as Pell's mistake.\"\n\n\"Did he.\" Brennick turned and looked into the fire. \"I have suspected for some months that something in the guild was not as it should be. I asked Master Vael to look into the water gate in the summer, and he told me there was nothing to look into. Now I see why.\" She turned back. \"Leave this with me, Miss Marrow. I'll take it before the council tomorrow, in closed session. And I'll speak to the magistrate about your brother tonight. He'll not go before the bench on Friday. You have my word.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Go home. Speak to no one. If Master Vael asks you anything, you know nothing. Can you do that?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe maid showed her out. On the way through the hall Ilse passed a writing table by the window, with a stand of pens and a dish of sand and a stack of paper on it, heavy and cream, and a stick of red sealing wax.\n\nIt meant nothing. Every house on the Mount bought its paper from the same stationer. Every councillor sealed in red.\n\nShe walked home very fast and did not look back once.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Watch House\n  Characters: @@Sefa @@Tobin Marrow\n]\n\nThe captain's leave, as it turned out, could be had for a basket.\n\nSefa brought bread, and a pot of Mistress Orley's fish stew with a cloth tied over it, and a clean shirt of Tobin's that she had taken off the line at Coil Lane, and a little jar of goose grease for his hand. She set them down on the table in front of the sergeant with the cold and smiled at him, and kept smiling, until he got up and unlocked the iron door.\n\nTobin was sitting on the bench in the cell with his back against the wall. His left hand was bound up in grey rag. He looked up when she came in and his whole face changed, and then changed again, carefully, into something else.\n\n\"Sefa.\"\n\n\"You owe me a shilling,\" said Sefa. She sat down on the bench beside him and put the basket on his knees. \"Eat that. Mistress Orley made it and she'll ask.\"\n\nHe ate. He ate like a man who has had nothing but watch-house gruel for three days, which he had, and she watched him and did not say anything until the pot was empty.\n\n\"Where's Ilse?\"\n\n\"Out. She left me a note last night. *Gone to look at something.*\" Sefa took his bad hand and began to unwind the rag. \"She came back at noon looking like something the tide brought in, and went to the watch, and then she went home and put her good shawl on and went up the Mount.\"\n\n\"The Mount? Who does she know on the Mount?\"\n\n\"Nobody. That's what worries me.\" Sefa got the rag off. The burn ran across his palm and up the inside of his fingers, shiny and red. She opened the goose grease. \"Hold still.\"\n\nHe held still. She worked the grease into the burn, gently, and he did not make a sound, though his jaw went tight.\n\n\"I saw her go out,\" Sefa said. \"The *Gull*. I was on the roof at Orley's. Somebody was steering her. I told Ilse and I've told the captain.\"\n\n\"I know. He told me.\" Tobin looked at his hand. \"Sefa. When I got to her, out at the bar. She was already burning. Nobody aboard. But there was a boat pulling away. A gig, four oars, going east, toward the drowned warehouses. I saw it against the fire. And there was a man in the stern of it, and he turned round to look at the *Gull*, and the light was on his face.\"\n\n\"Did you know him?\"\n\n\"I didn't think so. Not then.\" Tobin was quiet. \"But I've been sitting here three days with nothing to do but think, and I've been thinking about it. And I've seen him since. I've seen him in the Exchange. Talking to Master Vael by the clock, a week back, the day I came in for my pay. A big man, soft-spoken. Southern. With a ring.\"\n\n\"Corran,\" said Sefa.\n\nTobin stared at her. \"How do you know that name?\"\n\n\"Because there's a girl called Kesh who drinks at the Lantern and talks too much when she's had three, and she's been talking about a man called Corran since the summer.\" Sefa wound a clean strip of linen round his hand, firmly. \"Half the rope walk knows that name, Tobin. We just don't know who it belongs to.\"\n\n\"Tell Ilse,\" Tobin said. \"Tell her Vael knows him. Tell her tonight.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\n\"And Sefa.\" He caught her hand with his good one. \"Tell her not to go up the Mount again.\"","wordCount":2236,"createdAt":1794680440188},{"id":"uY6WCYCOwY_GbEKANRkuo","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and said nothing for a long time.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"Yesterday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":996,"createdAt":1794682770758},{"id":"ubbjXT8ZjC6RgUfZohK5o","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"z9GUtIuI2cYraAA2OPlo_","text":"The bell at the Salt Exchange rang six times before the light came over the flats, and by the sixth stroke Ilse Marrow had her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut and the day's first ledger open on the slope of the tally desk.\n\nShe liked this hour. The great hall was empty except for the porters sweeping out yesterday's grit, and the windows along the east wall were still grey, and the only sound was the brooms and, under them, the long breath of the tide going out across the mud. In an hour the hall would be full of factors shouting prices and lightermen shouting back and the clerks of the six houses arguing over whose scales were honest. For now it was hers.\n\nThe ledger was the lading book for the *Constant*, a coaster out of Harl that had come in on the evening tide with forty-two hundredweight of white salt in sacks. Ilse ran her finger down the column. Forty-two sacks, each a hundredweight, each stamped with the Harl mark. Received at the north quay by the weigher, a man named Pell whose sevens looked like ones. Carried by lighter to the Exchange cellars. Entered.\n\nShe opened the cellar book, where the same salt should have been entered again by the cellarman, and frowned.\n\nForty-six.\n\nShe checked the date, checked the mark, checked that she had not turned two pages at once. Forty-six sacks of Harl white, received into cellar three on the night of the ninth. Four more than had come off the ship.\n\n\"Morning, Miss Marrow.\"\n\nShe did not jump, though it was a near thing. Merrit Vael stood at the end of the desk with his hat in his hand. He was the master of the guild, a tall, soft-spoken man with grey in his beard and ink on his cuffs like any clerk, and he had a way of arriving in a room without having walked into it.\n\n\"Master Vael. You're early.\"\n\n\"So are you. So are you always, I'm told.\" He nodded at the open book. \"The *Constant*?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Anything wanting?\"\n\nShe could have said no. Afterwards she would wonder why she didn't, and the only answer she could find was that she had been a tally clerk for six years and her whole worth in the world was that she said what the numbers said.\n\n\"The cellar book has four more sacks than the lading book. Forty-six against forty-two.\"\n\nVael came around the end of the desk and looked for a long time, long enough that she could hear the porters' brooms again.\n\n\"Pell,\" he said at last. \"Pell writes his sixes like his twos. I've told him.\" He tapped the lading entry with one finger. \"That's a forty-six, Miss Marrow. Look at the tail on it.\"\n\nIt was a two. She had seen Pell's twos a thousand times and this was one of them, and the tail on it was the tail on all his twos, a little hook like a fishing barb.\n\n\"I'll check with the ship's master,\" she said.\n\n\"The *Constant* sailed on the morning ebb.\" Vael closed the ledger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over. \"Write it up as forty-six. I'll have a word with Pell about his hand.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\nHe smiled at her and put his hat on and went out through the side door, and Ilse sat looking at the closed cover for a while. Then she opened it again and copied both entries, the lading and the cellar, onto a scrap of tally paper, and folded the scrap twice, and put it inside her stays where it lay against her ribs all day like a cold coin.\n\nBy seven the hall was full. Factors from the inland houses stood under the great clock with their samples in little linen bags and tasted salt off the backs of their hands. A Harl agent argued with a Varrow weigher about moisture. Somebody's dog got in and was chased out. Ilse entered the *Grace of Tidings* and the *Meg* and a lighter-load of grey salt from the south pans that was mostly sand, and did not think about the *Constant* more than once an hour.\n\nAt noon Sefa came by with bread.\n\nSefa was apprenticed to a dyer in Weld Street and her hands were blue to the wrist and would be, she said, until she died, and probably after. She sat on the step below the tally desk, which was not allowed, and tore the loaf in half.\n\n\"You've got your counting face on,\" she said.\n\n\"I always have my counting face on. I count.\"\n\n\"Your other counting face. The one where something doesn't add.\" Sefa chewed. \"Is it a man?\"\n\n\"It's four sacks of salt.\"\n\n\"Oh,\" said Sefa. \"Well, that's much worse.\"\n\nIlse almost told her. She had the words in her mouth. But Master Vael crossed the far end of the hall just then, talking with a woman in a grey council cloak, and he glanced up at the tally desk, and Ilse swallowed the words with the bread.\n\n\"Tobin's in tonight,\" she said instead. \"He's bringing the *Gull* round from the south pans. Come and eat with us.\"\n\n\"Can't. Mistress Orley's got a vat of woad going and she says if it isn't stirred on the hour it'll go green, and if it goes green she'll have my hide for a dye bag.\" Sefa stood and brushed the crumbs off. \"Tell your brother he still owes me a shilling.\"\n\n\"He'll say he paid it.\"\n\n\"He'll be lying,\" said Sefa, and went.","wordCount":937,"createdAt":1794682762302},{"id":"uczWPcsWf7PImK-bJRRb5","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. In the lamplight he was grey. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the top of Thursday's spring tide, when there's water enough in the old channel.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was what the lightermen still called the old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin knocked the pipe out on the bollard. \"He said forty sacks. A full load, the *Gull* down to her marks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down towards the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.","wordCount":907,"createdAt":1794685271432},{"id":"vNPB-8bhwRRu98smI3hSN","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"yeCsfmCdHea6ba_6kfmQt","text":"Inside, the warehouse was a well of black. The upper floors had fallen in long ago and their beams lay heaped across the ground floor in a tangle, slimed with weed, and through the tangle the mud was littered with things the tide had brought: a cartwheel, a ship's bucket, a dead gull, a child's shoe. Kesh opened the lantern a crack and the light went up the walls and showed the weed line all round them, far above their heads.\n\n\"Spring tide comes in fast,\" said Kesh. \"Faster than you'd think. We've got an hour and a half at the bottom of it. Maybe less. When I say go, we go, and we don't stop for anything. Agreed?\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nThey found the culvert in the north-west corner, behind a fallen beam: a low brick arch at the bottom of the wall, half full of mud, with a trickle of black water running out of it. Someone had been there before them. The mud in front of the arch was trodden flat and there were marks in it, long straight drags, like something heavy being pulled, and beside the drags, very clear, the print of a boot.\n\nAnd there were sacks.\n\nThey were stacked against the wall beside the culvert under an old sail, a dozen of them, maybe more, each stamped with a mark Ilse had seen a thousand times. The Harl mark. White salt. Kesh twitched back the edge of the sail and whistled through the gap in her teeth.\n\n\"There's your four sacks,\" she said. \"And their brothers and sisters.\"\n\n\"They're bringing it in from the sea,\" said Ilse. \"Not from the ships. Somebody's landing white salt out here, at the bottom of the tide, off the books, and carrying it up the culvert into cellar three. And then it's in the cellar book. And then it's real.\" She stared at the sacks. \"Once it's in the cellar book, it's guild salt. Nobody can ever say it wasn't. It's been weighed and entered. It's been made honest.\"\n\n\"Clever,\" said Kesh. \"Duty-free Harl white, laundered through the Exchange. You could sell that anywhere.\" She let the sail fall. \"That's why they wanted your brother. A lighter can bring it right up the channel to the river side. It's too slow carrying it by hand through the mud.\"\n\n\"And when he said no, they burnt his boat and put him in the water beside it.\" Ilse's voice was shaking and she could not stop it. \"So he'd be in a cell on Thursday and nobody would listen to him.\"\n\n\"Or so he'd be dead,\" said Kesh. \"I don't think they cared which.\"\n\nThe lantern light moved over the mouth of the culvert. Something pale was lying in the water there, just inside the arch, where the black trickle ran out. Ilse thought it was a sack at first. Then she saw the hand.\n\nIt was Pell.\n\nHe was lying face down in six inches of water with his arms out in front of him as though he had been crawling out of the culvert when he stopped. He had been there some time. Ilse knew him by his coat, which was a weigher's coat with the guild badge on the shoulder, and by the ink on his right hand, the same ink that was on hers. She had seen that hand write a thousand twos.\n\nShe did not scream. She was proud of that, too, afterwards, though she was not proud of very much else about that night. She sat down in the mud because her legs would not hold her, and Kesh crouched beside her with the lantern and looked at Pell for a long time without saying anything.\n\n\"He was coming out,\" Kesh said at last. \"Not going in. Look at his hands. He was crawling out.\"\n\n\"He came to Hanne,\" said Ilse. \"Yesterday. He asked her if there were copies of the cellar books. He was frightened.\"\n\n\"He had reason.\" Kesh reached out, and hesitated, and then put her hand into the pocket of Pell's coat. When she drew it out she was holding a fold of oilcloth, tied with string. \"He had this. I saw him with it, on the quay, last week. He was showing it to somebody. Arguing.\"\n\nShe untied the string. Inside the oilcloth was a letter.\n\nThe paper was good, heavy and cream, the kind that came from the stationers on the Mount where the council houses were. It had been folded three times and sealed, and the seal had been broken, and Ilse could see the half of it that remained: a ship, under full sail, in red wax.\n\n\"That's Corran's ring,\" said Kesh.\n\nThe letter was short. Ilse read it twice in the light of the lantern while the water ran past her knees.\n\n*C.— The gate will be cleared by the eleventh. After that the culvert is yours at every spring ebb until the council votes. I will see that the vote does not come before the new year. The clerk is a concern. M. says he can manage her. If he cannot, I will. Burn this.*\n\nIt was not signed. But at the bottom, where a signature would be, there was a small mark in the same ink: two crossed keys inside a circle.\n\nIlse knew the mark. Every child in Varrow knew it. It was carved over the door of the council house, and printed on the council's bills, and embroidered in silver on the grey cloaks of the six councillors who sat for the town.\n\n\"The clerk,\" said Ilse. \"That's me.\"\n\n\"That's you,\" Kesh agreed.\n\n\"And M. is Merrit Vael.\"\n\n\"Or any other M. in a town full of them.\" Kesh folded the letter back into its oilcloth. \"But I'd not bet against it.\"\n\nThe water at Ilse's knees had grown deeper while she read. She noticed it all at once, the way you notice a sound only when it stops: the black trickle out of the culvert had become a stream, and the stream was flowing the wrong way. It was flowing *in*.\n\n\"Kesh.\"\n\n\"I see it.\" Kesh was already on her feet. \"Go. Now.\"","wordCount":1031,"createdAt":1794682770431},{"id":"vPWFMIC6aLS_oNeIAQAt6","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"iau6bBLQzXaxJozk0pxQo","text":"Captain Orrin was sitting on the stair outside her door when she got home.\n\nHe had a lantern beside him on the step and his hat on his knee, and he stood when he saw her, which no one from the watch had ever done for her before.\n\n\"Miss Marrow. I've been waiting an hour. Your neighbour the chandler thinks I've come to arrest you, and has been very kind to me in consequence. I've had two cups of his tea.\" He picked up the lantern. \"May I come in? It won't take long.\"\n\nShe let him in. The room was cold and she knelt and made up the fire while he stood in the middle of the floor with his hat and looked at everything — Tobin's cot, the table, the shelf of their father's ledgers, the oilskin hanging on the door — in the slow, even way of a man adding a column.\n\n\"Your brother owes money,\" he said. \"To a man named Ferris, on the south pans. Eleven crowns. Did you know?\"\n\nShe had not known. She kept her back to him and fed the fire a stick at a time.\n\n\"He owes it for the *Gull*'s new mast, I think,\" Orrin went on. \"Which is now at the bottom of the main channel. It's the sort of thing a magistrate likes to hear, Miss Marrow. A debt, and a boat, and a fire. He'll think he's heard the whole story in the first five minutes, and he'll stop listening.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\n\"Because I don't think it is the whole story.\" He set the lantern on the table. \"Gerrit Pell is missing. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"I heard. I looked for him.\"\n\n\"So I'm told. You looked in the sail lofts, and the foreman's office, and the Lantern, and at his house. You were quite thorough.\" He turned his hat round. \"So was somebody else. A man came to Pell's house this morning, before you. Big man, his wife says. Polite. Asked for her husband and left a coin for the children.\" He paused. \"She said he had a ring.\"\n\nIlse stood up. The fire had caught and she could feel it on the backs of her legs.\n\n\"What sort of ring?\"\n\n\"She didn't say. Do you know what sort?\"\n\nFor a moment she nearly told him. The scrap of tally paper and the page from cellar three were both against her ribs, and the copy of the culvert was in her sleeve, and it would have been the easiest thing in the world to take them out and put them on the table under his lantern and say *here, you add it*. He had a good face for it. He had stood up when she came along the landing.\n\nBut Tobin had said *somebody with a key*, and Orrin had a key to the cell her brother was sitting in.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\nOrrin nodded slowly. He did not seem surprised, or angry; he looked, if anything, a little sad, the way her father used to look when a column came out wrong for the second time.\n\n\"You said that to me last night, too,\" he said. \"In the same voice.\" He put his hat on. \"When you're ready to tell me, Miss Marrow, I'll be at the foot of Tarrant Stair. I'm always there. It's the curse of the job.\"\n\nHe went out. She heard him go down the stair, and the chandler's door open, and the chandler say something hopeful, and Orrin say something short. Then it was quiet.\n\nShe sat down at the table where his lantern had been and took a fresh book off the shelf — one of her father's, never used, with a marbled cover — and cut her pen, and began to write down everything that had happened since six o'clock on Monday morning, in order, with the times as near as she could put them. She did not know why. It was what she knew how to do.\n\nShe wrote until the fire went down, and then she went to bed, and did not sleep.","wordCount":691,"createdAt":1794682768694},{"id":"wCEy8peyR5IPHbFvtvBmv","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"fnpcRM-9c3_2tsVcBLbDJ","text":"The fog came in with the evening tide, as it did most nights in the last month of the year, rolling over the flats in a low white wall that swallowed the marker poles one by one. By the time Ilse reached the north quay the far end of it was gone, and the lamps on the lighters moored along the wall were only smudges, and the water slapping against the stones sounded close and secretive, like someone talking in the next room.\n\nTobin was sitting on a bollard with his back to her, smoking.\n\n\"You'll catch your death,\" she said.\n\n\"Catching it's easy. It's keeping it I can never manage.\" He did not turn around. \"You're late.\"\n\n\"I had a ship from Harl and a dispute about sand.\" She sat on the next bollard. The stone was wet through her skirt. \"You're early. I thought the *Gull* was coming round on the tide.\"\n\n\"She did. I brought her round on the last of the flood and got her in before the fog.\" He nodded down at the water, where the lighter lay against the wall, black and squat, with her single mast unstepped and lashed along the deck. \"Empty now. We offloaded at the south cellars.\"\n\nSomething in the way he said it made her look at him. Tobin was two years younger than she was, and broader, and he had their father's face, which meant that he could not hide anything at all and had never once in his life managed to. In the lamplight his face was grey.\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\n\"Nothing's wrong.\"\n\n\"Tobin.\"\n\nHe drew on the pipe and let the smoke go into the fog, where it was lost at once. \"There was a man on the south pans. Waiting when we came in. He wanted to know if we'd take a load across to the Exchange tonight instead of in the morning. Paid in advance. Silver, not scrip.\"\n\n\"That's not wrong. That's lucky.\"\n\n\"He wanted it brought to the river door,\" said Tobin. \"Not the quay. The river door, under the cellars. At the bottom of the tide.\"\n\nIlse said nothing. The river door was an old water gate under the Exchange, from the days when the salt barges came down the Varrow instead of across the flats. It had been bricked up when she was a child. She had walked past the brick of it a hundred times.\n\n\"The river door's closed,\" she said.\n\n\"That's what I told him. He said it wasn't.\" Tobin turned the pipe over and knocked it against the bollard. \"He said four sacks. He said no one would weigh them and no one would ask.\"\n\nThe scrap of tally paper inside her stays seemed to grow colder.\n\n\"What did you say?\"\n\n\"I said I'd think about it.\" He looked at her for the first time. \"I said no, Ilse. I'm not a fool. I said no, and he said that was a pity, and that the *Gull* was a fine old boat and it would be a shame if anything happened to her at the bar. And then he walked off into the fog, and Dall and I unloaded, and I came here to wait for you.\"\n\n\"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I don't know. Not a pans man. Not a Varrow man, I'd say. He had a southern way of talking, soft at the ends.\" Tobin shrugged. \"He had a ring. A seal ring, with a ship on it. That's all I saw.\"\n\nIlse took the scrap of paper out of her stays. It was warm now, and a little damp, and she unfolded it and held it so the lamplight fell on the two numbers.\n\n\"There were four extra sacks of Harl white in cellar three this morning,\" she said. \"Four that didn't come off the ship.\"\n\nTobin read it. He read slowly; he always had. Then he folded it and gave it back.\n\n\"Then someone's already doing it,\" he said. \"And they wanted me to do it next.\"\n\n\"Master Vael said it was Pell's handwriting.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey sat on the bollards while the fog thickened and the lamps on the lighters went out one by one as the lightermen turned in. Somewhere down toward the bar a bell buoy rang, slow and lopsided, the way it did when the tide was turning.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone,\" said Tobin.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I mean it. Not Sefa, not your clerk friends, not that old woman in the archive. If somebody's walking salt in through a bricked-up door under the Exchange, it isn't Pell and it isn't some southern man with a ring. It's somebody with a key.\"\n\n\"There's no key to a brick wall.\"\n\n\"Then somebody with a trowel,\" said Tobin, \"and the say-so to use it.\" He stood up and stretched, and the bollard creaked as his weight came off it. \"Come on. I've got a fish and you've got a fire, and if we don't eat soon I'll start on the rope.\"\n\nShe walked with him back along the quay, past the dark lighters, to the stair that went up to Coil Lane and the two rooms over the chandler's where they had lived since their mother died. At the top of the stair she looked back. The fog had closed right over the quay now. She could not see the *Gull* at all, only the place where her lamp had been.\n\n\n# The Bar\n\n## Fire on the Water\n[\n  Place: #North Quay\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Sefa @@Tobin Marrow\n  Items: ^The Gull:destroyed\n]\n\nSomebody was hammering on the door.\n\nIlse came up out of sleep the way you come up out of cold water, all at once and gasping. The room was dark. The fire was a red eye in the grate. Tobin's cot across the room was empty, the blanket thrown back.\n\n\"Ilse! Ilse, open the door!\"\n\nSefa. She knew the voice before she knew she was standing, and she had the bar off the door and Sefa was in the room, still in her dye apron, her hair coming down, her blue hands grabbing at Ilse's arms.\n\n\"It's the *Gull*,\" Sefa said. \"She's burning. She's out on the bar and she's burning. I saw it from the roof at Orley's, I was up stirring the vat, I saw the light—\"\n\n\"Tobin's here.\" Ilse turned to the cot as if he would be in it. \"He was here. We ate. He went to bed.\"\n\n\"Then where is he?\"\n\nThey ran. Down the stair, along Coil Lane, down the second stair to the quay, where the fog had thinned to rags and there were people already, lightermen in their shirts, a woman with a baby wrapped in a shawl, two boys from the rope walk. Everyone was looking the same way, out across the black water toward the bar, where a long orange smear of fire lay on the dark like a wound.\n\n\"That's the *Gull*,\" said one of the lightermen. \"That's Marrow's boat.\"\n\n\"Who took her out?\" Ilse's voice came out high and strange. \"Who took her out? She was empty. She was moored right there.\"\n\nThe place where the *Gull* had been moored was empty water. The painter was gone from the ring.\n\n\"Saw her go out on the ebb, an hour back,\" said the lighterman, a heavy man named Coby whom Ilse knew a little. \"Thought it was your brother. Didn't think anything of it. Lot of the lads go out to the bar on the ebb to fish the channel.\"\n\n\"In the fog?\"\n\nCoby shrugged, and did not look at her.\n\nOut on the bar the fire leaned over and went down, slowly, the way a candle goes when it falls into its own wax. Then there was only a red glow on the water, and then nothing at all, and the bell buoy ringing.\n\n\"Get a boat,\" Ilse said. \"Somebody get a boat out. He might be in the water.\"\n\nNobody moved. It was Sefa who said it, gently, her blue hand on Ilse's back.\n\n\"Ilse. It's slack water at the bar. If he's in it, he's—\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"—he's swimming. Tobin swims like a seal. You know he does.\"\n\nTwo of the lightermen were already unshipping oars. A boat went out, and then another, their lanterns swinging. Ilse stood at the edge of the quay and watched them go into the dark. She found that she had the scrap of tally paper in her hand, though she did not remember taking it out. She had slept with it under her pillow.\n\nThe boats were gone an hour. When they came back, the first held three men and a coil of burnt rope. The second held Tobin.\n\nHe was sitting up in the stern, soaked to the skin and black with soot, his left hand wrapped in somebody's shirt. He was alive. Ilse's knees went, and Sefa held her up, and for a moment the whole quay swam.\n\nThen she saw the two men in the boat behind him, in the dark blue coats of the harbour watch, and the way one of them had his hand on Tobin's collar.\n\n## The Watch House\n[\n  Place: #The Watch House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow @@Dace Orrin @@Tobin Marrow @@Sefa\n]\n\nThe harbour watch kept its house at the foot of Tarrant Stair, a squat stone building with barred windows that had been a customs post in the old days and still had the king's arms over the door, half worn away by salt. Inside it smelled of tallow and wet wool. There was a long bench, a table, a fire, and at the back an iron door with a grille.\n\nTobin was behind the iron door.\n\n\"He's not under arrest,\" said Captain Orrin. \"Not yet. He's under my eye. There's a difference, and I'd advise you to be grateful for it.\"\n\nDace Orrin was younger than Ilse had expected for a captain of the watch, perhaps thirty, with a long serious face and a scar through one eyebrow that gave him a look of permanent mild surprise. He had taken off his coat and was writing in a daybook at the table, and he did not stop writing while he talked to her.\n\n\"He was in his bed,\" Ilse said. \"He went to bed at ten. I heard him.\"\n\n\"Did you hear him get up?\"\n\n\"No. I was asleep.\"\n\n\"Then you heard him go to bed and you didn't hear him get up, and an hour after midnight his boat was seen going out on the ebb, and an hour after that it was burning on the bar, and he was in the water beside it.\" Orrin dipped his pen. \"Miss Marrow, I'm not saying he set it. I'm saying I've a burnt lighter across the main channel at the bottom of the tide and every ship due in on the morning flood will have to stand off until we clear it, and the owner of the lighter was the only man found in the water.\"\n\n\"It's his boat. Why would he burn his own boat?\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" said Orrin, without looking up. \"Debt. A woman. A quarrel with his partner. I've seen men burn their boats for all of those, and once for a dog.\" He wrote something. \"What's the matter with his hand?\"\n\n\"I don't know. He was hurt. He was hurt getting her off, or putting the fire out, I don't know.\"\n\n\"He says he woke and the boat was gone and he went down to the quay and saw her going out, and took a skiff after her, and when he got to her she was already burning and there was nobody aboard. He says he burnt his hand on the tiller trying to bring her round.\" Orrin looked up at last. \"Does that sound like your brother?\"\n\nIt did. It sounded exactly like Tobin. It was the most Tobin thing she had ever heard.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It sounds like a story to me,\" said Orrin. \"But I've been wrong.\" He closed the daybook. \"Go home, Miss Marrow. He'll be here in the morning. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"That's three days.\"\n\n\"It's two. Today's Tuesday.\" He stood, and she saw that he was tired, and that the hand he put out to take his coat off the chair was not steady either. \"If there's anything you know about how that boat got out to the bar, now is the time to tell me. Not Thursday. Now.\"\n\nShe thought of the southern man with the ring. She thought of the river door, bricked up, and the four sacks of Harl white, and Master Vael closing the ledger gently, like a book someone had fallen asleep over. She thought of Tobin saying *don't tell anyone*.\n\n\"No,\" she said. \"Nothing.\"\n\nOrrin looked at her for a moment longer than was comfortable. Then he nodded, as though she had told him something after all, and opened the street door for her.\n\nOutside, Sefa was waiting on the step, her apron wrapped round her shoulders against the cold. The fog had gone. The sky over the flats was black and pricked with stars, and the tide was coming back in, filling the channels with a sound like a long slow pour of sand.\n\n\"Well?\" said Sefa.\n\n\"Thursday. The magistrate sits on Thursday.\"\n\n\"Then we've got until Thursday.\" Sefa put her arm through Ilse's and started walking her up Tarrant Stair. \"What do you need?\"\n\nIlse stopped on the stair.\n\n\"Sefa,\" she said. \"What were you doing on the roof at Orley's at one in the morning?\"\n\n\"Stirring the vat. I told you. It goes green.\"\n\n\"The vat's in the yard. Not on the roof.\"\n\nSefa looked at her, and then laughed, a short tired laugh with not much in it.\n\n\"There's a man,\" she said. \"He's a ropemaker. We go up on the roof. It's the only place Mistress Orley can't hear the door. Is that what you want to know?\"\n\n\"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Don't be. He's not much of a man. He talks about hemp.\" Sefa tugged her on up the stair. \"But I saw what I saw, Ilse. I saw the boat go out. There was a light on her when she went out, a little light at the stern, like a lantern. And there was somebody at the tiller. I couldn't see who. But they were steering. Somebody was steering her out to the bar.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n\"Before she burned.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Salt Exchange\n  Characters: @@Ilse Marrow\n  Items: ^Cellar page:@Ilse Marrow\n]\n\nShe did not go home. She went to the Exchange.\n\nIt was not yet four. The night porter, an old man named Gedd who had known her father, let her in by the side door without a question, because she had a key to the tally room and was often in before dawn, and because, she thought later, he had heard about the *Gull* and was too kind to mention it.\n\nThe cellar books were kept in the tally room in a cupboard with a lock. She had a key to that, too. She lit a lamp and took down the book for cellar three and opened it to the ninth.\n\nForty-six sacks of Harl white. The same hand, the cellarman's careful round hand. She turned back a week and found another entry, a coaster called the *Pelican* out of Cray: thirty sacks lading, thirty-three in cellar. Back another fortnight: the *Wren*, fifty and fifty-five.\n\nShe sat down on the floor of the tally room with the book in her lap and turned pages until the lamp began to gutter. It went back to midsummer. Every week, sometimes twice in a week, a ship came in with a lading of white salt, and the cellar book took in a few sacks more than the ship had brought. Never many. Three, four, five. Never from the grey salt or the pan salt, which nobody wanted except for curing. Always the white.\n\nAt midsummer it stopped. Before midsummer the books matched, sack for sack, as far back as she cared to look.\n\nIlse took out her penknife.\n\nShe had never in her life defaced a ledger. In six years she had never so much as blotted one. Her father had been a tally clerk and his father before him, and the one thing they had both told her, the one thing that she had believed the way other people believed in the gods of the tide, was that a ledger was a promise: that whatever else was false in the world, the book was true, because a clerk had made it so.\n\nShe cut the page for the ninth out of the cellar book, close to the binding, the way you'd take out a page that had been spoiled with ink. She folded it with the scrap of tally paper and put them both inside her stays. Then she put the book back in the cupboard and locked it and blew out the lamp and sat in the dark until the six o'clock bell.\n\nWhen the porters came in to sweep, she was at the tally desk with her sleeves pinned back and her pen cut, as she always was.\n","wordCount":2880,"createdAt":1794680400147},{"id":"xbqJRBzt_lE-1CTgorQ_z","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"Kghlu5z8Cey3C2HTmpZvF","text":"The east causeway ran out from the end of Gull Street onto the flats for half a mile and then simply stopped, as if whoever had built it had looked at the mud ahead and lost heart. Beyond it there was nothing but the dark and the smell of the sea and, very far off, the low black shapes of the drowned warehouses, which had been the warehouses of the old river port before the river was turned, and which now stood up to their knees in mud and up to their shoulders in water twice a day.\n\nIlse was there at midnight in her father's sea boots, which were too big and which she had stuffed at the toes with rag. She had told nobody. She had left a note for Sefa at Orley's that said only *gone to look at something, back by the bell*, and she had not gone to the watch house, because she did not know what she would say to Tobin through a grille.\n\nKesh was late. Ilse stood on the last stone of the causeway with the wind coming in off the sea and watched the water go out. It went quickly on a spring ebb. You could hear it going, a hiss and a rustle in the dark, and you could see the mud come up out of it, shining under the cloudy moon, ridged and puddled and laced with channels where the last of the tide was running.\n\n\"You came,\" said Kesh, behind her.\n\nIlse did not jump this time. \"You said I would.\"\n\n\"I said you'd want to. Wanting and coming aren't the same, in my experience.\" Kesh had a coil of rope over her shoulder and a shuttered lantern and was wearing, Ilse noticed, no boots at all. Her feet and shins were bare and grey with mud. \"Boots are no good out there. The mud takes them. You'll see.\" She held out the end of the rope. \"Tie that round your waist. If you go in past your knees, don't struggle. Lie flat and shout.\"\n\n\"Have you lost people out here?\"\n\n\"Not yet,\" said Kesh, and stepped off the end of the causeway into the dark.\n\nThey walked for an hour. Ilse had lived beside the flats her whole life and had never been out on them further than a child goes for cockles, and she had not known how big they were, or how strange. There was no up or down. The mud and the sky were the same colour and the water in the channels reflected the sky, so that she seemed to be walking on a thin skin stretched over nothing. Twice she went in to the knee and Kesh hauled her out on the rope without a word. Once something moved in a channel beside them, large and slow, and Kesh stopped and shuttered the lantern and they stood in the dark until it had gone.\n\n\"Seal,\" said Kesh afterwards.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe warehouses came up out of the dark one at a time. They were bigger than Ilse had thought, four storeys of black timber and brick, roofless, their windows empty, the high-water line drawn round them in weed at the height of a man's head. Doors hung from one hinge. A crane jutted out from the top of the nearest, its chain still hanging, and the wind made the chain swing and knock against the wall with a sound like someone patiently asking to be let in.\n\n\"Which one?\" said Ilse.\n\n\"You tell me. You've got the plan.\"\n\nIlse unrolled her copy under the lantern. Her hands were cold and the paper kept curling. The culvert came off the edge of Hanne's drawing pointing east by a little south. She found the Exchange, a dark bulk against the faint lamplit haze of the town, and turned until it was behind her, and looked along the line.\n\n\"That one. The one with the crane.\"\n\n\"That's what I'd have said,\" said Kesh. \"But I wanted to hear you say it.\"","wordCount":678,"createdAt":1794682769633},{"id":"xp24hsdI16YCqngzhir8F","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"u1gvWrmAmgDWQJcwGImFZ","text":"She did not remember, afterwards, finding the stair. She remembered the water, which was so cold it did not feel like water but like being struck all over at once, and the salt in it, which got in her mouth and her eyes and seemed to be inside her chest. She remembered Vael's hand finding her sleeve. She remembered the sacks of white salt going past her in the dark, turning over in the current, melting.\n\nAnd she remembered a light at the top of the cellar stair, and a voice she knew shouting her name, and then Tobin's hand, his good hand, closing on her wrist and pulling.\n\nThey came up into the great hall of the Exchange. The windows on the east side had gone and the wind was in the hall, and the rain with it, and the floor was an inch deep in water that had come over the seawall and down Quay Street and in under the doors. Somebody had lit the lamps under the great clock. In their light Ilse saw Tobin, soaked and furious, with his bound hand held up against his chest; and Sefa behind him, with a boathook; and Captain Orrin, in his blue coat, with four of the watch.\n\n\"How,\" said Ilse. \"How did you—\"\n\n\"Sefa came to the watch house,\" said Orrin, \"with a pot of stew and a story about Master Vael and a man called Corran. I didn't believe the stew or the story. But your brother did, and he was making a great deal of noise about it, and I thought I'd rather have him where I could see him.\" He looked past her down the stair. \"Who's down there?\"\n\n\"Brennick,\" said Vael, behind her. He was sitting on the top step of the cellar stair. There was blood in his beard. \"And Corran, whatever his name is. And Mott, I expect, somewhere. And the river.\"\n\nOrrin turned to his men.\n\n\"Ropes,\" he said. \"And lanterns. We're going down.\"\n\nThey brought up Corran first, half drowned and cursing, with his ring hand broken where the gate had caught it. Then Mott the cellarman, who had been hiding behind the stacks of grey and who came up weeping and talking before anyone had asked him anything. Brennick they found last, at the top of the old slip, beyond the gates, clinging to the iron winch that had stood there for a hundred years waiting for barges that never came. She had lost her cloak. She did not say anything at all when they brought her up, and did not look at anyone, and when Orrin put the irons on her she held out her wrists as though she were accepting a glass of wine.\n\nAt the top of the stair she stopped beside Ilse.\n\n\"I burned the letter before you were home,\" she said. \"And the page is in the sea. You'll find it hard to prove anything, Miss Marrow.\"\n\n\"I'm a clerk,\" said Ilse. \"I wrote it all down.\"","wordCount":503,"createdAt":1794685518222,"run":"replace","runUntil":1794685518222},{"id":"xxzYgqrBpUTMlalrx2rpR","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"mv7F8AXlCupQUKma1rwF2","text":"They pulled the *Gull* off the bar on the next ebb. There was not much left of her. Tobin stood on the north quay and watched the salvage men bring her in, a black spine with a few ribs, and said nothing for a long time, and then said that she had been a good old boat and that he supposed he would have to learn to fish from the shore.\n\nPell they brought in on the same tide. His wife came to the quay and stood beside the cart with her hands folded and did not cry, and Ilse went and stood with her, and neither of them said anything, and it seemed to be what was wanted.\n\nThe magistrate sat on the following Tuesday, in the long room over the customs house, under a carving of the king's arms worn half away by salt, like the one over the watch-house door. He was a thin, dry man named Aske who had been a shipowner's clerk before he was a magistrate, and he heard Orrin, and Vael, and Mott, who talked for an hour and a half and could not be stopped, and the sergeant with the cold, without once changing his face.\n\nThen he called Ilse.\n\nShe had brought the marbled book. She laid it on the table in front of him and stood back with her hands folded, the way she stood at the tally desk while a factor checked her figures.\n\n\"What is this?\"\n\n\"A record, sir. Of everything I saw from Monday the tenth, and when.\"\n\nAske opened it. He turned the pages slowly, and she watched him read the four sacks, and the cellar entries copied out week by week back to midsummer, and the culvert, and the warehouse, and Pell. At the letter he stopped, and stayed stopped, over the two crossed keys she had drawn at the foot of it.\n\n\"Councillor Brennick's counsel tells me,\" he said, \"that the original of this letter, if there was one, no longer exists. That what I have here is a tally clerk's fancy, written up after the event to hang the woman who accused her brother.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. She told me that too. In the cellar.\"\n\n\"Was it written after the event?\"\n\n\"Some of it, sir. Monday is written on Tuesday night; I say so at the top of the page. From Tuesday it's written each night, or the next morning where I couldn't. On Wednesday night I was on a crane on the east flats. That's written on Thursday, at noon. It says so.\"\n\n\"And the letter?\"\n\n\"Copied on Thursday afternoon, before I took it up the Mount. Before I'd thought what it meant.\"\n\nAske turned the book to the light from the window. \"And how am I to know that?\"\n\nIlse had lain awake for five nights with that question, and she had expected to be frightened when it came. She found that she was not. It was only a column.\n\n\"Look at the ink, sir,\" she said. \"I cut my pen on Tuesday night before I began, and it was a good point, and the strokes are thin. By Thursday it wanted cutting again. You can see where it splays, there, and there, all through the letter. I cut it again on Thursday night before I went to the Exchange, and it's fine again after that. If I'd written it all at once, afterwards, with one pen, it would be one hand all through. It isn't. It's a clerk's hand over a week, with a pen going blunt in it.\"\n\nThe magistrate looked at the page for a while. Then he took a glass from his pocket and looked again.\n\n\"Master Vael,\" he said, without looking up. \"Is this how your clerks keep their books?\"\n\n\"It is how she keeps hers,\" said Vael, from the bench. \"The rest of us try.\"\n\nSomebody at the back laughed, and the sergeant with the cold sneezed, and Aske closed the marbled book with one finger, gently, the way you'd close a book someone had fallen asleep over.\n\n\"I'll keep this,\" he said. \"You'll have it back.\"\n\nTobin was discharged without a stain. The man who called himself Corran went down to the assize with his hand in a splint, and Councillor Brennick with him in a closed carriage, and Mott in a cart behind, still talking.","wordCount":731,"createdAt":1794685301059},{"id":"yuc24Z3G59Yw0JO0pGrg2","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"L9HZJ1wm2lOVs5-n9_KqI","text":"The archive of the Salt Exchange was below the waterline, which everyone agreed was a terrible place to keep paper and which nobody had ever done anything about. You went down a stair behind the cellars, and down another, and along a passage where the walls sweated and the lamps had to be kept in glass, and at the end there was a door with a brass plate that said RECORDS in letters so worn you could only read them with your fingers.\n\nHanne Sallow had kept the archive for forty years. She was small and bent and wore three shawls at once, and she had a little brazier going at all hours that she said was for the damp and that Ilse suspected was mostly for her feet.\n\n\"The Marrow girl,\" Hanne said, without looking up from the book she was mending. \"I heard about the boat. Sit down. Don't touch anything.\"\n\nIlse sat on the only other stool. The archive was a long low room with a vaulted ceiling, and shelves ran the whole length of it, floor to ceiling, packed with ledgers in leather and ledgers in board and ledgers in nothing at all, held together with string. At the far end the shelves disappeared into a darkness that the lamps did not reach. Somewhere in that darkness water was dripping, slowly and steadily, as it had dripped, Hanne said, since before the Exchange was built.\n\n\"I need to see the plans,\" Ilse said. \"The old plans of the building. From when the river door was open.\"\n\nHanne stopped sewing.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because somebody's using it.\"\n\nHanne put down the needle and looked at her properly for the first time, over the top of a pair of spectacles that were mended at the bridge with wire.\n\n\"Your father,\" she said, \"used to come down here and sit on that stool and ask me questions he oughtn't. He never once told me why. You've a better manner than he had.\" She got up, all three shawls rustling, and took the lamp. \"Come on, then. Mind the floor, it's slick by the third bay.\"\n\nThe plans were in a flat chest at the back of the room, under a tarpaulin, in a drawer that stuck. Hanne pulled it with both hands and it came out in a gust of mould. Inside, between sheets of yellowed tissue, were drawings of the Exchange as it had been a hundred years ago, before the river was turned, when the river still came down past the north wall and barges unloaded straight into the cellars.\n\n\"There,\" said Hanne. She put a finger on the drawing. \"The water gate. Here's your cellars, one through six. Here's the gate, under cellar three. There was a ramp up from the river, and a slip, and a winch for the sacks.\"\n\n\"Cellar three,\" said Ilse.\n\n\"That mean something to you?\"\n\n\"It might.\"\n\nHanne grunted. \"The gate's at the cellar end, the slip beside it where the barges came up, and the culvert runs out from behind the gate. When the river was turned they bricked the gate and filled the slip with rubble. But the culvert's still there. It has to be. The cellars would flood every spring tide if the water had nowhere to go.\" She traced a line on the drawing with her fingernail, a thin double line that ran from under cellar three out across the edge of the sheet. \"Runs out under the old town. Comes up, if I remember, in the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Nobody's been down it in my lifetime. Rats, maybe.\"\n\n\"Could a man walk it?\"\n\n\"A small man could walk it at any low water, if he knew it and didn't mind rats. At the top of a spring tide, with the gate open, you could float a barge up the old channel to it, same as my grandfather did.\" Hanne looked at her sideways. \"Or a woman.\"\n\nIlse bent over the drawing. The ink had faded to brown, and the culvert was only a faint ghost of a line, but it was there, and it went where Hanne said.\n\n\"Can I take this?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Can I copy it?\"\n\n\"You can sit here and copy it with my pen and my ink and then you can put it back in the drawer and close it, and if anybody ever asks me, you came down to look at the lading books for your father's year, which you've done before, and I gave you a lamp and went back to my mending.\" Hanne lowered herself onto a stool by the chest. \"And then you can tell me why the master of the guild came down here three days ago and asked to see the very same drawing.\"\n\nIlse's pen stopped.\n\n\"Master Vael?\"\n\n\"Who else is master of the guild? He came on Saturday afternoon. He looked at it for half an hour and asked me where the culvert came out and I told him what I've told you. He was very civil. He's always very civil.\" Hanne pulled her shawls tighter. \"He didn't ask to copy it. I suppose he didn't need to.\"\n\n\"Did he say why?\"\n\n\"He said the council was considering whether to open the gate again, for the spring tides. Drainage. Councillor Brennick's notion. He said she'd asked him to look into it.\" Hanne shrugged. \"It's possible. They've been talking about the spring tides for years. Every year the water comes a little higher.\"\n\nIlse finished the copy without saying anything. When it was done she rolled it inside her sleeve, and Hanne put the drawing back between its tissues and shut the drawer, which took both of them, and then they stood together in the lamplight among the shelves.\n\n\"Your brother,\" said Hanne. \"Is he a fool?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\n\"Is he a liar?\"\n\n\"No. He can't. He's got our father's face.\"\n\n\"Then you'd better be quick,\" said Hanne. \"Because Gerrit Pell, who weighs on the north quay, came down here yesterday to ask me if the archive kept copies of the cellar books, and I told him we did, and he went away looking like a man who'd swallowed a hook.\" She sat back down to her mending. \"He's frightened. Frightened men talk. Go and find him before somebody else does.\"","wordCount":1055,"createdAt":1794685274453},{"id":"zzPn4tV8bpbrKTnzFpd3o","worldId":"eyxIiAN5CNITvU3oZItJG","eventId":"cyvLsOMTegZIwIJEdR1im","text":"She did not find Pell. Pell, the north quay foreman told her, had not come in that morning, nor sent word, and his wife had been round twice asking for him. The foreman was not worried. Pell drank, he said, and when he drank he sometimes slept in the sail lofts.\n\nIlse looked in the sail lofts. Pell was not there. She went back to the Exchange and did a day's tally with her copy of the culvert in her sleeve and the page from cellar three against her ribs, and she did not make a single mistake, which she was afterwards rather proud of.\n\nIt was dark when she came out. The streets of the old town were narrow and crooked and the lamps were few, and she had walked them all her life and knew every step, which is why she knew, by the bottom of Weld Street, that she was being followed.\n\nShe did what her father had taught her. She did not hurry. She did not look round. She turned into the rope walk, which was a long covered alley three hundred yards long where the ropemakers twisted hemp in the daytime, and which at night was empty and dark and smelled of tar. Halfway down there was a place where the roof had fallen in and you could step out of the walk into the yard of a cooper's. She stepped out, and waited behind a stack of staves, and counted.\n\nAt thirty, a figure came past the gap in the roof. It stopped. It looked into the cooper's yard.\n\n\"You're very good,\" it said. \"But you breathe like a bellows.\"\n\nIt was a woman. She was young, younger than Ilse, with cropped black hair and a sailor's coat too big for her, and she stood in the gap with her hands in her pockets as though she had all the time in the world.\n\n\"Who are you?\"\n\n\"Kesh.\" The woman came into the yard and sat down on an upturned barrel. \"Just Kesh. You're the Marrow girl. Tally clerk. Sister of the man who burned his boat.\"\n\n\"He didn't burn it.\"\n\n\"I know he didn't. I watched who did.\" Kesh smiled. She had a gap between her front teeth. \"That's what I've come to sell you.\"\n\nIlse came out from behind the staves.\n\n\"Sell.\"\n\n\"I'm a woman of business. Everybody in Varrow is. You count salt, I carry things.\" Kesh took a hand out of her pocket and turned it palm up. \"Five crowns.\"\n\n\"I haven't got five crowns.\"\n\n\"Then you can't afford what I know.\" Kesh shrugged and began to stand.\n\n\"Wait.\" Ilse put her hand to her sleeve, where the copy of the culvert was. \"I can't pay you. But I can tell you something you don't know.\"\n\nKesh sat back down.\n\n\"There's a culvert,\" said Ilse, \"under the Exchange. It runs from the old water gate out to the drowned warehouses on the east flats. Somebody's using it to bring white salt into cellar three that never went through the scales. Three or four sacks a time, since midsummer. And the master of the guild went and looked at the plans of it on Saturday.\"\n\nKesh was quiet for a while. Somewhere out in the old town a dog was barking, and a woman was shouting at the dog, and the dog was not listening.\n\n\"That's worth something,\" Kesh said at last. \"That's worth something to the right people. All right, Marrow. Here's mine for yours. The man who took your brother's boat out was a southerner. Big, soft-spoken, wears a seal ring. I've carried for him, once or twice. He calls himself Corran. That's not his name.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\n\"Because I've seen his real name,\" said Kesh. \"On a letter. In a place I'm not going to tell you about tonight.\" She stood and pulled the big coat around her. \"Spring tide's on Thursday. Bottom of the ebb is two hours before dawn. If you want to see where your culvert comes out, meet me at the end of the east causeway on Wednesday night, and wear boots you don't love.\"\n\n\"Thursday's when the magistrate sits.\"\n\n\"Then you'll want to be back by breakfast,\" said Kesh, and went out through the gap in the roof, and was gone.","wordCount":720,"createdAt":1794682767630}],"tombstones":[],"blobs":[]}